      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>St. Paul's Church. Annual Sale Realises £240.</p>
        <p>There was a large and representative gathering at the annual sale of work in connection with St. Paul's Church, whtch wai held in the Winmarleigh-street Assembly Rooms, on Tuesday. The amount the workers were anxious t ) realise was £00) for the Assistant Clergy Funds. The various stalls wvre well stocked with an endless variety of articles, ornamental and useful, and were in charge of enterprising lady members of the congregation who did their utmost to dispose of their warts. The opening ceremony was performed by Mrs John J Crosfield, of Frodsham. The Vicar (Rev W Bracecamp) presided and amongst others present were the Revs Canon Willis, E E Peters, J II liankrs, B Percival, H V Pigot, G Vale Owen and T W Sturges, and a large number of the leading members of the congregation. The VICAR said he was grateful to all who had made a special effort to be present art afternoon. 1 bey were sorry to have to come before them etvdt year ant ask for help but he assurred them they needed it very much indeed. hey were not asking them to provide luxuries, but absolute necessities. At St. Paul's they had no grants from any society, and they had therefore to appeal to them for help. The societies who gsnerally gave aid to the church's looked on the amount of money a church was receiving but never stopped to think what were its ex penses.The £3OO they were sating for went entirely to the additional clergy fund, but it did not represent sll their needs. They would shortly ask them to help them in many other ways. In the Bewsey district they were overcrowded, and would very shortly be compelled to erect a new Mission Church ; in fact they had started the work, and they would have to raise at least fifty thousand shillings. It was said history repeated itself, lie was recently looking up old records in connection with the church, and found that in 1865 Mr. John Crosfield was a very influential mem- ber and a great worker in connection with their day school. Now they had as a member of their congrega- tion his son, Captain George Crosfield, who not only lived in the parish, but was a keen helper and sympathizer with them in their work, and he showed his sympathy ev,ry now and then in a p-actical way (Applause). That was Mrs. Grostleld's first appearance in Warrington in the capacity of opener of a sale of work, and they at St. Paul's felt themselves highly honoured in having secured her services. (Hear, hear.) Be thanked the members of the Ladies' Working Party and the members of the congregation who had worked so hard and denied themselves during the past year to bring about that great success. The articles on the stalls had cost a great deal of self-denial, and be thanked them for their loyalty and devotion. (Applause) Mrs. Caosetsio, in declaring the sale open, said it needed no words from her to recommend the object for which they had met that afternpon when they remem- bered that the stipends of the curates depended upon the amount raised from year to year by that sale. She wished it every Success and felt sure they would do their utmost to secure a good result. (Applause). The VICAR apologized for the absence of Captain ' Crosfield, who, he said, had sent a splendid substitute in Mr B S Townroe. (Appbuse). Mr TOWNROP said Captain Crosfield was extremely sorry he was not able to le present, because be appre- ciated to the full the work Mr. Bracecarep and his colleagues were doing in the Bewsey district. He felt that after such a disaster as they had read of that morn- ing, a disaster which must have senta thrill all through the Anglo-Saxon world, they had to think of disasters happening close to their own doors in the town. There was need of men in the district to save spiritual lives as there was need of men to save lives in the Atlantic. That was the work in a town like Warrington which they had to do, and a work be asked them to support. Mr Bracecamp, Init.') indomitable courage, was laniard-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>Burning Pain Banished.</p>
        <p>" ISUFFERED from piles nine years," writes Mrs. F. W. Harrison, 61, St. John's Street, Coventry. "I was in )nstant torture, the aching and burning pain making my life miserable. When the attacks were on work was out of the question; in fact, I got so run-down through the terrible pain that 1 didn't feel fit for anything, • For eighteen months I was under one doctor, with the piles all the time getting more objection- Able. Besides doctor's treatment I tried all ads of ointments and salves without getting iny relief. "Reading how another sufferer's piles had been cured by Zam-Buk caused me to try this new herlial balm. I never can be thankful enough that I took this step, for .-cz pain, and - very grp+-</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk w0..._ it soothed the burning, acu..., for the relief Zam-Buk brought me after my nine years' torture. "My perseverance with Zam-Buk has now resulted in the piles being completely cleared away. I want you to publish my experience so that other poor sufferers be induced to try Zam-Buk, which cured me so wonderfully."</p>
