      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>the work of the Church in that district, and he could not do so without the help of such magnificent men as Nit. Peters and Nlr BAnkes. (Applause.) Help was also given by Mrs Bracecamp end other ladies in the parish, who had given up eudless afternoons to prepare for that sale. While their hearts we re cpen because of the disaster to the Titanic, let their purses he opened to support the gold wor:c being done in St. Paul's Parish. (Applause). Mr J II SNIETHURST, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs Crosfield, gild as treasurer he ward prepared to recehe all they were willing te give end to spend it g 0 the best advantage. He admired the Crosfleld family for the good they were doing in the town, and their hest thanks were duo to Mrs Cr.-field for her kindness in coming down that afternoon and officiating at that sale (Applatia). Alderman SMETHURST, in seconding, said he was sure they were all proud of Mrs Crosfield and her brother- in-law, Captain Crosfleld, and in fact, of the whole of the Crosfleld family for the good work they had accom- plished in the town. ((IPar, hear.) He hoped all the members of the audience would support the Vicar, because they must have the £3OO per year to keep their machinery in perfect working order. (Applause). The proposifion was heartily carried, arid the VICAR, replying on behalf of Mrs. Crostleldo banked his brother clergy for their presence,and ladies and gentlemen from the other churches for supporting them.l f they were all determined that tbey would eci their best they would be able to achieve some work for God—the work they all Fad at heart. (Applause). I The sale was then proceeded a ith in a brisk manner, snd the sum of £2lO was realised.</p>
        <p>Christ Church, Latchford. TLe animal vestry meeting in connection with this church was held on Saturday evecing, the Rev G C Briggs presiding. Among others present were : Mr George Venn (vicar's warden), and Mr Thomas Whyman (people's warlen).—Mr Whyman submitted the accounts which showed that the year began with a debit balance of .£3 lie ,and closed with a debit of £2 14s. During the year there had been forty-four general ofterteries, and the i7come from that source was £147 23 4d., while .£32 3s Sd use recebed fur Eprcinl objects. The Easter offertories amounted to .£57 ISs 7d.—On the proposition of Mr Gorton, the accounts were passed The Vicar thanked I,lr Venn for his services during the past two years. Ile nominated Mr Banks as his warden for the ensuing year. For Banks had regularly attended the services for over thirty years. Mr James Lowe was elected people's warden. Mr Banks nomin- ated Mr. Venn es his sidesman for the ensuing year, arid Mr Wbyman was nominated by Mr Lowe. Messrs J P Booth, Jr Mt Fairclough, Ii T Fairclough, W Limb, A Lee e, Short, W Tinsley Mockett, A Davidson and Hodgkinson were elected 2idesm.a. Mes:rs J Lowe and Banks were elected lay representatives. On the proposition of Mr Starkey, seconded by Mr O, rt)e, a vote of thanks was accorded to the retirirg church officer.F. Mr Bans, in proposing a vote of thanks to the choir, said the choir at Christ Churzn was second to none in the district. (Hear, hear).--Mr John Fairclough seconded and said the services of the choir were highly appreciated by the congregation.—The proposition was carried unanimously.—A vote of thanks/pis accoread to the Church Workers' Association on the proposition of Mr Sh, rt, seconded by Mr Starkey.—The decorators wee also thanked.— i he Vicar sand the year had been a memorable one in the history of the church. They gelebr•ted the fiftieth anniversary of the consecration Of the church, and they had a perm:infra memorial of the celebration in the porch, which had been built. The Rev W Bendy Cock bad been engaged as the curate during the year, and they bad been most happy and fortu- nate in securine the services of a go ,c 1 man. (Applause) Mr .7 Fairclough said the total income of the curacy fond was £34 9., and they had a balance in hand of £9 93. The accounts were passed. Mr Fairclough, in presenting the report of the jubilee fund, said the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>FOUND. THE GLAD Eye—and it was Mother's. No wonder she was pleased. She had just proved what a blessing</p>
        <p>Steedman's Powders are to Parents. Nothing else keeps children so fit.</p>
      </div>
