      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>An open air meeting of the Wycliffe Society was held on Sunday. Mr Mark Howarth presided and spoke of the pleasure it gave him to be present at another open meeting. He was quite satisfied that the P.S.A. move- ment, which was quickly spreadirg not only in this country, but in other lands, was one of the mightiest levers for batering the brotherhood of man. He was afraid that while many believed in the Fatherhood of God, they were slow to put into practice and help forward the brotherhood of man. The Rev W A Beckett gave an interesting address upon the word "H." Mr William Booth sang "My hope is in the Everlasting," Which was greatly appreciated. Mr l'ayton presided at the organ. Mr W Beswick and Mr Henry Osborne also took part in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>St, John's Presbyterian Church,</head>
        <p>St, John's Presbyterian Church,</p>
        <p>Preaching on Sunday evening cn the power given to men by God, to Le used for purposes of right or wrong as they so willed, the Rev W Reid introduced the character of Bahian'. Keenly desirous of obtaining reward and position by methods be (Belem) knew to be wrong, he yet pursued them, whilst at the same time h 3 wished for God's permission. At first com- manded not to go, 113 was afterwards ordered to go, is which, said the preacher, there was no contradiction, and no real paradox. God's will remained unchanged, and ever did but the fact of a moral life made it incumbent upon God to allow man to follow his own will. God couldn't pre- vent it or the moral system collapsed. This free will of men, the power by which he was able to separate himself from God, constituted the danger which attended every life, but in the same place safety was to be found and enly there. Their wills were theirs to make them God's, and they were safe only when they had received the impress of his sacred will, made holy sanctified. Their concern was not to have Him will filet they wanted, but to be on the side He</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>New Church.</head>
        <p>The New Church.</p>
        <p>On Wednesiay evening the Rev. W. H. Claxton delivered an address at Atkinson's eras; on " Redemp- tion and Salvation," under the auspices of the West Lancashire Mission of the New Church. Redemption, to raid, was a work purely divine, and consisted in the rescue of Alan by the Lord Jesus Christ, who was Jehovah, the Creator Incarnate itt His humanity, from the infernal powers which held man in bondage, and his restoration to a state of freedom. Redemption was- wrought for all. Salvation was spiritual health, and was secured only by those who in the use of their restored freedom showed their belief in the Saviour who was also 'heir Creator and Redeemer, by living in accordance with his precepts as the Great Pbysiclan of human souls, and who in His strength ceased to do evil and learned to do well.—An interesting discussion followed, and many questions wera asked. On Wednes - day next at 8 p.m. Mr. Claxton is to give an adlress on " Miracles." This will probably be for a tirno the last visit of Mr. Claxton to Warrington, as he has accepted an appointment under the Missionary and Church Extension Board of the General Conference as Missionary Minister for the Metropolis, and will remove from Lacclsbire to London at the end of Juno, ,</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission Anniversary. . The tof ty-first anniversary Eery i ces of the Workingmen's Mission Sunday School were held on Sunday last, in the morning at the Chapel, Thewlis- street, and in the evening at Parr Hill. The preacher was the Rev J S Chedhern, minister of the Wash-lane Baptist Chapel, Latchford, whose sermons were listened to with great Interest.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon the scholars numbering over 900 walked in procession to Parr Hall, where a musical service was held at 2.30. An interesting addros was given by Cdtincillor Henshall and Mr. Thomas Dale (senior superintendent) occupied the chair. The children and choir sang very effectively under the leadership of Mr F Atkinson, supported by Mr T Tanner at the organ, and an orchestra. The collections for the day, which were in aid of the school funds, amounted to £32 Is.</p>
        <p>' LINSEED COMPOUND is a good Cough Mixture. Miceli% e and Economical. Of Chemists only.—Advt</p>
        <p>, ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April, 20, 1912.</p>
        <p>Telephone-1214 Royal.</p>
        <p>RAY &amp; IVIILES 34 to LitRLpOoNODLON-RD.. One of the Largest General House Furnishing Emporiums in the North of England. RAY &amp; MILES give THE BEST VALUE FOR OASH ONLY. Hotel and Boarding House Keepers and those about to furnish have £lOO,OOO worth of FURNISHING GOODS, TO SELECT FROM.</p>
