        <p>THE COAL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>SOME FACTS THAT ARE WWI BEARING IN MIND.</p>
        <p>The wealth produced in a coal mine is distil thrcugh three channels, namely: —(1) Milling and loyalties to landowners, (2) Wages to 1510</p>
        <p>„ Interest on capital. The first charge on the working of a coal not, as has been so often stated during the Pr'cio struggle, the wages of !about., but the lealloe. e rents, royalties and wayleaves paid to the landoe'n Before a ton of coal is won the dead-rent oluet t guaranteed, and for every too that is taken frool ( mine the landowrer talus a toll of from 31. to le' id royalties and Nvayleaves t In the House of Commons on G.h March, Chancellor of the Exchequer, in reply to Thorne, M.P., stated that the estimated annual aloet of royalties on coal was 6,000,000, and Ih° estimated average royalty per ton was 51,d. a 0 This sum of [six million pounds per annum doe- t represent the full toll of landlordism on the c,3, industry. There is the question of dead-rent° it mining rents over and above the royalties raid. di4 qaite safe to say that the exactions of lardloroi total every year no less a FUM than ten million Pe°o Before a penny can be paid in wages or interest, t] to the value of £10,000,000 has to be won fer,til landlords who do ,nothing whatever towards ge` the coal. _it</p>
        <p>Mining Rents and Royalties are not the anything that has been done by the owners of c4l bearing land. They did not put the coal there ;f't did not explore for it • they did not get it out o'o, mines. The coal is there naturally and all tho and risk of the operations is borne by those Aril°, °To the mines and pay the royalties. Owners of ing lands play no part in the development of the 13,1„1 Their sole function is to levy a toll on those ri'w develop them Under the present system of rating and tast,i owners of mining rents and royalties not onlY;) without any effort or risk whatever, a large s'ic,ci the wealth produced, but they are also WI). withhold coal bearing land from use. This Lues.„.,ci restricted coal producing area, therefore re° I' employment. Restricted employment rn091,6 wages, because of the competition of men for io The only hope of permanently improved for the miners, and for all workers, is in the abi▪ jo of land monopoly by means of Land Values l'"lr,'„f. The taxati n of land values means the repea`ititi present rates and taxes on improvements (O ylI a machinery, workshops, houses, food), and the lecloi of public revenues on the actual value of the 1ane1.,.!0. whether the land be developed or with-held fr°"" It is unnecessary to emphasise the stimaltst would result to all industry, the coal mining in,";ef no less than any other, in consequence of the improvements from rating and taxation. 00"1,• 0 idle land—there is a vast amount of coal bearing re held out of use—would be forced by the Pre9sll3o the tax to throw their lands open to develfl;trii This means an increased production in all /o°4o increased demands and markets for coal and 9. eee modities, therefore increased employment. 10°4,10 tl employment means high wages because owing 'ffer demand for men employers would be forced to decent living wage and improved conditions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE..</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE..</p>
        <p>•" The Mousme."</p>
        <p>In many respects "The Adousnid " is c'ne most delightful musical comedies which ll5:%1 59,1 Wartingt,n. It does not touch the mole-0 el' a" dramatic excellencies of " The Merry Wide 97,,t so" " The Quaker Girl," but it is exceedingly brig' TI dainty in the fairy-like fashion of far Japan;soortt scenes are positive tiiumphs from the brush a u, Tritschler, and in their warmth of colourilldroer! delicate contrasts, surpass anything of the k,i° Or, on a Warriogton stage for msny years. T"l3 p•-4 effects are also produced with admirable skill, realism of the earthquake in Act 2—accornP3l;oo; sulphurous lightning flashes and the collapse c"bordi;t ings—must be seen to be believed. One need add that the dresses are beautiful copies of thee, f thing," and rival their surroundings in I), illisacido "The Maids of Japan " is the English yersa tot "The Mousmo," and indicates the nature 04;3, creation which has run for over 150 perforralloa the Shaftesbury Theatre, London. There arefte„ begeot and Geisha girls, Japanese army officers, and d4doltill', people in humble spheres of lire. There are lid a,llll of love-stories running through the cottleibtqo amount of excellent fun-making, some u,e,, 000 dancing, and singing of an excellent qualitY,`:oelli/0 which is above the average. Miss Hope ir" especially distinguishes herself as 0 Hana Sel'toptp;o in the Temple of Tempura, her light, tunern`be- re ,;c1 being admirably suited by such dainty nunl ve dfrer " Dear Old Temple Bell,' "Say you'll f"r.,g Pgil understand," and " What do I know sofilliSos;il role is, unfortunately, rather sad and pr apart from the vocal calls upon lrf'dotere Trevelyan hardly appeared to the best Quite the opposite criticism applies to tr:tie Taylor as Mio Ko San, the daughter Of t-fe" /Atli and his English wife. She not only solg, Flower of Japan" and the humorous baby 1/I,°oati fiet charming expression, but was, throug"nt. itb embodiment of winsomeness and merrimedoet jl dancing, too, was a delight, and the dance d softof Tanaka, a Tokyo journalist (Mr. Leonar., clot brought down the house. The humour, sustained by Mitsu, Mistress of the Gell,s,dotag;on Minnie Rayner), Suki, a fortune-teller (Mt % v 'tee, do, Brierley), and Hashimoto, a tea-house toc.,tltort (Mr. Billy Rex). These three are in 05' Odd Suki and Hashimoto revolving reoll ts qatin (who is very round herself) like PltWdectdoel the sun. Their comic by-play jact49lllle though one hardly appreciated Mitsifte co. in burst of" cockney "—it clashed with the kill' oitatbe drolleries of Suki the sly, and Hashimote °goner Dlt' were never-ending, and reached a clicaOestioe:bt suffering infant to whiah both &amp;aimed a (V rladefil9 parentage. This was one of the funniett idoo musical comedy we have seen for sortie If 146003 hero of the piece is Captain Fujiwara (Nlr• „fg.ottr'kittg,, Hancock) who sings aud acts with flro,',l-e-0008", throws any amount of sincerity into hie tbre with 0 liana San. Captain Yamakki stal"'r Id irel the scenes with maligant purpose,and Mr. 115 o°9 Liet"o must be admitted to do well in the part. 0,11t3 (Mr. Hugh Wakefield) is a sprightly foil to .0-.v'd (105ei and has many a mirthful duel with that eltigitm cPq ent before she finally succumbs ts his ardour Oct orteidn Mr. Richard Andean makes an imPressi Okubo, and the chorus of gieshi and te3obillfr,o; particularly (alien, lively and musical c.°o of Pi The augmented orchestra under the directio tt" ,„0 oi'°dot, reta TV H. Yorke gives a most capable interP dot p tuneful music by Lionel Monckton and go- while the whole producion undoubtedly en3st, high reputation of that fatuous producer, Courtneidge. ,0 jilt! PROBLEM OF THE COST OF MVP ing OS 111 CANADA. CoriPrlbtso The three great problems confront J*eh ob legislators are production, equalization; tl'ep tion. In a recent speech, Hon George. j;"ded t'olrfor Minister of Trade and Commerce retpl Canada has natural resources that hnvetoo wes P 10 realized or prized. The consumer