      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>AS EXIIIIIITWN FOWL</head>
        <p>AS EXIIIIIITWN FOWL</p>
        <p>Of late years V‘e Black Leghorn has lost some of its populatity as an exhibition fowl. However, there is generally a good classification for them in most shows, and the novice might very profitably take up the Blacks for show purposes. They are easy to breed, and good specimens can be bred frcm a single pen mating. Of the two varieties at the present time I think there is more money in the rose-combed variety. As a matter of fact, the general tendency seems to be for rise-combs in preference to single- combs in most varieties where we have the two varieties of comb. The Black Leghorn is a very hand- some fowl when bred true to colour. Only those with good sound yellow legs and beaks and with good sound feather colour should be bred from.</p>
        <p>SEASONADLE lIINTS. Altbcugh I prows° giving you something like a full treatise on exhibition poultry, we n ust not neglect the season's work amongst the utility stock. There- fore you should note the following seasonable hints. Remove all the p ter specimens from the sally hatched chicken., and any that are abso!utely deformed would be best killed off. If they are suffering merely from deformity and not disease, fatten them off, and 11S9 them for table purposes.</p>
        <p>GREEN FOOD.</p>
        <p>Don't forget to give an abundance cf green food to the }Tung stock. If you have difficulty in this direction, you should purchase the greengrocer's offal and before boiling it (to mix in the mash) see that it is thoroughly well washed, and abstain from putting in the mash any inecap d leaves. Give the stock birds an occasional feed of green bone, and every week a mild dose of Epsom salts in the drinking water.</p>
        <p>LIGHT DREEDA. You may continue to set eggs from the non sitting varieties, no time should be lost in doing so, because it will be well on into the month of May before the chicks are cut.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>TABLE BIRDS.</head>
        <p>TABLE BIRDS.</p>
        <p>Ycu should set as many eggs fps possible now from crossbreds for table purposes. These will fetch good prices in the autumn. You should also get as many duck eggs down as possible, remember that good fat young ducks aro profitable.</p>
        <p>VERMIN,.</p>
        <p>Constantly examine the chicks for vermin. As the days get warmer insect pests will be troublesome, and if not kept down the chicks will be seriously deterred in growth. Examine the nest boxes and perch sockets and soak the affected parts with paraffin oil; this is a most useful as well as being a very cheap insecticide. It is not advisable to do much limewashing at present The weather is too uncertain, you may leave this part of the work until the days get a little warmer.</p>
        <p>'POULTRY FOR ExIIIBITION. (continued). Last week we got as far as the poultry show. With- out doubt a novice can have ro better demonstration than by paying periodical visits to a few good shows. This, together with the thecoet'cal training now obtained, shculd give you a fair idea as to what breed to go in for. Always calculate as to local conditions and accommodation at your disposal. For icstance if you are living in an industrial centre it would not be wise to go in for light coloured varieties. And if your accommodation is limited you should be satisfied to keep only a small stock. Our next point is on the value of</p>
        <p>SPECIALISING. It is an unwise venture to start in more than one variety. When you have decided on a certain biced, you should stick to this until you have thoroughly mastered all there is to kn )w about it ; and further- more until you have made a name with the variety selected. Then if you have a desire to extend you might profitably do so, because having mastered one variety 3ou get to understand, and are better able to maLages numerous varieties. First of all then you will observe the importance of specialising on one breed at a time.</p>
        <p>PURCHASING STOCK.</p>
        <p>I am often asked, is it better to purchase stock birds in preference to eggs ? I would certainly advise the i purchase of stock n preference to eggs, but although this method is best, you might profitably make a start with purchasing eggs from the right quarter. Some of cur best breeders will not dispose of eggs from their best pens at all, so the safest course to adopt is the purchase of stock birds. To qualify this remark I n ill specify the advantage. Whtn making a start from stock birds, you are able to know exactly from what class of fowls you are breeding, and furthermore you are better able to raise a greater number of chicks in the first season (at much less in actual cost) than you would do by the purchasing of prize eggs for sitting. A trio of prize bred fowls would cost you on an average about £7. From these you should be able to rear at least 40 chicks. This is estimated at a very low egg average indeed, inasmuch as it is allowing for six eggs per week from the two females. Supposing they d:d lay on an average six eggs per week, and supposing again that you hatched eggs from February to May, this would give an egg yield for the pet iod named of 96. So that you will see it would be a very poor season indeed if you did not manage to rear 40 chickens. Then besides all this you still have the original stock left to breed from next season. Let as examine the egg side of the question. To make fairly sure that you would rear 40 chicks estimating for loss through breakages, unfertile eggs etc., you would have to purchase n) le-s than five dozen eggs. Now the cost of a dozen eggs from prize stock such as are represented by the value of the stock mentioned, would be about .£1 is. per dozen. Sa you see that five dozen eggs at this price would cost £5 ss. This certainly is £1 15s. less than the cost of the stork birds, but you see you still have the stock left as already pointed out, and you can be quite sure that for a second season they are quite worth half the original cast, or £3 10s. This then would actually leave you with a balance in favour of stook of £1 15s. as against the purchasing of eggs. And still further, seeing that you are unable to actually tell what class of birds the eggs are from, I have absolutely no hesitation in recommending the purchase of stick birds in preference to eggs. I hope the case is clear. Well now, as to general cost. For the sake of argument we have used certain figures, but this by no means decides the whole question of value. Thero are so many circumstances which decide this matter, that in all fairness I am bound to go into details.</p>
        <p>BLUE ANDALUSIAN EGOS, 3e.: first cross Anda- lusian—Buff Orpington, 2s. 61 per sitting.—F. SANDERSON, Penwortham, ['rostra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>ICTVAL COST.</head>
        <p>ICTVAL COST.</p>
        <p>Generally speaking, the popularity or otherwise of any bleed of exhibition fowls decides their market value. Fen— instance, White Orpingtons, Buff Orpingtons and White Wyandottes are popular fowls, while on the other hand, Buff Legborns and Buff Rocks are not near so popular, consequently while you might have to pay £25 for a good trio of White Orpingtons, you could purchase an equally good trio of either of the two latter breeds mentioned for So you will at once see the difficulty of fixing prices. What then is the best way to operate? Always seek good advice first and then act upon it. uet to know from someone reliable who are the best and most reliable breeders of the variety you have decided upon, and then unreservedly place your- self in their hands. Tell the fancier how much you intend to sren d, and then he will tell you whether or not you could profitably make a start with such a purchase. A gotd reliable breeder will never ill-</p>
        <p>IVOTHING WILL REAR yrur Baby Chicks like 11 PERFECTION CHICK FOOD, used and recom- mended by Tom Barron, E. Cann, and many other Utility Competition Winners.—Local Agent : Messrs. Harrison and Co., Fearnhead. A trial will prove its value —Manufacturers, J. ContiALL &amp; SON, Kirkham.</p>
      </div>
