      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District League does not open the season until Saturday, May 4th. The prospects are regarded as entirely sati,factory, although the number of clubs in the League has dropped from nine to eight. Sankey Wire Mills and Latchford Wesleyan clubs have ceased to exist, but a new club—St. Helens Cable Company - has been admitted to membership and in all probability another local team will form before the tourney begins. The officials of the League are :—Chairman, Mr J W Travis, who Las had con- siderable experience in Liverpool league cricket; Mr C Hackney, treasurer; and Mr C E Hawthorn, secretary. The rules have been revised and the League is Low on a thoroughly business-like basis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT ASSOCIATION. The sub-committee appointed by the Council of the above association are making elaborate arrangements for the coming season and are fixing up all the engagements. It is expected that the Individual Merit Competition will take place in the season. The season will open eariy in May. All the green to which members are attached are reported to be in good order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB'S ATTRACTIVE</p>
        <p>PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Cycling Club opens its season to-day with a hot-pot and smoking concert at head- quarters, Patten Arms Hotel, at 7.30 p.m. and in view of the unqualified success of the last similar venture carried out by the club a large muster of members is expected. A musical programme of the usual high standard associated with the club gather- ings has been arranged and members and their friends who intend being present can anticipate spending an enjoyable evening. The wheel section of the club is to renew its acquaintance with the open road on Saturday next, when the opening run will be carried out to Hunts Cross, where the bowling green at the Hunts Cross Hotel has been reserved for the afternoon. A party of the bowling section of the club will attend the meet- ing and make the journey by rail. As the initial Ifixture usually has some bearing on the runs to follow, a good send-off to the season is hoped for. A very diversified and attractive list of fixtures has been compiled for the season. Many'popular arrange- ments such as the Beeston Visit, Fishing Match, inter- club runs, tours and rides through country unsur- passed for delightful surroundings, are again down on the list, and will it is hoped, bring out good musters of the wheel section during the season. Whilst an attractive list of runs has been arranged for the road section the interests of a strong bowling section of the members has been fully recognized and the programme for that department has been made quite as attractive as the one arranged for the road section and where possible the cycling and bowling interests have been brought together. Apart from the Wednesday evening bowling com- petitions carried out at Sankey Chapel House which are well supported during the summer, meet- ings are to be carried out with the Warrington Bowl- ing Club at Sankey and Arpley, the Caledonian Association at Sankey and Walton, Commerciall Travellers at Hunt's Cross, etc. The Wednesday evening bowling meetings at Sankey will be resumed on May Ist and will again be attractive to cycling and non-cycling members alike. The club is to render assistance at the " M.P.F." Old English Fair to be carried out on the 11th, 12th and 13th July at Victoria Park, and whilst helping a worthy effort the members duties on those days will no doubt be of a congenial nature. It is hoped that the Club will have a successful season, and of its doings we hope to bear anon.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CYCLING CLUB. The afternoon of last Saturday took the club to Dunham Massey. Seventeen sat down to tea at Mrs. Bragg's, near the Corn Mill, and as the evening was still young, and very beautiful, the captain was persuaded to lengthen the run by Bowden and Ashley, returning by the road skirting the west side of Tatton Park. Many times did the riders remark on the superb view to the left 8,8 that two miles is enjoyed. It consists of a brick wall eight feet high, and ought to be taxed in the next Budget at so much per foot lineal per annum. Perhaps, then, our noble landlords would permit cyclists and other tourists the privilege cf looking at the grass and trees of their parks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ASSOCIATION (N.A. CENTRE). On Sunday last the train service was again convenient for angling at the Gowy and the weather being ideal several anglers took advantsge of it. The Northern anglers who visited this spot seemed all in the same mind—intent on beating the trout already weighed in. But they were disappointed as the water has not been seen so low for considerable time and trout were not to be lured. Several took a few eels but not being satitfied with th,se they all visited the bottom End during the afternoon to try if they could entice Mr Jack with the worm, but with to better results and taking the day into consideration sport was very poor indeed. If the weather holds out like it is doing up to the time of writing we shall have several members trying the canals this week end to have the first bag of eels to weigh for the special prize. Acton Bridge is now worth a trial and the member who first catches them on the run will no doubt introduce them to the scales. I have heard of several members preparing for pike fishing this wrek end, and now that cheap travelling is open again for anglers, Chester way will no doubt be their rendezvous. A.S.</p>
        <p>GOLF. M ATC Er.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LADIES \;":17,171"qm. LADrEs.—Played at Lymm, on Wednesday. Scores : Miss D. Richardson 1 Miss Nicholson 0 Miss K. Picton Mrs Hepherd Mrs E A White 0 Mrs Wood 1 Miss Bainbridge 0 Mrs Palmer 1 Miss D Hall 0 Miss Norris 1</p>
      </div>
