      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>CUMBERLAND LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>CUMBERLAND LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Brookland Rovers 2 1 16 Dearhatn United 1 2 8 HULL AND DISTRICT CUP.—Final</p>
        <p>Hull Kingston R A 4 5 23 Pearson Instit... 1 0 2</p>
        <p>NORTH-WESTERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Millom ST. HELENS OUSTED FROM THE 10P FOUR. By their victory over St. Helens at Parkside the Honslet Club have made good their claim to meet Huddersfield at Fartown on Saturday next in the supple- mentary championship of the League. It was a win that was in accordance with popular expectation and one that was also deserved on the play. About 10,000 onlookers were present. Ilunslet were the stronger and cleverer side, especially in the rcar division, and the introduction of Whitater as stand-off half proved a great success. As the Hunslet forwards were assiduous in their duties in heeling out to Smith, the home backs were constantly in possession of the ball, and some sparkling play was exhibited by IV Batten Jenkinscn, J Batten, Banks, and Smith, whose quick exchanges were delightful to watch. Hunslet should have teen three points up in the first few minutes had Banks immediately grounded the ball after he hadireceived it from W Batten, but the American tried to run round some opponents, and in doing so he stepped over the dead ball line. The quality of the Hunslet back play was shown when NV Batten Whittaker, Banks, and Jenkinson got away in combination. It was a fine movement, the defence being completely outwitted and Jenkinson easily scored a try. The following twenty minutes proved a trying lime for St Helens, so brilliant was the combination shown by the Hunslet team. It was effective, too, as shown by the fact that Smith, Jukes, and W Batten pierced the defence, the last- named being successful twice, During the second period, when Hunslet were lead iog by 19 points to 2, Browning having been successful with adrop at goal, St. Helens were deprived of the services of Turtill, who retired finally from the game with an injury. Jenkinson, Jukes and J Batten, by sound work in conjunction with the whole of the mem- bers of the side, succeeded in penetrating the visitors' defence, and St. Helens were beaten at all points. A fitful rally towards the end enabled Prescott to cross the line, Molyneux scaling. Whittaker kicked four goals for Hurslet, and W Batten was successful with one shot. Result :—Hunelet 5 goals 8 tries (31 points), St. Helens 2 goals 1 try (7 points). WIDNES OUTCLASSED AT WIGAN.</p>
        <p>A remarkably fast game was witnessed by about 7,000 persons at Central Park. Jenkins was in particularly fine trim, and be and Thomas and Btadley figured to great advantage throughout the encounter, the famous Welsh centre crediting himself with a couple of tries, and Thomas a couple of goals prior to the interval, the only response being a try gained by Swinton. In the second half Widnes did a considerable amount of attacking, but were never able to cross the line, whereas Bradley and Williams both best the defence, and Thomas annexed two more goals, one from a penalty kick soon after the resumption, and the other from the try secured by Williams. Result :—Wigan 4 goals 4 tries (20 points), Widnes 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM DISTRICT LEAGUE. FR ST DIVISION. RESULTS TO DATE. _</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. F. A. PTS. Mobberlev "2 .15 ... 4 ... 3 ... 62 ... 27 ...33 Bowdon Vale "2 .14 .. 4 4 51 40 32 Lostook Gralam ...20 ...14 ... 3 ... 3 ... 94 ... 23 —.31 Latch ford 99 ..13 . 7 2.. 50 36 ..28 Lymm "1 .11 .. 6 4 .52 39 —.26 Oughtrington Park2o 7 ... 8 ... 5 ... 50 ... 36 ...19 Oughtrington Am'...22 ... 7 ...10 ... 5 ... 39 ... 33 _l9 Broadheath ... 7... 9 ... 5... 48... 48 ...19 St. Vincent's 1-Pio . 6 8 6.. 25 ... 36 ...18 Altrincham Lads: ... 6 ...14 ... 1 ... 31 ... 56 ...13 Sale Albion 20 . 3 ...14 3 ... 26 ... 52 ... 9 Kuutsford St Cross2l ... 1 ...17 ... 3 ... 27 ...128 ... 3 BROADHEATII V. LOSTOCK GRALAM. At Navigation-road, Altrincharn, on Saturday. Final : Lostock Gralam, three goals ; Broadheath, one goal. OUGIITRINGTON AMATEURS V. ALTRINCITAM LADS.</p>
        <p>At Hee tley, in splendid weather. This was the Amateurs' last engagement in the Senior Division, and as they had the previous Monday easily accounted for the Lads away from home, they anticipated finishing up with a victory. The game opened remarkably even, midfield play being the general order, neither side claiming any advantage. As the game went on the orwird play imnroved and at the interval matters we d a rved'y 'qua!, with the [core one goal each, Grestyr scoring fur toe Amateurs and Brown (penalty) for the Lads. In the second half the superior combination of the Lads told its own tale and Johnson gave them the lead. No further scoring took place. Altrincham Lads, two goals ; Oughtrington Amateurs, one go3l.</p>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON PARK V. KbaTTSVORD Sr. CROSS. At fleatley. This was a terribly one-sided game all through. Oughtrington Park had matters all their own way, running out the easiest of winners with the huge score : Oughtrington Park, 15 goals ; St. Cross, nil</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>OUGHT-RINGTON PARK Y. LOSTOCK GRALAM•</head>
        <p>OUGHT-RINGTON PARK Y. LOSTOCK GRALAM•</p>
        <p>—.,......... At fleatley. Play opened fair and even, both defences being very sound. As the game progressed Oughtring- ton Park took the major share of the game; in fact they showed far better form than at any other part of the season, in the end running out winners with a score : Oughtrington Park, two goals; Lostock Gralam nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LYMM V. ST. VINCENT'S.</head>
        <p>LYMM V. ST. VINCENT'S.</p>
        <p>At Lymm. For this game both teams were well represented and a stern struggle was witnessed. Taking the game all through, the " Saints" showed vast improvement on recent games and sprung a rare surprise on their opponents, the final score being : St. Vincent's three goals; LVmm, one goal.</p>
        <p>_ . _ SECOND DIVISION. SANKEY V. STOCKTON HEATH Sr. THOMAS'.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former. Although occupying such a lowly position, St. Thomas' gave their opponents a good game. Scores: Sankey, six goals; St. Thomas', two goals. WELT.FIELD V. CHRIST CHURCH. On the ground of tbe former. At the commencement Christ Church showed the better form and at the expiration of ten minutes opened the score. Even play followed, both defences having hard work to clear their line on many occasions. About five minutes before the interval Welltield equalized matters. In the second half Welifield had the better of the game, but could not overcome the Church defence, which gave a good account of itself,a very pleasant game ending with the scores of a goal each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The Peter Peacock Challenge Cup.</p>
        <p>Great interest is being taken in the forthcoming con- test for the handsome challenge cuptpres-nt-d by Mr. Peter Peacock, and 38 tetms, representing 529 boa lers, have entered. Final arrangements were made at a largely-attended meeting of the committee held on Wednesday evening Older the chairmanship of Coun- cillor Wilkinson. The games are to he played on the green attached to the Bowling Green Inn, Bank Quay, commencing on Saturday, May 4th. The draw is es follows.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>FIRST ROUND.</head>
        <p>FIRST ROUND.</p>
        <p>Games to be played on Saturday, May 411 :- 1 Whitecross G v St. Austins E 2 St. Austins B v Whitecre ss C 3 Mitre-Tors D v Penfield A Referee, Mr John Willett ; gatekeeper, Mr J Davies ; assistant, Mr G Fairfield. 4 Fairfield C v Town Hall B I 5 Howley A v Orford C SECOND ROUND. Orford B v Latchford C Whitecross E v Whitecross H St John's B v Winner of No 3 - St John's C v Fairfield D St Austin's C v Winner of No 2 Winner of No 5 v Town Hall A Winner of No 4 v Winner of No 1 Bewsey C v Fairfield B Bewsey A v Bewsey B Whitecross F v Latchford A 'Howley D v Orford A • Whitecross A v St John's D St Austin's A v St Austin's D Latchford B v Juniors</p>
        <p>Howley B v St John' A Ho*ley C v Whitecross B The games will begin at 2 o'clock. Should any of the matches be postponed on account of bad weather or any other unforeseen circumstance, the committee will re-arrange them after the remaining games have been played. All teams will be notified by the secretary (Mr E A Sanders) as to the time they will be expected on the green. Non-competititors will he admitted to the green at a charge of one penny each.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Finlayson Cup Competition.</p>
