        <p>The latest news is</p>
        <p>THE TITNIC little better that the AND HER BOATS. first. Nothing tha</p>
        <p>can alleviate the; character of the disaster in the Atlantic is to be hoped for. We must not I look for the re-appearance of those who are believed to be dead. It will be said, of course that such a disaster could not have taken place unless the big ship had been trying to make a big record. This may or may not be true, but it is not a thing to be said at the present moment. The Titanic was not going further north than other vessels which were crossing the Atlantic at the same time. The French liner which warned Captain SmrriCabout the iceberg, had actually passed over the spot— or something very nearly the spot—which was fatal to the Titanic. We may assume, therefore, that there was no foolhardy taking' of risks in the interest of a quick passage. This may, of course, mean nothing more than that all great steamships take risks in pro- ceeding to America. Few ships cross the Atlantic without in the latest stages of their journey feeling the chill, if not coming within the danger, of icebergs. Ocean-racing might be regarded as the cause of the loss of the Titanic if that gigantic vessel had not been pursuing the ordinary path through the Atlantic. Is that ordinary path a safe one? Obviously not. It now appears that we may dismiss alto- gether the story that great quantities of ice fell on the deck of the Titanic. This fact which would be destructive of all earlier speculations as to the cause of the disaster seems to have been invented, like so much of the other news that has come from America It is far more likely that the Titanic came across a "washed " piece of ice, which is known among sailors as a "growler." That is, she ran up against a submerged iceberg. The berg against which the tremendous steamer came in contact must have been very large indeed, and the Titanic, it seems clear, must have been shattered by the first blow. If she was then going at sixteen knots an hour, or at some slight slowing down from that pace after the warning from the French liner, nothing could be " unsinkable " in face of such a tremendous impact. Every platt must have started. Probably enough, every means of securing safety must have been put out of gear. What is beyond question is that the greatest steamship ever built was as helpless before the obstruction it encountered as if it had been a North Sea fishing boat in a tremendous gale.</p>
        <p>This is not the time to form opinions as to why this occurred or why the other did not take place. We forbear, for the present, to make inquiries as to whether the provision of lifeboats was sufficient for so tremendous an occasion. But that is a question of the most tremendous importance, which will have to be faced. It must have come as a violen shock to most people to learn that liners go to sea with boat provision for only a fraction of the human beings they carry. It is said that the provision of boats for all is impossible</p>
        <p>in the giant ships o to-day. "Impossible is not a word to be used in this conn( We are not surprised to learn that tht an agitation already in America for legislai on this subject. A similar agitation _ certainly spring up in England. But for th' moment it is well not to apply these; ings to the case of the Titanic, since we not yet know exactly what happened board that vessel. It is not reasonable, course, to say that the disaster is a quence of the building of enormous ships. same accident might have occurred t° Newfoundland fishing vessel, and we shot' not have heard of it. There are at pr no grounds for any sort of generalisation to our methods of crossing the Atlantic. are simply in face of an unprecedt disaster. How it came about is a matter inquiry, and for inquiry we can wait. the time being we can only wonder - lament.</p>
        <p>The clock controv( seems to be resol' itself into a 'r argument between Mr. Charles E. Ps and Mr. W. P.Rylands, the respective' leaders. Lady Greenall, though a wc. allowed us to have the last word in the pute proper, which, we would. at emphasise, turned on the propriety of Ladyship purchasing German clocks prizes for the exhibition which was str</p>
        <p>THE CLOCKS.</p>
        <p>to demonstrate the evils of dumping. Ladyship admitted the purchase of duali clocks, and then embarked upon an ing( attempt at justification, forgetful of the that "he who excuses, accuses." We Pro. beyond cavil or contradiction that</p>
        <p>Gieenall could have saved her reputation consistency by purchasing clocks mc England, and no amount of arguint fiscal or otherwise, will disabuse public mind of the effect of that tunate purchase of German clocks anti-dumpers. Mr. Rylands may ingenious _ questions, now that</p>
        <p>_ . . Greenall has retired, from the fray, IAI,I will only get involved in one Of dialectical nets which Mr. Parker is so in spreading for correspondents who swords with him on questions colic economics.</p>
        <p>The election TOWN HALL WARD Thursday, to fill ELECTION, vacancy in the re) sentation of Town Ward, caused by the death of Colllc Rogers, seems likely to resolve itself n? keen party fight. A contest on 190111 lines was unnecessary and undesirable. Firth Lindley fought the late memf November, 1910, and received such a measure of support, that he had a I claim on the next seat for the ward. soon as the vacancy was declared Lindley came forward, and would have, an unopposed return but for the fact the", Conservatives could not forget that be a Liberal. They have, in conse(ll persuaded Mr. J. Banks to contest the in their interests, and the party macl is now in full swing. We have no - that Mr. Lindley will be returned e,t head of the poll. His worth and poPnii were testified in no unmistakable fee when he enteted the lists for the first 18 months ago, and his claims have strengthened since then. He not only and carries on business in the Ward, is a man of sterling character and sL judgment—just the type of man rie,6 to-day when the rates are going up bY and bounds. We hope to see him retll..' by a majority which will give palls` opponents who have forced an unnr contest.</p>
