      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>orb, •</head>
        <p>orb, •</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS. '</p>
        <p>Brabazon Sale.</p>
        <p>A sale of work made by inmates of the Warrington Union Hospital will be held in the Winmarleigh Cafe, on Thursday next. It will be opened at three o'clock by Mrs. Roscoe Brunner.</p>
        <p>Judge Stanger's Appointment. His Honour Judge H. Yorke Stanger, K.O , County Court Judge of Circuit 7 (which includes Warrington) has been appointed independent chairman for Nottingham, under the Minimum Wage Boards created by the Mines Act.</p>
        <p>LAWN MOWERS ground by special machinery. Send your machines now before the rush.—J. S. GARLAND 51, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>, . The Old English -Fair. At a committee meeting, which was largely attended on Monday evening, it was decided to again hold an Old English Faiir in Victoria Park for the benefit of the Infirmary Million Penny Fund. The dates decided upon were the 116, 12th, and 13th July. Muslim' Successes.</p>
        <p>At the recent examinations held in Liverpool under the auspices of the London College of Music, Miss Eva Whitby was successful in passing the inter- mediate test in pianoforte playing, obtaining 93 marks out of a possible 100. Miss Whitby, who is only 11 years of age, is a pupil of Miss A. Oakley, A L.C.M , Wellfield-street. Picturedrome.</p>
        <p>There has been another successful week at the Picturedrome and crowded houses have been the rule The films shown have been of the best as usual and the reputation of this house has been well maintained. For next week the management have secured in addition to numerous others the two excellent films, '• The vengeance of Mafia" and "For the Crown." MOTOR CYCLES.—Agent for " Douglas " &amp; "Premier.' New Models now in stock. Trials and all information free.-J.S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warrington.-Advt Death of Mr. G. Ratcliffe. It i 4 with regret that we antr:unce the death of Mr. George Ratcliffe, brushrnaker, Colborne-street. Mr, Ratcliffe first complained of illness on Saturday last while at his shop. and in the evening he had to be c nveyed home. Dr. Smith was called in but he gradually became worse and died at one p.m. on Thursday. Mr. Ratcliffe leaves a widow and five daughters. The deceased had been a member of St. Paul's Church Choir for the past 31 years. He was also a member of the old Apollo Choir and had recently joined the Warrington Male Voice Choral Union. He took a great interest in the Warrington Musical Festival.</p>
        <p>The Late Councillor Rogers. At a meeting of the Conservative Town Hall Ward Committee, held on Monday evening at the Conserva- tive Club, the following resolution was unanimously adoved, on the motion of Councillor Jenkins, seconded by Air. L. A Voisey :—" That this meeting desires to express its deep regret at the irreparable loss which the party has sustained by the death of one of their representatives, Councillor Rogers, and begs to convey to Mrs. Rogers and family its sincere sympathy and condolence in their sad bereavement." " PREMIER " CYCLES.—SpeCifiI lines at £5 10s. or with S speed gear, £6 15s.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Male Choral Union.</p>
        <p>In connection with this organisation the annual meeting and dinner was held at Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge-street, on Monday. The Hon. Secretary and the Hon Treasurer, Messrs W Taylor and J respectively presented their reports, which showed the society to be in a very flourishing condition and in a happy position financially. The following officials were elected for the ensuing year:: President, Mr A H Crosfield ; musical trainer, Mr W S Nesbitt; hon. conductor, Mr S Hassall ; accompanists, Messrs F Atkinson and H J Idtion ; hon. treasurer, Mr J Phillips; committee, Messrs N Cook, It W Cook, T F Griffiths, H Wild, W Critchley, W A Clay and J Powell; hon. auditors, Messrs J Moss and P Gillespie. VACUUM CLEANERS, thoroughly reliable machines rom 378. 6d.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmongers, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>TOWN HALL WARD ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL WARD ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>A Political Fight.</p>
        <p>On Thursday next the electors of the Town Hall Ward will be called upon to elect a tepresentatiye to fill the vacancy on the Town Council caused by the death of Councillor W. Rogers. Mr. Firth Lindley, who made such a plucky fight in November, 1910, when he was defeeted by the late Councillor Rogers by a majority of 36, is again standing as the Liberal candidate. He was selected by the Liberal Ward Committee at their meeting last Friday evening and •on Monday the Conservative Committee met and adopted Mr. Joseph Banks, gentlemen's outfitter, Horsemarket-street, as their candidate. A keen party fight is promised but there is every indication that Mr. Lindley will be triumphant.</p>
        <p>„ , 1.• • NI: f••••,...,PeNt`,s•:!#.4, tgiklf?:;92.l.§l4: :„.••••••••:,....!.,4,t-i4 • • , • •• , . . . „ . . . . , , N _</p>
        <p>Photo Billies. LEY.</p>
