      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>R. FIRTH LIND</head>
        <p>R. FIRTH LIND</p>
        <p>Mr Firth Lindley . (L.), (fruit merchant), Bewsey- road, is proposed by Councillor James Halliwell, seconded by Air Thomas Locker, and supported by Messrs William Taylor, J A Gibson, John Allman, William Ford, R A Halford, W G Stephens, W H Pickup, aud Hugh McDermott. The candidate is also proposed by Mr George Wallington, Mr William j Barlow, unr., and Margaret N Mitchell, and seconded by Mr Charles Healey and Mary R )berts. In his first paper Mr Joseph Banks (C), gentlemen's outfitter. Hunt s-lane, Latchford Without, is proposed by Councillor Jenkins and seconded by Mr. Robert Straw. Sir other papers were banded in on his behalf. Mr. Lindley is a Yorkshireraan and in early life worked on the railway. He then became traveller for a Hall firm of fruit merchants and represented them until fifteen years ago when he came to Warrington and began business on his own account as a wholesale fruit merchant. A shrewd business man he would make an ideal representative of the ward. He not only carries on business but resides in the ward and may be relied upon to study its interests in every way. In his address to the electors, Mr. Lindley says: • It is with alarm I view the increasing rates, and the consequent detrimental effect upon trade, and you could rest assured of my efforts being used to curtail any unnecessary expenditure ; also all matters relating to sanitary arrangements affecting the health of the inhabitants would be carefully considered. I am in favour of a standard rate of wages to all work- men, including Corporation men. I also feel strongly that townspeople should have preference of employ- ment.</p>
        <p>WINDFALL FOR PRINTERS' CORPORATION. The Secretary of the Printers' Pension Corporation has received a letter from a firm of solicitors saying that by a will of one of their clients the Corporation will be entitled to a reversion which will probably amount to £750 or £B5O, subject to duty. Tbe solici- tors add that their client's interest in your Corporo- tion was aroused through his perusal of 'Printers' Pie. " Printers' Pie for this year will be published on May 13th.</p>
      </div>
