      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BRIDE AND BRIDEGROOM.</head>
        <p>THE BRIDE AND BRIDEGROOM.</p>
        <p>There were picturesque scenes at Penketh on Tues- day afternoon, when the marriage was solemnized at the Wesleyan Church of Miss Helen Mary Parker, eldest daughter of Mr. and Mrs C. E. Parker, Penketh Lodge, Penketh, and the Pm Edward Joseph Gawne, The Manse, Leyland, son a Mr. Edward Gawne, of Ramsey, ls!e of Man. The bridegroom was stationed in the Warrington Wesleyan circuit a few yeats ego, having special charge of the Penketh Wesleyan Church. While in this ciicuit he made many warm friends, and his worth and work were much appreciated. At Leyland, near Preston, a here Mr. Gawne now successfully labours, he is held in high esteem, and several of his Leyland and Preston friends were amongst the guests. Miss Parker is universally beloved in her native village, there her quiet efficient helpfulness in every useful work, and her self-forgetfulness in the service of others, have endeared her to all.</p>
        <p>The brilliant sunshine cf a delightful spring day gave the ceremony a perfect setting, and on every hand were evidences of the exceptional interest which the occasion aroused. The village was gay with flags and bunting, and its usual peacefulness gave place to animation, large crowds assembling in the approaches to the church to watch the arrival of ' the guests and bridal party. The body of the church was filled with</p>
        <p>guests and visitors, and the beautiful dresses I of the ladies made a striking array of colcur. A simple but artistic scheme of decoradon had been carried out, liliums, smilax, tulips, spirtra, white geraniums and tall palms having been arranged with beautiful effect around the pulpit and screen. While the guests were assembling, the organist (Mr. A. W. Clatworthy) played choice selections, and the bridal precession moved up the aisle to the strain, of the "Bridal March " from "Lohent-°wm." The service, la which was fully choral, began wit the hymn "God of all being, throned afar." The psalm " Lord be ' merciful unto us, and bless us," and the hymn "0 perfect love " were also sung. Th, officiating ministers were the Rev. T. Galland Hartley, of Preston, and the Rev. John Gunnell, of Peaked'. The bride was given away by both parents. Mr. and Mrs. Parker appear to think there is s• mething wrong with the idea from which springs the old custom of the father alone givina away his child. We suppose it comes ' from far off days when the husband and father was considered to own wife and children as pcssessions to be dealt with at will. In this case both parents stood by their daughter at the altar and answered the usual question of •` Who give th this woman— " ? by the response" We do," and together joined the hands. The bride was beautifully attired in ivory•crpe de chine, with long train of Oriental satin. She wore a veil of Brussels net, with lace appliqut: border, and carried a sheaf of lilies. The four sisters of the bride—the Misses Ruth,Rosamond, Victoria, and Phyllis Parker acted as bridesmaids. Their dresses were of liberty satin, these of the senior bridesmaids being pale pink and those of the younger bridesmaids, pale green. They wore fiat's of cream ninon and line French lace, held with clusters of pink carnations, and the two little maids carried baskets of flowers. The Rev. J, D. Hall, of Sutton Coldfields, discharged the duties of best man.</p>
        <p>On leaving the church the Bev. E. J. and Mrs. Gawne were greeted with showers of confetti. Enter- ing an open carriage, they drove through the village to Penketh Lodge where the reception was held, the guests passing through the house to the marquee which had been erected on the lawn. The guests numbered about 140, and included many of the bride's relatives, Mrs. Garnett (who looked very happy at the marriage of her first grandchild), Mrs. William Parker (Bristol), Doctor J. Gordon and Mrs. Parker (London), Mr. and Mrs. Charles Doeg (Blackpool), Mr. and Mrs. It. E. Garnett, Hr. and Mrs. R. 11. Posnett, Mr. and Mrs. C. B. Heal, Mr. and Mts. P. C. Garnett, each with their families. The Penketh Tannery Brass Band supplied an excellent programme of music in the grounds at Penketh Lodge. Later in the day, the newly-married couple left for London, en route for the Italian Lakes, where the honeymoon will be spent. The wedding presents were very handsome and numerous, and included a silver salver from the indoor and outdoor staff of Penketh Lodge, and a huge silver plated horse shoe from the village blacksmith, which was suspended vith a chain of thavers over the entrance to Penketh Lodge. The Winmarleigh Caf6 Co. supplied the bride's cake, and catered at Penketh Lodge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>SHAKESPEARE COMMEMORATION. NEXT WEEK'S PERFORMANCES OF " RICHARD III." AND " JULIUS CESAR."</head>
        <p>SHAKESPEARE COMMEMORATION. NEXT WEEK'S PERFORMANCES OF " RICHARD III." AND " JULIUS CESAR."</p>
        <p>Warrington's second Shakespeare Commemoration will take place at the Royal Curt Theatre on Thui s- day, Friday and Saturday next. It speaks well for the courage and ambition of our local amateur players that they have undertaken the great task of presenting two tragie and historical plays which make exceptional demands even upon the abilities of experienced professionals—" Richard III." and "Julius Cmsar " —and the forthcoming performances have naturally aroused much interest and eager anticipation. Mr. Charles D. Law, who is producing both plays, was yesterday interviewed by a representative of the " Examiner ' and courteously supplied information as to the scope and prospects of the enterpi ise. The succces3 of last year's revival of " Macbeth " was so gratifying that there was a general opinion • that the society might confidently embark upon a three nights' commemoration on its second appear- ance before the public. It was &amp;so thought that greater interest would be added to the commemora- tion if repreeentaticins of two plays instead of one, could be given. The company engaged in the present venture numbers nearly eigh.ty - undoubtedly the largest company ever seen on the Royal Court stage —and the mounting of the plays will be on a strikingly lavish scale. " Richard III." will be presented far oae night only, Thursday.—While considerable ettr'?:ii.Grilt. has been found necessary to cô.jsies t Within the limits of an evening's production,itwill be found that a consecutive story is preserved and that the main issue —the all-consuming ambition of Richard, realizing his physical disadvantages but intellectual superiority, to secure the Crown of England is always kept well to the fore. The play will open according to the Skakespearian text—instead of being preceded, as is frequently the case, -by a scene from Henry VI. and the &amp;queries of events will be closely adhered to. Some of the scenes attendant upon the consignment to thetower and the murder of the little princes have been omitted but the fine third scene in the first act, in which Queen Margaret figures, has been retained, and considerable prominence is also given to Richmond. The society is presenting the play in the way Hazlitt advocated,that is by curtailing the originaltext,instead of eubstituting new versions, such as the notorious Colley Cibber "Richard III." "We are playing 'Richard III.'" Mr. Law remarked "because we regard it as an educational work from the historical point of view, adherence to the facts of history being generally, though not absolutely, so exact, as to give a very true idea of the state of England at a time when the country was torn asunder by the rival factions of York and Lancaster and there was an absolute disregard of the value of human life." _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Law readily conceded that for amateurs to'tackle such a work was a very big undertaking. As Hazlitt has put it, "Richard III." is a play primarily for the stage and not for the student. "It is," said Mr Law, "so full of life and vigour and the noise of war that it cannot be otherwise than appealing to the people who act it as well as to the spectators. On that account' ' Richard lII.' has a most from time immemorial been regarded as the most successful Shakespearian play by means of which to entertain an audience."</p>
        <p>The cast for next Thursday's production is as follows: Ring Edward IV. Mr H M Ashton. Edward, Prince of Wales Miss M Truelove Richard, Duke of York .. Miss D Truelove. George, Duke of Clarence Mr John Lowe Richard, Duke cf Gloucester (Richard III.) Mr Chas D Law. Henry, Earl of Richmond Mr Leslie L Langford. Duke of Buckingham Mr Harvey OAt S. , Lord Hastings Mr W H Brown. Lord Stanley Mr 0 Wheeler. Queen Elizabeth Miss Elsie Tunstall, Queen Margaret Miss Mlle Royston. Duchess ef Yorz Miss Mary Brundrett. Lady Anne Miss Gladys Adams. Through the kindness of Mr Richard Flanagan, Manchester, and with the assistance of the governors of the Stratford Memorial Theatre, and of the F. R. Benson Company, Ltd. (through their manager, Mr. \V. H. Savory), the anciety will be enabled to stage the play, as far as dresses and equipment are con- cerned, in a way that coed not be witnessed anywhere except in London or one of the great provincial towns. From the spectacular point of view, the funeral of King Henry VI; the entry of the princes into London, the departure of Richard to fight against Richmond, and the battle of Bosworth should be exceedingly effective stage pictures. On Friday and Saturday evening " Julius Caner" will be played '1 his work has been selected because of its classical nature and the high plane of language throughout, and also because it gives opportunities for the assembly of large stage crowds in such great and moving spectacles as the entrance of Ctesar and the Forum scene. The work will be presented in four acts, instead of in the customary five, the whole of Act 3 being devoted to the Forum. The play will open with the tribunes rebuking the crowd for show- ing supreme regard to Ca-sar, instead of remembering Pompey. Then we pass to the triumphal passage of Cesar through the streets of Rome, on his way to the games, at which he is offered the crown. The persuasion of Brutus by Cassius and the other con- spirators to kill Cwsar is the next episode. Scene 3 of Act 2is set in the Senate where Caesar meets his death. In the succeeding Forum (Nene there will be an animated crowd of eighty people and it is anticipated that the climax will provide a triumph of stage management. After Act 3 there is considerable curtailment, every effort being made to terminate the play after the great dominating episodes have passed, but the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius and the parleying between Octavius and Antony and Brutus and Cassius are retained. Then follows a stirring tableau of the battle on the plains of Phillipi and after the play has concluded with the death of Brutus there will be a final tableau which the audience should make a point of staying ta see. The principals in "Julius Ca'sar ' are as follows : Julius C COE ar Mr. Harvey 0. Adams Octavins Csar Mr. W. H. Brown Mark Antony Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. Brutus Mr. C. D. Law Cassius Mr. John Lowe Caeca ... Mr. H. M Ashton Decius Brutus Mr. Leonard Daintith Calphurnia Miss E. Tunstall Portia Miss Molly Fairclough Lucius Mrs. Law The mounting of this play will also be of the most complete description. The scenery will be from the Memorial Theatre, Stratford-on-Avon, and the society is using a very fine wardrobe obtained for them by the F. R. Benson Company, and magnificent Roman armour lent by Mr. Flanagan. Some idea of the magnitude of the undertaking may be gained from the fact that about 100 costumes will be used in the two plays. Our local amateurs have been working most strenuously and conscientiously on the two plays for nearly six months. Every effort is being made to ensure that the productions shall be worthy of the commemoration of Shakespeare which, after all, is the primary object. It entirely depends upon the response of the Warrington publicwhether the commemoration shall be well established as an annual event for, even with the kind assistance which has been forthcoming from the sources named, the expenses are very considerable. An enormous amount of work has also been involved. for the production of two- Shakespearan plays on three evenings is a task sufficiently formidable to perturb even 8 professional manager. Mr. Law was very cordial in his acknowledgment of the kindness of Sir Joseph Beecham in placing the theatre at the disposal of the Society and of Mr. A.M. Loader, general manager, whose co-operation has been invaluable.</p>
        <p>The Shops Bill. Sir,—lt would be interesting to know if the shop- keepers of Warrington intend to take any action under the new Shops Bill which comes into force on May Ist next, with regard to the earlier and uniform closing of shops. This would require a closing order. Surely, now that great concern the Co-operative Stores are closing at six p.m. and nine o'clock on Saturday, the other traders could close a little earlie r.—Y ou r s etc.,</p>
        <p>FORWARD.</p>
        <p>‘COAGULINE,' Transparent Cement for Glass, China ao. 6d. and is., post free.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The populatity of the-vaudeville theatre increases as each year passes by and it needs no extraordinary mental exertion to discover the reason. It is comm.,n knowledge that the motto of our American cousins— hustle and bustle—is being followed in every phase of life and in view of the fact that there was so little time to be devoted to amusements it was round necessary to provide a first-class entertainment in a very short time, hence the advent of the music hall. It is not every man who can spare a whole night on a visit to a theatre, and therefore he naturally turns to the music-halt, where for two hours he is entertained in a really pleasing manner. One of these popular places of amusement is the Hippodrome, where large numbers of Warrington people make a weekly visit. A slack ' week at the Hippodrome, so far as turns go, is the exception and not the rule and this week's programme is a typical one, We had a fine exhibition of what could be done in the lasso business last week, but it is safe to say that the turn provided by Shields and Rogers is far and away the better. In addition to several demonstrations of tieing up an Indian, and several tricky throws, these two individuals perform a series of intricate aerobatics in and through the whirling rope, one of the most thrilling being that in which one of the two does a daring somersault the while he manipulates the long lasso. James Dixon possesses a good round baritone voice And his renditions of "The Trumpeter '' and " Come back to me " were thoroughly appreciated. In a novel comedy musical scena, the Rurals score a decided success. In addition to some excel- lent singing there are also many humorous incidents embodied in the scena, which are sure to amuse. The Wedburns are clever cross-talkers and comedians, their "gags" being very smart and out of the common. Sh'rley and Ransom collaborate in a Cockney sketch, which is humorous and pathetic in turn. Madge Osmond at once captivates the audience with her buoyant air and her catchy songs. She is a first:class male impersonator, and has a winning style which marks her a favourite. Hurl and Ford are styled as comedians and dancers, but the humour of the opening duet was difficult to discover. However they are thorough masters of the terpsichorean art, and if they failed as laughter provokers they were successful as dancers. The Two Boyds also appear in an instrumental and dancing act. In addition there are two splendid cinematographic productions. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. Next week patrons of the Palace and Hippodroffie will have the opportunity of hearing the Jackson family who have a good reputation as musical enter- tainers. Ara, Zebra and Vora will present their novely, "Indians on the Prairk" and George Payne's Wanderers will appear in a whistling, singing and dancing scena. Edna Lyall, described as the Melba, of variety will also appear. Ada Catley, wire artist and juggler, will no dnubt please as also will the other numerous artistes booked to appear.</p>
        <p>WORK RESUMED. Less Distress in Warrington. Several of the large works which were closed down in consequence of the strike*are now in full 'swing and in others there has been a partial resumption of employment. It is expected that early next week only a comparatively small number of the workpeople who were displaced by the shortage of coal will remain idle. With the re-opening of the works there has been a considerable diminution of distress, reflected in the reduced call upon the Mayor's relief fund. Work at the Central Relief Offices has been critinued but it has consisted chiefly of renewing the orders for assistance given in the previous week. There are many cases in which workmen who have resumed work have not earned sufficient to enable them to provide • for their families until they have had a full week's employment, and in other cases no wages will be forthcoming for a week as it is the custom of some firms to keep a week's wages in band. The number of families dealt with at the Relief Offices during the week has been aboat 050, in addition to others who have been fed at the works soup kitchens. We under- stand that all the soup kitchens, with the exception of those at the Whitecross works and Bewsey Adult school, will be closed this week-end. One of the out- standing features of the relief work has been the large number of cases in which the wives have been wholly ignorant of the amounts earned by their husbands. Inquiries have revealed the fact that some husbands, having large families to maintain, have retained for their personal use almost as much money as they have given to their wives to keep their homes going. A regrettable circum- stance has been the lack of any thrifty provision for a "rainy day" on the pait of many of the artisan class who have been in receipt of good wages. This remark, of course, does not apply to the lower kinds of labour in which the small amounts earned make it a practical impossibility to save. The Relief Offices will be opened on Tuesday next, but is expected that two days will suffice for the renewal of relief in needy cases. The feeding of school children continues at a rate of between 1,200 and 1,300 daily, but next Wednes- day there will be a revisioh of the books which will probably result in the figures assuming a more normal level. The Guild of Help has co-operated with the authori- ties and given valuable help in sifting various cases and testing the bona fides of the applicants for relief. THE RAILWAY SERVICES. The Londonund North-Western Railway Compant: give notice that commencing on Monday next the passenger train service shown in the company's time tables dated January to April will be restored with some exceptions, particulars of which can be obtained at the stations. _</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Lines Committee give notice that the Sunday service of passenger trains will, with few exceptions, be resumed to-morrow. Most of the ordinary week-day passenger trains will be reinstated , on Monday next.</p>
        <p>Bewsey Adult School. The Committeo of the Bewsey Adult School beg to thank everyone who contributed in money or kind to this fund. The amount in cash received by Mrs Pemberton amounted to .£26 Gs 7',d, and the accounts for food, coal for cooking, postages, etc., were .£26 4s lid, leaving a balance in hand of 19 SA. The accounts have been audited and found correct by Mr Charles Healey, accountant. The number of meals given out during this time was 6,876, with 226 quarts of milk. The work is still being carried on, but the money is being provided by the Mayor's fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MR. AND MRS. J. F. AWBERY.</head>
        <p>MR. AND MRS. J. F. AWBERY.</p>
        <p>Presentation from tne Tradesmen of Warrington. Mr. and Mrs. J. F. Awbery, who relinquish their work as master and matron of the Warrington Work- house this week-end, were on Thursday evening the irecipients of a handsome presentation from the trades- men of the tiwn. It took the form of two silver entree 1 dishes and a silver egg-stand, the dishes being inscribed 11 as follows :—" Presented to Mr. and Mrs. J. F. Awbery by the tradesmen of Warrington, to mark the esteem they have been held in by them, for their undeviating courtesy. April, 1012." The gifts had been supplied by Mr. F. Hallett, jeweller, Bridge-street The presentation was made by the MAYOR (Councillor Dr. Joseph), in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall, in the presence of a representative gathering. Mr. T. J. LEE, in an introductory speech, said Mr and Mrs Awbery were specialists in the administration of the Poor Law, especially in their own particular department, and it was gratifying to see the manner in which they were respected and how highly their work was appreciated. In recognition of the kindly and fair treatment and the great courtesy which the trades- men of Warrington had received at their hands, they had most cheerfully contributed to that handsome gift which he now asked the Mayor to present. The MAYOR raid it was not everyone who deserved appreciation that had the pleasure of experiencing ' it, whilst some °Aims who got appreciation did cot deserve it. In the cue of Mr and Mrs. Awbely, however, appreciation had been merited and be-towed. He was not talking of the I intrinsic value of those beautiful presents but of tbfi. goodwill of those with whom their duties bad brought them into contact, which was expressed in the presentation. He (the Mayor) had not known Mr and • his Awbery so long or so well as some of those present, but he knew them sufficiently well to be able to speak with great confidence. His worship referred to his official visit to the Workhouse on Christmas Dayi 1010, to the kindnets wh'ch Mr and Mrs Awbery then showed to all under their care, and to the affection which the inmates felt t3wards them. He also recalled the sensible, plain, homely address which Mr Awbery gave on that occasion. There was a touch of the human in it which everybody—rich and poor, but particularly the poor—could appreciate. It went to his (the speaker's) heart and he at ODCO said : " That man is a real gentleman at heart He treats these poor people more like friends than any- one else." Mr. and Mrs. Awbery had done their duty quiety and unostentatiously, but during the past twelve months romantic episodes had happened. He was glad to think that although they might have ended tragically in the case of Mr. Awbery his presence that night, look- ing so well, was a proof that they had had a happier outcome. There was no doubt that those events had cast a certain glamour over the lives of Mr. and Mrs. Awbery and had attracted proper notice to them and to their services which otherwise might have been neglected. His worship then made the presentation and expressed the hope that it would be a constant reminder to the recipients of the high est;ruation in which their long and valuable services under the Poor Law were held. (Applause). Mr. AWBERY, who was received with applause, said the presentation was unique. In a Poor Law experience extending over twenty years, he did not know of one single rase where the master of a Workhouse, retiring from service, had been made the recipient of a present from the tradesmen of the town in which he lived. The presentation demonstrated that the tradesmen, with whom he bad had considerable dealings, had been satisfied with the manner in which they bad been treated and that the business of the Guardians had been carried on not only in a straightforward and honest manner but with the consideration that one should always give to others. He did not. know of any other Board in England that was so well served by its tradesmen as the Warrington Board was At one time the tradesmen had a proper grievance that they frequently had to wait three or four months for the payment of their accounts, and he was glad to say he was successful in inducing the Guardians to make monthly payments. The dealings of the tradesmen during his connection with the Work- house had been of a clean character all through. He could not recall one instance of an attempt to rush into the Viorkhouse goods of an inferior quality or other than those specified in the contract. That was a great deal to say when a matter of between £lO,OOO and £12,000 worth of goods were purchased every year, and it was a striking tribute to the business methods of the tradesmen. He Was gratified that his relations with the tradesmen had won their friendship. There was not ODO of them with whom he had been it variance. One conspicuous line he had kept in front of him in his administration was due and proper consideration for others, and that had proved the best way of avoiding friction. The Warrington Board of Guardians could congratulate themselves that they bad one of the finest systems of contracting and a number of contractors of whom any Union could be justly proud—in fact they could take it from him that the Warrington Union stood pre-eminent in the matter 9f c3ntracts. (Applause). The Mayor was thanked for allowing the use of the Council Chamber, on the motion of Mr WW WESTWELL, seconded by Mr FRANK MILLING. The MAYOR, in responding, said it was sometimea felt that the actions of the members of the Council were not in accordance with the wishes and aspirations of the tradesmen, but they did their best for all sections of the community. Mr. J. A. Rushworth acted as secretary to the presentation committee.</p>
        <p>MR. JAMES WHI,TLEY, who has been elected Chairman of Stockton Heath Parish Council, after 15 yeats' service.</p>
