      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>DRUNKENNESS ON LICENSED PREMISES. "SPREAD EAGLE" LANDLORD FINED. The presiding magistrates at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Messrs Alfred Watkin (chairman), W P Rylands and T J ttidge ay were engaged for nearly three hours in hearing the ca.e of the Police v Sya'ney Georee Collins, landlord of the Spread Eagle Hotel, Lymm,who appeared in answer to three summonses, for permitting drunkenness on licensed premieee, end for selling drink to two drunken persons named Simpson and Baskerville on the 10th March. Mr A Browne of Warrington appeared for the defend- vitt and Superintendent Sutherland conducted the case on behalf of the police. Sergeant Washington stated that at 10.30 p.m. on the 10th March he was en duty in the village when he heard a disturbance rannd by Mill C er in the direction of the Spread Eagle Hotel. On visiting the spot he found the young men, Join Simpson and Richard Baskerville in a drunken condi] icn and stagger- about the road. Baskerville fell down near a chemist, shop door. Witness picked him up and. followed Simpson who W.IS by the Cross with a bicycle. It was with great difficulty that he got Simpson to the Police Station. He again went in searth of Baskerville and found him lying down in the road with some men trying to get him on his feet. Four of them carried him down to the Cross where they met a man with a barrow into which they put Baskerville and brought him to the Police Station. The men were tried the follow- ir g morning for having been drunk and disorderly and were fined lOs each. In answer to Mr. Browne witness said he did not see Mr. Collins that night. He had not found an empty bottle in the possession of either of the ye nag men. Simpson had a cut over his eye. Mr Browne said they did not dispute the evidence but their defence was that the men were not drunk in the Spread Eagle and tbat what happened must have been the result et drinking raw whisky which they purchased before leaving the public house. Sergeant Westall corroborated the evidence of the first witness and John Simpson, labourer, High Legh said that he was in Lymm on the day in question and went into the Spread Eagle about seven o'clock. He bad one drink and went out to return at 8.30 and remained until 10 o'clock. During that time he had four beers and three small whiskies. He got 71d worth of whisky in a bottle as he left. He was about "half and half" when be left the house and could just remember giving the bottle to one of his friends. In answer to Mr Browne witness said he was talking to the barmaid for ten minutes after closing time. He saw Baskerville get on a bicycle and ride away and he called after him. Richard Baskerville, labourer, High Legh, stated that he had one drink in the Spread Eagle at 6 45 and went out and returned about nine o clock and stayed until ten o'clock. He had four or five Mail whiskies. He was in a similar state to Simpson—about "half and half." He did not drink out of the bottle, and did not remember what became of it. He got off his bicycle at Mill Corner to speak to his friends. That was the last be could remember. Herbert Lear, Hon Green, said he was in the Spread 'Eagle, on the night in question from nine o'clock until nearly ten. He saw the two gentlemen served with about five or six small whiskies each. They seemed all right when be left them. They were not noisy. He was surprised when he heard they had been locked up. Arthur Simpson, brother of John, said he was in the Spread Eagle about 8.45 and raw the two men served with two small whiskies and left them in the house about four minutes to ten. They were all right when he left them. There was nothing in their conduct to indicate to Mr Collins or anyone else that anything extraordinary was the matter. Herbert Moss, the Grove, Lymm, said he SSW Simp- son acd Baskerville in Bridgewater-street about 10 15, when they seemed excited over something. He helped to get one of them into the barrow. At this juncture Mr Browne said he submitted that there was nothing whatever proved against the defend- ant. A man might be absolutely helplese at 10.45, but it did not. necessarily mean that Mr. Collies was liable. Sarah Haddon, barmaid of the Spread Eagle, gave evidence to the effect that she bad seen the two men in the house and had served them and had not noticed anything wrong. They went cut of the house, at closing time, all right. She stood at the door talking to one of them for about ten minutes. There was nothing in conduct or speech to indicate that anything was wrong with him. When asked if she could account for the disturbance which had subsequently taken place,witness said she could not because the men were perfectly sober when they left the house. Edward Kerfoot. barman et the hotel, said the day in question was his Sanday night out. He came tack to the Spread Eagle about 9.45 and supplied the two gentle- men with one drink each. After he had finished his work he went for a walk round the village to the Mill Corner. He saw Simpson and Baskerville, and Simpson came and struck him without any evident cause. He bad not quarrelled with him and they had been the best of friends. The men were not in a drunken condition in the house and he had served them with their last drink. When he was at Mill Corner another person had the bottle. Superintendent Sutherland : Did you drink out of the bottle ?—No. You swear that ?—I was going to drink and I had got the bottle to toy mouth, but it was knocked away. Heigh Holt, Lymm, said be was talking to Mr. Collins at the door after closing time on the night in question. He SSW Baskerville light his lamp and ride away eteadily. He saw the bottle of whisky go round and Baskerville drank first. That was when they were talk- ing together and Kerfoot came round. When the fight- ing commenced, Simpson got the worst of it. Baskerville chased Kerfoot round the village, and, to use the words of the witness, "they went like March hares." Peter Brazenda/e, Higher-lace, Lymm, said be raw Baskervillebring his bicecle tbrc ugh about three diffi- cult turnings at the Hotel and there seemed to be nothing wrong whatever. Witness was surprised to hear what had happened afterwards because neither of the two men looked drunk. The defendant was then called. He stated that on the 10th March he went several times into the smoke room where Simpsrn and Baskerville were. They COD- dncted themselves properly. He had not served them with any drink. There was nothing in the conduct of the two at closing time to show that there was any- thing wrong. He had never seen them the worse for drink and he had known them a long time. He heard nothing about the disturbance until the followieg morning when he Was called to the court. Samuel Boond, said be was in the hotel and by the smoke-room door and all the young men left about three minutes to ten. There were no signs of drunkenness about them. Mr Browns said the prosecution charged them with selling drink to a drunken person and permitting drunkenness. A person could not be guilty of permitt ing drunkenness unless there was a slight indication that something was wrong. The witnesses for the prosecution bad supported his case because they bad given evidence which bore out his statements that the men were alright when they left the Spread Eagle. Mr. Collins had had the licence for 23 years without aaingle complaint having been trade against him. He had conducted his house properly on the night in question, and the fact of his repeatedly coming into the room showed that he was desirous that nothing wrong should occur. The young men must have drunk the raw whiskey after leaving the house. He suggested that the case was not proved, and if there was any doubt he asked for the benefit to he given to Mr Collins. The magistrates retired, and on their return the Chair. man said that the law wrs that a licensee should prove that be bad taken reasonable steps to prevent drunken- ness on the premises. They were of the opinion that reasonable Eters had not been taken in that case. In view of the landlord having held the licence for 23 years without a complaint they would deal leniently with him and floe him Zl and costs. DRUNK ON LICENSED PREMISES.—For having been found drunk on the licensed premises of the Bull's Head Hotel on the lOth April.—Richard Holt, Booth's Hill, Lymm, was fined 2s. 6d. RIDING ON FOGTPATll.—Frederick George Scott, 106, Flixton-road, Urmst on, was fined 55. for having ridden a bicycle on the footpath on the 27th March.</p>
        <p>0 - I- A 011 lITS I • _ , LYMM PETTY SESSIONS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Borough Police Court</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Mr J W Hayward (in the chair). Alderman Tinnion eine Messrs R T Fairclough, T Pemberton and A J .7011ey. BICYCLE TllEFT.—Williani Iloardman, of Ashton- nnder-Lyne, was charged on remand with stealing a bicycle, value £B. 8s , the property of Mr T Wallington. The Chief Constable stated that the case had been remanded from the previous Saturday and the particu- lars of the case were that on the 3rd of April the prosecutor left his bicycle outside an office in King-street end on returning about ton minutes later he discovered that the machine had dis- appeared. Later in the dry a telephone message was received from Liverpool to the effect that the prisoner was there with the blot cle. The youth admitted the theft and the Chief Constable proceeding said he appeared to he highly respectable, but had bad some quarrel with his manamar at Ashton and left without a word. The Clerk : What made you take the bicycle ? —1 had no money and I was hungry.—Why did you go to Liverpool ?—I wanted to get on a boat and work my passage abroad —The prisoner was bound over for six months under the Probationers Act. MISTAKEN IDENTITY.—WiIIiam Shelmerdine, 4 Lyttgoes•lane, was charged with assaulting Dorothy Forster, 19, Back Crossley-street, and further charged with assaulting his wife.—Prisoner admitted the former cffence, but denied the latter.—Dorothy Forster said that c n the 10th April the prisoner came op to her and struck her several times. She did not know him, and he had said that he thought she was his wife. Mrs. Shelmerdine said she had not lived with her husband since the lfi,th August. On the 50th ult she was in School-brow when her husband came up</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO THE REV. FATHER DOWLEY ON SUNDAY BY THE ANCIENT ORDER OF HIBERNIANS, D</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO THE REV. FATHER DOWLEY ON SUNDAY BY THE ANCIENT ORDER OF HIBERNIANS, D</p>
        <p>to her and used threatening language. He got hold of her by the hair and struck her en the forehead. There were nine previous convictions against Sheirnerdine and he was sent to prison for tbree months, end the wife was granted a separation order with en allowance of 8s per week. THEFT OF COAL,—Jarnee Carbill, 96, Sac 01-brow,l and Thomas %Visitley (16), 90, Tanners-lane, were charged with stealing 3cwt. of cod, va'ue 4s Gd, the, property of Mecsrs Wand J R Richardson.—The Chief Constable stated that at 515 that morning two officers were on duty in Tarners-lane when they saw the youth wheeling a truck with coal in it to No. 40, Tanner's. lane, He admitted it bad been tsken from Richardson's, and also stated that he had been with the elder yrisoner. —Carhill said he had no fire it his house.—Mr Burdett, of Messrs Richardson's, said both prisoners were in the employ of the firm. He thought the youth bad been led away by the elder prisoner.—Carhill was fined lOs and Whitley Wf s discharged. MosneY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst and J Evans, and Messrs %V Roberts, T Locker trod S Wright. MORE COAL THElTS.—Bridget Dale, Cloth Mall Yard, Margaret Collins, Union-street, and Priscilla Tynan, Union-street, were charged with stealing coal to the value of 4s 6d, the property of the London and North Western Railway Company, on Saturday, the 13th inst., from Arpley Sidings.—Detective Clarke stated that at 4.20 OD the day in question be was in company with Detective Goode, D hen they saw the three prisoners coming from the direction of the St. Helens Cable works across the lines. The prisoner Daley got on the buffers of a waggon and threw the coal out to the other two eho were holding a bag below.—Detective Goode gave corroborative evidence and stated that the company had, during the strike, allowed persona go on the tips but now that the waggons were coming tbrouch they bad discontinued the practice. The plea of the prisoners was that they had no fires and the magistrates wishing to be as lenient as possible, sentenced the prisoners to one dAy's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY—Before Councillon Roberts (in the chair), and Jackson Holmes, and l'iffs=rs J Rymer Young, W Richardson J Hepherd and Colonel Edelesten.</p>
        <p>- Two MEN AND A DOG.—James Smith, 87, Plumpton- street, was summoned for keeping a dog without a license.—Coief Inspector Williamson stated that the defendant purchased the dog from another man who had found it and had not reported the matter. Defendant had no license. He pleaded guilty and said he bought the dog on the day before Good Friday. On the Friday he went away and returned on the Saturday and thedog had disappeared—The case was dismissed and Smith was further summoned for assaulting William Allcock, of 10, Ashton-street on the 10th inst.—Defen- dant pleaded guilty and complainant said he bought the dog in Chester. The dog had left his home and returned three weeks later and on the day in question he (com- plainant) was hawking in Sankey Bridges with the dog when defendant came up to him and dealt him several blows rendering him nnconscioua,end took the dog from him. Defendant was fined lOs including costs.</p>
        <p>THIMSDAV.—Before Mr Rymer Young (in the ebnir), Messrs W Roberto, A J Jolley, W T Williams and Dr Naden.</p>
        <p>_ COACH BUILDER FINED.—J H Heywood, Wilderspool Crossing, Warrington. coach builder, was summoned by Mr W J MoCaghey, ELM. Inspector of Factories, for employing two young persons without having them examined and err:lied as physically fit by the Certify- ing Surgeon for the district. Mr McCagbey stated that defendant had employed two lads under the age of 16. One bad been employed since June and the other since February 1911, and neither of them had been certified. Defendant, who said one of the lads bad only been employed from September and not from February, was ordered to pay £1 in each °age.</p>
        <p>were insufficient boats to save all the people assembled there, he called upon them to get theit instruments. This they did, and as Miss Bonnell was being•rowed away in one of the boats she and her companions could bear them playing" Nearer, my God, to Thee." The Titanic appeared to have been broken in twain rather than telescored by the force of the Miss Bonnell said that her boat tws continually graz- I ing ice floes during the four and a half bouts that elarsed before they were picked up by the Carpatlaia. The men were splendid," she said, "and heroism is no word for the behaviour of the second and third class passengers. Some of the fist-class passengers, hoe ever, fought like madmen for places in the boats, and I heard that the officer in charge of one of the boats had to fire at some of them."</p>
        <p>Several passengers agree as-te.the splendid heroism with which Mr. and Mrs. laidor Straus met their end. The aged millionaire and his wife walked up and down the deck arm-in-arm, talking calmly, and endeavouring to comfort one another. Several times appeals were made to Mr. Straus to persuade his wife to get into the boats, but the brave old lady flatly declined to leave her husband, and declared that, as death was inevitable, it was better that the younger women should be saved. CAPTAIN SMITH'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>There is considerable uncertainty as to how Captain Smith met his death, there being one rumour that he had committed su'cide, and another that he jumped. into the sea just as the liner sunk and was drawn down in the vertex,</p>
        <p>Colonel Astor personally placed his wife, Ntbo was unconscious, into a boat, and then rushed back for more women. Another millionaire, of whom the survivors speak in plowing terms, was Mr. Howard Case, who pushed off the last boat, thus deliberately cutting tff his own chance of escape.</p>
        <p>VISION 422, WARRINGTON.</p>
