      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO THE REV. FATHER HOWLEY.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO THE REV. FATHER HOWLEY.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening a presentation from the Ancient Order of Hibernians in Warrington was given to the Rev. Father Dowley, late chaplain of the society, before his return to Ireland. The presentation, which was made at the Sacred Heart school, took the form of a beautiful sash of the Order together with a solid gold cress of Celtic design with suitable inscription. Bro D Currie, President District 422, made the presenta- tion, and asscciated v ith him were Bros E Kelly, treasurer; M Handley, VP; E L Riley and T Griffin, secretaries ; Councillor Toole, Dr Liston, J Riley, William H McNevin, and a very full gathering of members. On this same occasion Bro Currie had the unique privilege of enrolling three priests at once as honorary members of the A.O.H.—Fathers Howley, Rockliff and Murphy being invested with the sash of the Order.—Bro. Currie made reference to their chaplain's good work in connection with the Order, and to their great loss in his recall to Ireland.—Father Rockliff spoke of the value of such a Catholic Friendly Society in their midst, aid promised it his hearty support. He also expressed his willingness to allow Father Murphy to be nominated as chaplain in succession to Father Dovrley, and expressed the hope that in Father Murphy they would find an equally zealous priest and warm- hearted friend as the one who was about to leave them.—Councillor Toole and Dr. Liston also spoke in reference to the work of Father Dowley In Irish circles in Warrington. Father DOWLEY returned thanks for the gifts. He severed his connection with the Order in Warrington, he said, with regret that would be a hundred times deeper were he not returning to the old land. Their gifts he would treasure to the last, and the remem- brance of his happy connection with the. A.O.H. in Warrington would ever urge him to greater efforts on behalf of the noble objects of the society. He pre- dicted for the District Branch of Warrington a great and growing meccas under the presidency of Bro. Currie and the chaplaincy of his successor at the Sacred Heatt Church. The members were afterwards photograped in the school yard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>"GLAD HE WAS'NT A</head>
        <p>"GLAD HE WAS'NT A</p>
        <p>WOMAN."</p>
        <p>The late Lawson Tait, the gnat woman's doctor, said in his memoirs that be never rose up in the morning and never went to bed at night, without thanking heaven that he was not born a woman. No doctor ever knew better than Lawson Tait what practically every woman has to suffer at some time of her life. Women tell these things to each other ; and one woman, Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkharn, found ease for herself in a herbal mixture which she used to prepare over the kitchen firs and give to other women who suffered. In time, the fame of Mrs. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound, and of the marvellous way in which it gave ease to suffering wcmen, became so greatly spread abroad that she was persuaded to give the whole public the benefit of it. Mrs, Lydia E. Pink- ham's Vegetable Compound can now be br ught from any chemist, price 2s. 9d. (or very large bottle 4s. Gd.) and the relief which it gives to women of all ages can only be known to those who have experienced its effects. Let every woman vc ho reads this go to the chemist and buy from him, or write to The Lady Manager, c/o Lydia E. Pinkbam Medicine Co.' 42a, Hampstead Rd, London N.W. for a ho tie of Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound and she will bless the day when this paper came into her bands. A copy of Mrs. Pinkham's private book, for women only, may be had free of all charge by writing to her representatives at the same address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CAPTAIN'S WIDOW.</head>
        <p>THE CAPTAIN'S WIDOW.</p>
        <p>The following message from Mrs Eleanor Smith, widow of Captain Smith, of the Titanic, was posted at Southampton on Thursday afternoon :—" To my poor fellow-sufferers. My heart overflows with grief for you all, and is laden with the sorrow that has been thrust upon us. May God be with us all ! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ICEBERG SEEN TOO LATE.</head>
        <p>THE ICEBERG SEEN TOO LATE.</p>
        <p>A Committee of surviving passengers has published a statement in which they say: On Sunday, April 14th, 1912, at about 11.40 on a cold, starlit night, the ship struck an iceberg which had been repotted.to the bridge by the lookout, but not early enough to avoid the collision. " We feel it our duty to call tl e attention of the public to what we consider the inadequate appliances provided for modern passenger steamships, and recommend that immediate steps be taken to compel passenger steamers to carry suffisient boats to accommodate the maximum number of people carried on board."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WRECK OF THE TITANIC.</head>
        <p>THE WRECK OF THE TITANIC.</p>
        <p>1,635 LIVES LOST WITH THE LARGEST LINER. AN APPALLING CATASTROPHE.'</p>
        <p>The White Star liner Titanic, the largest ship yet launched, which left Southampton on Wednesday last week for New York on her maiden voyage, collided late on Sunday night with an iceberg off the Grand Banks of Newfoundland. She at once sent out signals of distress by wireless telegraphy. The Virginan (from which the first news of the disaster reached land) and other liners responded to the call. There was much anxiety on Monday on both sides of the Atlantic. The worst fears as to the dimensions of the chatacter of the tragedy were confirmed on Wednesday after many misleading messages had been received. The Cunard liner Carpathia was then steaming to Now York with the only known survivors, whose captain, in a wireless message to his com- pany, says:</p>
        <p>. I am proceeding to New York unless otherwise ordered. After having consulted Mr. Bruce Ismay, considering the circumstances and with so much ice about, I considered New York best. There is a large number of icebergs and 20 miles of field ice with bergs amongst it.</p>
        <p>Titanic survivors on board the Carpathia 705 Total passengers and crew 2,340 Missing 1,635 These are the latest figures of the disaster to the Titanic. The only other authentic message from the scene of the disaster is an earlier one from the Carpathia's captain, saying that he reached the Titanic's position at day-break, and found boats and wreckage only. The Titanic had foundered about 2.20 a.m., and 705 souls of her crew and passengers were saved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>SURVIVORS.</head>
        <p>THE SURVIVORS.</p>
        <p>Harrowing Details of the Disaster_</p>
        <p>The Carpathia, with her load 705 striken and afflicted survivors reached New York, on Thursday evening, and was met at the docks L - a crowd of at least 500 people all in mourning. Several survivors have already been interviewed, and describe the awful occurrence of Sunday evening in the most moving language. The Titanic appears to have struck the iceberg between 11 o'clock and 11.30 oclock on Sunday night, and to have sunk at 2 o'clock. A scene of the wildest confusion followed the impact, for the tons of ice which fell on the scattered decks killed a number of the crew instantly. The stern of the vessel rose in the air, and the passengers ruthed from below panic stricken and made for the boats. The crew, or such of them as were on deck at the time of the collision, behaved with admirable courage and discipline, and tore women and children from the arms or their relatives and bundled them into the boats. The boats were lowered as fast as they were filled, , and many frantic passengers flung themselves into the sea rather than run the risk of being crowded out of the remaining boats. "THE COLD FRIGHTFUL." The majority of the survivors,who were in a fit state to be questioned, maintained that the Titanic was travelling at a tremendous speed at the time of the crash, a Mr Charles Stengel estimating that her speed was 22 knots.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Andrews, an old lady who was still in a state of extreme exhaustion, says : "We got off in the life- boats about midnight, arid were cruising round for over six hours. The cold was frightful, and very few of us had much clothing on. Many of the women lin my bcat were raving, and we had given up all hope when the Carpathia picked us up." Dr. Frauenthal declared that the shrieks of the women who bad been torn from their • husbands were heartret,ding, and many of them tried to throw them- selves into the sea to swim back to the sinking liner. Some of the boats got away only half mantled, the sailors not daring to wait in case they were over- crowded, and these boats were only too glad to pick up some of the men who jumped overboard. One of the few connected stoties chtainable was that of Miss Bonnell, of Youngstown, Ohio, who described an impressive scene which took place on the stern of the liner, where the passengers were marched to be put into the boats.</p>
        <p>"NEARER, MY GOD, TO THEE." The Titanic's bandmaster had marched his bands- men to that part of the ship, and finding that there</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>MR. BRUCE ISMAVS ESCAPE.</head>
        <p>MR. BRUCE ISMAVS ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>Speaking with great emotion, Mr. Bruce Ismay, on arrival at New York on the Carpathia, said: "The Titanic, after striking the iceberg on Sunday night, remained ab.tve the water for two hours and twenty-five minutes. She then sank. Whether there were enough lifeboats on the Titanic to care for all her passengers I do not care to say." Mr. Ismay was asked in which lifeboat, in the order of their leaving the Titanic, he made his escape. Mr. Ismay : I took the last lifeboat which pulled out from the Titanic.</p>
        <p>Were there any women or children on board when you left the Titanic ? Mr. Inlay : lam sure I cannot say. DiscuFsing the nature of the Titanic's injuty, Mr. Isrnay said the whole bilge as ripped cut where the iceberg caught her. She struck the berg a glancirg blow on the starboard side. There was no explosion. He was in bed asleep, and was awakened by the shcck. It felt as if she were going up on something. • The vessel seemed to climb upon a submerged part of the iceberg.</p>
        <p>SEARCHING VESSELS RETURN. The hope of other survivors having been , picked up by the Allan liners Virginian or Parisian has had to be given up. Both vessels report that they have no passengers on board, and are proceeding on their voyage This ill-omened news came both by wireless messages to stations on the American Continent and in the following official cable received on Tuesday night at the Allan Line offices in London:</p>
        <p>Virginian advices too late to reach Titanic. She is proceeding on her voyage. MESSAGE FROM THE KING AND QUEEN. The King sent this message on Tuesday night to the White Star Company : The Queen and I are horrified at the appalling disaster which has happened to the Titanic and at the terrible loss of life. We deeply sympathise with the bereaved</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>TITANIC'S CAPTAIN.</head>
        <p>THE TITANIC'S CAPTAIN.</p>
        <p>LOCAL ASSOCIATIONS.</p>
        <p>The tragedy of the Titanic has a sad association with this district, for the wire of Captain Smith is a native of W inwick, and her family have been in the occupation of Wo dhead Farm for four generations. Captain Smith roartied Miss Pennington, the second daughter of Mr. William Pennington of Winwick, about twenty-five years ago, the ceremony being per- formed at Winwicic Church by the Rev. J. Carson, who is now Rector of Wargrave, Eatlestown. Captain Smith's relatives are well known not only in the dis- trict but in Lancashire agricultural circles . . . .</p>
        <p>It is only about six weas ago that Captain and Mrs Smith celebrated their silver wedding. There is one daughter of the marriage—a child of about 13. A sister of Mrs. Smith, Mrs. Johnson, lives at Winwiek, and Mr Orford Johnson, of Warrington, is a nephew of Captain Smith. \ Captain Smith was at one time a resident of Great Sankey, and for about five years be made his home at tha Poplars, in'that parish. It is nearly ten years ago that the unlucky ship-master transferred his resi- dence to Waterloo. Ile is still remembered by a large number d the inhabitants of Great Sankey. Elk quiet sincerity and his geniality delighted all with whom he came in contact, and made him highly popular with passengers on the North Atlantic route. Whilst resident at Great Sankey, Captain Smith was in command of the Sfajestic. He has had charge of seventeen White Star liners in succession.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>"WENT DOWN WITH BIS SHIP,"</head>
        <p>"WENT DOWN WITH BIS SHIP,"</p>
        <p>"Although I have no official information on the point," writes.a representative, "I am in a position to state that this was to have been Captain Smith's last voyage. As his name has not so far appeared in the list of survivors, it is feared that he is among the drowned ; in fact, those who know him best in Liver- pool are confident that he'went down with his ship." "Although he had passed his sixtieth year," remarked one shipping expert who kcew him, "he was as fine a spscimen of the Atlantic commander as our merchant service could show."</p>
        <p>It is an unhappy coincidence that Captain Smith was in commarid of the Olympic last September, on the occasion of her collision in the Solent with the cruiser Hawke. At the time of the collision, the Olympic was in charge of a duly licensed ninity House pilot. Captain Smith was acquitted from any blame for the collision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>LEVIATHAN DANGERS.</head>
        <p>LEVIATHAN DANGERS.</p>
        <p>THEORIES THAT MUST UNDERGO A CHANGE.</p>
        <p>In shipping circles it is admitted that the tragedy of the Titanic would seriously affect Transatlantic passenger traffic for some time, and especially such principal lines as attract the travelling public with welbadtertised monster vessels. But in the end," said the manager of a shipping company "the superior appointments, speed, comfort, and safety to be Lund only in the largest craft, will tell again. "The public have been allowed to suppose that these big vessels are unsinkable. It is quits clear, on the reports from New York, that important shipping folk, who should have known better, have said so often that modern engineering science has made these boats unsinkable that they have come to believe it themselves. They have actually accepted it as a fact, and built on it as a fact. But the unsinkable ship has not been devised.</p>
        <p>" Hurricanes she takes little notice of. Collisions which would be grate in smaller craft are but minor affairs with her. But when she is struck vitally, unless plenty of help is near at hand, it is a certainty that many of her company will rerish. "I doubt very much, too, whether any ship has enough boats to accommodate 3,000 people. BIGGER SHIPS BEING BUILT.</p>
        <p>Of the four largest vessels in the world only one, the Lusitania, has escaped serious accident. The other thiee, Mauretania, Olympic, and Titanic, have each bad sensational experiences. The overwhelming fact is that these huge vessels cannot be controlled in narrow waters, writes a Liverpool correspondent. They may be safe enough at sea, apart horn such accidents as could befall any vessel, but within narrow waters they are helpless. No anchor will hold them, and they are at the mercy of the-tide and wind. Big ships are still being built. The Cunard have the Anuetania on the stocks—a 50,000 ton vessel. The Hamburg-American line will launch next month the Imperator, of about the same tonnage. The White Star has in hand the Gigantic a 44,000 ton ship, tho keel of which is now being laid in Belfast. Shipping men have said that there is nothing to prevent a vessel of 100,000 tons, but they reckon without the harbour and dock facilities, which will not be increased indefinitely except at the expense of the shipowner. The suggestion that these vessels should carry great headlights does not find much support.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>PARADISE</head>
        <p>THE PARADISE</p>
        <p>OF THE</p>
      </div>
