      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April, 20, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April, 20, 1912.</p>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR Hill READERS.</p>
        <p>THE THIRD HOME RULE BILL.</p>
        <p>For the third time in six and twenty years the Imperial Parliament has now before it a Home Rule Bill, having for its object "the better Government of Ireland." Mr. Asquith's Bill we do not hesitate to describe as being at once the greatest, the most generous and the most acceptable of the three. Since the memorable day in 1886 when Mr. Gladstone raised the question in the House of Commons, a huge advance has been made on this side of the Irish Sea in understanding and in sympathy with regard to the Irish national demand and this 'advance has made it posssible for Mr. Asquith and his colleagues to go beyond the limit of the proposals of the Gladstonian Cabinets Even in the comparatively small matter of titles, the present Bill is nearer to the aspira- tions of the Irish people than its predecessors. With all his intense affection for the Irish people, Mr. Gladstone, full as he was of a hyper-sensitive feeling about words, always designated the new government of Ireland as a "legislature." In boldly naming the new body for Ireland as an Irish " Parliament " and in the description of the proposed second chamber as an Irish "House of Commons,"the present Prime Minister has shown clearly that he proposed to establish not a sham but a real Parliament which would revive the glories of Ireland's native Parliament, thus paying tribute to a tradition which any man who is not barbarian in his want of historic sense must surely admire. At any rate the Irish people respond to that feature of the measure with no less joy than their representatives evinced when Mr. Asquith in passionate tones told the supercilious Leader of the Opposition that the references to the nation in the Premier's speech meant the Irish nation. Titles, however, are but titles. What of the facts? The facts of the Bill more than support the approval we have just expressed. In the Bills of 1886 and 1893, Mr. Gladstone enumerated certain defined subjects on which the Irish Parliament might legislate. Out- side that area the Imperial Parliament was to be the legislating body. Now the exact converse of that plan is to be adopted under the present Bill. A certain number of sub- jects—such as succession to the throne, the Army and the Navy, the powers with regard to treaty-making, all relations with foreign powers—are to be placed within the sphere of the Imperial Parlia- ment and to that every Irishman will most readily assent, recognising as he does that they are the proper theme for the con- sideration of the Westminster Imperial legis- lature rather than the Dublin purely Irish Parliament. But everything over and above these reserved points is to come within the purview and the functions of the Irish Parliament. What a great advance is repre- sented by that second plan will be clear on even the most cursory examination. For it means that on all essentially Irish affairs, on all the matters, economic, social, and intellectual which go to make up the warp and woof of a nation's life and development, the Parliament in Dublin will have full control. The Judicial Bench for example will come straight away under the jurisdiction of the Irish Executive. In six years, shorter by more than half, than the periods previously fixed,the Irish Constabulary will be under the direction of Irish powers, freed entirely from the exacting if not impossible conditions imposed by Mr. Gladstone 's proposals, while the Dublin Metropolitan Police Force will immediately come under Irish control. In finance also an equal advance is seen. Under the Bill of 1886, it will be recalled, Ireland was to pay,after discharging the costs of ber own Government, a sum of three and a quarter millions sterling into the Imperial Exchequer, and this contribution to the Imperial fund was reduced to two and a quarter millions in the Bill of 1893. As Parnell insisted at the time, and subsequent history has vindicated Parnell to the refuta- tion of Gladstone on the finance sections, with such a Budget Ireland could only proceed to insolvency. Mr. Asquith now proposes that the Imperial Parliament should find the million and a half which wasteful ' and unrepresentative Government, otherwise Unionism,has put on the debit sideof the Irish account and in addition to clearing the deficit, it is proposed to make Ireland a grant of half a million a year for the present as a start in the heavy task of administering affairs in a country where misgovernment and profligate expenditure has had so long a spell. This proposal, whilst relieving Ireland, will be an even greater gain to England as the years pass, since the present method meaning a continuance of doles must entail an ever increasing tax on the Imperial Exchequer. In Customs and Excise too, enormous powers will be conferred on the Dublin Parliament and the Government may be congratulated on having designed so excellent a way of dealing with this admittedly thorny portion of the subject. The nominated Senate may not on prima facie considerations appeal to the democratic mind, but since it has worked with excellent results in other parts of the Empire, we Irish are prepared to accept such a proposal. On the point of safeguards we feel that the Government have been over-influenced by the Protestant unworthy fear. The whole world fails to produce a finer record for religious toleration than that shown by Catholic Ireland, and it is therefore regrettable, we think, to find more safeguards put into this Bill than are to be found in any Constitution of the Empire from the minor one of the Isle of Man to the great Common- wealth of Australia. Still we feel that this Bill will put Ireland in the position her people have most earnestly prayed and worked for—in a position to foster and develop all the resources of her national life and to take her rightful place among the nations of the earth. With her proper recognition in the Council of the Empire will come, we believe, the burying in blessed oblivion of the feuds of the past, and a better understanding between two peoples who in God's providence were surely meant to dwell together in peace and unity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>POSTAL SORTING CENTRE. NEW OFFICES AT CREWE.</head>
        <p>POSTAL SORTING CENTRE. NEW OFFICES AT CREWE.</p>
        <p>A new postal sorting office at Crewe, which has been built adjoining the railway station at a coat of £20,000, was opened on Monday by Mrs F E Adams, Shrewsbury, wife of the Chief Surveyor of Post Offices for the North Wales district. A letter was read from the Postmaster-General congratulating the town on haying such a well-equipped office.—The building is fitted up to meet the requirements of the Departmental Com- mittee which inquired recently into the methods of postal sorting. It is the first of its particular type in the country, and many new appliances are being tried experimentally. Every day upwards of 600 trains dis- charge mails at Crewe' where they are sorted and trans- ferred to all parts of the kingdom. Over a quarter of a million letters are dealt with daily, and 5,000 hampers and mailbags are transferred. The staff to cope with this traffic has been increased tenfold during the last few years. The offices are situated on the Marquis of Crewe's land, and every platform on the station is con- nected with the new offices by subterranean passages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST,</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST,</p>
        <p>GAME OF BRAG.</p>
        <p>Said one of two children whom I overheard in con- vergation : "My muvver's clever'n yours • she made this frock all by herself—yah " " " was the retort, "mine can take her tceE out an' put 'em in again, so there ! " It reminded me of the youngster who said to a schoolfellow: " We're having some carving ca cur new house," and was promptly met with, " Pcoh ! That's nothing. We're havirg mortgage on curs." And that in turn recalls the one of the boy who boasted to an acquaintance that one of his father's hens had that morning laid an enormous egg. " Ah, but," said the other "my father once laid a foundation stone." Easily and naturally one passes from this to the subject of THE BATHS EXTENSION,</p>
        <p>The stone-laying ceremony in connection ta ith which was " done ;n a corner," as it were, being for some rnysterinus and unexplained reason arranged under circumstances such as are supposed to attend the hatching of State conspiracies. Perhaps the promoters were a little dubious as to the necessity for the work and feared hostile demonstration. I write from memory, being unable at the moment to lay my hand on the reference, but I think it was Sir Almoth Wright who a while ego denounced the bath as not only superfluoua, but as positively enervating and mischievous. I discussed the subject with a man whom I came across accident- ally, and he assured me that, although his home was furnished with a bath, none of the family ever used it —only as a receptacle for lumber. I eyed him over as he thus spake, and the scrutiny confirmed the personal testimony. This fnnniosity and the physician above mentioned, moreover, are not alone in their convictions as to the needlessness and even harmful- ness of constant washing. Besides, if we are to believe the historians, the personal cleanliness to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>leaves from Germany's book, and we may well look in that direction for further hints. The English working- men who visited Germany sometime ago were impressed by the fact that there are no strolling beggars in that country: Germany expects men to .iork for their living if they can, and if they won't do so voluntarily she compels them. We shall base to iollow that example, I imagine. I write thus with a tinge of regret, for I have a sneakirg regard for the knight of the road, and am rather given to hobnobbing with him. In that way I have gathered material which wculd fill cclunins of ordinary newsparer space, and intend to publish it one of these days.</p>
        <p>A SAD ARMY. The latest volume of statistics published by the Poor Law Commissioners is an eye opener for the many who had somehw imbibed the notion that the number of paup, ra in England and Wales was considerably under a milli„n. The figures show that cur pauper army consists of about double that number; Ir, to be pacise, 1,709,436, instead of 793,519, as had leen pretty generally supposed. The facts are appalling, and the name of counsellors and remedies is legion. Many of these have abundant self- confidence, but cccasionally one is disposed to take a gloomy view. A nriter of the latter kind insists that no mere change of administration will work miracles ; there is no automatic method of ridding us from the cut se of pauperism. Pauperism arises from human frailty, and the seed will never die out, says this pescimist, and he proceeds—one of the great difficul- ties (4 all cffeetive reform is that, if changes are sudden and sweeping, intense suffering may be caused, while if they are gradual, the zealous persons who I supply the necessary fund of ener;ry are likely to be alienated, a lazy conservative spirit re-asserts itself,, and the whole benefit of the movement in lost. What we want, in the world of social endeavour, in men and women with more staying power, endowed with "purpose unwedded to plans," content to move slowly if needs must, provided that all movement is in the right direction.</p>
        <p>A GLANCE AT ELI3ERFELD. After that it is vt ith decided relief that one turns to the case of Etberfeld. A Burnley journalist with</p>
