      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>EAST NOTTS BY-ELECTION.</head>
        <p>THE EAST NOTTS BY-ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The by-election for the East Nottingham seat has been sharp and short, as the candi- dates had only a week in which to work the constituency. As this is the first by-election since the introduction of the Home Rule</p>
        <p>which many among us are nowadays supposed to be strongly addicted is quite a modern fad. Not very long ago I read in the' British Medical Jcurnal " that in the year 1800 there was not a single private house in London provided with a bath-loom. Perhaps I ought to cxplain that this appeared, n( t as an editorial, but as a communication from a correspon- dent, and the writer mentiored that '' a witness stated before the Hcalth Commission about the middle of last century that the only two occasions on which one of the labouring classes was washed all over was imme- diately after birth and after death." I have previously given in this column a striking instance of the kind that came under my own observation, but I did so in ihe presumption that it was not exceptional. OUR DIRTY FOREFATHERS.</p>
        <p>Exceptional, quotha ! The medical organ already quotect professes to doubt if even at the present day dirtier people can be found in the world than among our humbler classes. The libeller goes on to declaie that a French wcrkman would be ashamed to wear the clothes in which the British labourer goes to his dail3 work. There is ncehing that trarrps who app'y at Workhouses think a more brutal tyranny than the enforced bath. After all, those who apparently have conscientious scruples about washing their bodias are merely a trifle behind the age. What I mean is that they occupy a similar position to that habitually taken by members of the " hupper seckles " at a not very distant period. My principal au hority states that three centuries ago what most struck a Portuguese traveller in England was the fact that the English gentry never washed. Erasmus has left a picture of the dirt that prevailed io the houses of noblemen. Another correspondent wonders what the labourers of Queen Anne's reign mast have been like when Mr. McCarthy can speak of its royalty thus : " A royal prince or princess of that time would have been set down as a personage of very dirty habits by a well-brought-up working man in the reign of Victoria." We have read how, on the I whole, the medival saints seemed to exalt uncleanli- ness into a virtue. Here (once more from "the 8.M.J.") is a plausible explanation : In Imperial Rome, as luxury grew, baths became to an increasing degree houses of assignation, and the most frightful immorality prevailed, as may be gathered from the Roman satirists. It was for this reason that.the baths were denounced by some of the early Christian fathers. Later, as usually happens, their teaching was mis- understood and misapplied, and it was thought that the act of bathing the body in itself tended to immorality. SHAKESPEARE COMMEMORATION.</p>
        <p>I am glad that next week's amateur performances at the Royal Court Theatre are to include the first of the master's great tragedies " Julius Cresar." I once followed Mr. Fotheringham through a series of lectures in which this stood eighth, and be observed that it had a more historic cast than the plays that followed it, and though certainly tragic in conception, build, and issue, it was less caught in the strain and stress of tragic issues than the plays that came after it. The style, too, bad the strength, balance and adequacy of " Henry V." and "Twelfth Night," a all more passionate notes in it at the same time. The tragic time was their dramatist's richest, strongest, and fullest time. Shakespeare was great in history, and be was great in comedy ; he was greater still, greatest of all it might be, in tragedy. He put himself more fully, more passionately, and with richer interest and expression into that group of his works than into any other group. THE GENUS TRAMP. " Awbery, Awbery," I heard a woman call from a back wreet, the other night. I stood and peered into the darkness, puzzled and half expecting to find the genial tx-master of the Warrington Workhouse emerging therefrom. That, of course, was but on the spur of the moment, a second's thought sufficed to convince cue of the absurdity of it. Still, I hesitated and considered, and as I lingered a cat darted by with the irate female in hot but vain pursuit. On seeing me, she stopped and said apologetically-.—" It's a pet is gowd, an' I'm determined it shall not be out ov a neet. Oh, thar't theer, Awbery gem inside wi' thee." And the pair, lackin only a broomstick, disappeared together. " Awbery ' what? None of your common or garden Toms or Tabbies or Topsies, eh? In acknowledging the gifts as reported in last week's ' paper, Mr. J. F. Awbery made some characteristic remarks respecting vagrancy. He has all along recog- nised that tramping is not necessarily a sign of dis- honesty—tbat not all who drift to the casual ward are cadgers,wastrels,won't works;and has consistently and persistently urged that thcy should be treated sensibly and with discrimination, and not as if it were the fact that "the poor in the loomp is bad." The problem is to discover how to separate the sheep from the goats, the honest men wandering about in search of work, from the professional tramps, or, as it is sometimes exprr seed, the unemployed and the unemployable, and all who have thought the matter, out as Mr. A wbety has done will surely agree that we shall have, if possible, to remove those conditions which so easily turn the unfortunate honest wayfarer into the don't-care tramp. In various departments of late, we have not disdained to take a few</p>
        <p>Bill, the contest is arousing great interest. Mr. Dobson, shown on the left, formerly M.P. for Plymotth, is the Liberal candidate, and his opponent is Sir John Rees.</p>
        <p>whom I worked many yesrs ag) cslls my attention to the following instructive facts : In Elberfeld in 1852 the cost of pauperism, iccluding rates and charities, amounted to 47s per bead of the population, one in twelve of the population were paupers. In five years rates had become trifling, street begging had disappeared, and paupers numbered only ore in thirty eight of an increased population. In 1008 Elberfeld had no submerged tenth, no slums in our sense of the word, the rates had been reduced to a minimum, and charities were little required. In Barmen, which may almost be spoken of as the Salford to Elberfeld, being its twin-city, the system was not adopted for some yews The contrast, however, was so marked that Barmen followed the example cf its near neighbour, with excellent results.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>.e".",e‘,..-- ,_ _ _ ."./1 iCHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>.e".",e‘,..-- ,_ _ _ ."./1 iCHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>JUDGES WERE ONCE BOYS.</p>
        <p>Here is -a-- true story told by John McDonald: "The headmaster of a village school in Kansas one afternoon detected a r boy cutting the letters of his name in the desk in front of him. He angrily put forth his hand, intending to grasp the boy by the collar, when lo and also behold close by the newly formed letters were the initials of his own name written by himself when he was a pupil in the same school. "His grasp upon the boy's collar unloosened itself, and he returned to his desk a sadder and a wiser teacher. That principal is to-day judge of an important court in one of the greatest cities in the world." We often wonder wheher or not in the administration of justice the judge ever thinks of the incident in the village school l" THE POSTMAN'S STRIPES.</p>
        <p>What do the stripes mean on a postman's arm? Perhaps you have wondered. Each stripe represents five years service, so that the wearer who has four has put in twenty years of service. This is the rule so far as the older generation of postmen is concerned; but within the last few years a slight altera- tion has been made in the awarding of stripes to recruits. When a man has seen five years' service he is given his first stripe, and thereafter one every four years instead of five. _ _ _</p>
        <p>It must be clearly understood, however, that these stripes are withheld (and, indeed, withdrawn) if there is any marked slackness of duty, such as drunkenness, continual late attendance, etc. Either course is naturally very painful to the postal authorities, and comparatively speaking, it is seldom necessary. HOW ABOUT YOU? It matters little, dear young man, where your grand- sires were born. Or if your great-greatgrandfather read law or planted corn ; Nor does it matter much to-day what your grand- mother knew, But what the world desires to know is—What is there in you? Your father's uncle may, perhaps, have 'need] Napoleon. For deeds of valour in the field enduring honours 14013 Some of ycur mother's ancestors may have pierced Cresar through, But what the world asks now, young man, is—How much can you do Your mother's uncle may have been a soldier brave and great, Have made some great discovery or colonised a state, Or with the thousands that he made some college have endowed, But what, young man, have you e'er done of which the world feels proud? There is no harm for you, young man, your lineage to trace</p>
        <p>Back to some mighty giant mind, whose deeds have blessed the race, But let me whisper this to you in a soft undertone, If you a laurel wreath would wear, weave for yourself your own!</p>
        <p>27tomas F. Porter.</p>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S STis"fii-Y. Janet was only five years old and couldn't be expected to know much about entomology. i She was n the garden and saw a tiny cater- pillar. "0 come here " she cried "here's a caterpillar, the dearest little thing, oh so tiny I I believe it must be a kitten-pillar.'.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>GREAT INSURANCE</head>
        <p>THE GREAT INSURANCE</p>
        <p>ACT.</p>
        <p>THE DEPOSIT CONTRIB UTOIL</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR SHERWELL, M.P.</p>
        <p>No single set of provisions in the National Insurance Act has been more adversely and —it must be added—more ungenerously criti- cised than the provisions relating to deposit contributors. Instead of being considered and welcomed as an earnest, but avowedly tenta- tive, attempt to extend, to the utmost practic- able limits, the benefits of a State scheme of insurance to a minority of the nation who are not strictly an insurable class, Sections 42 and 43 of the Act have been frcely quoted as evidence of unjust discrimination in the dis- tribution of State help, and as inflicting posi- tive hardship upon a large section of the community.</p>
        <p>Such. suggestions are not merely tua• generous; they wholly misrepresent the facts. It is certainly the case that deposit contri- butors will not share in the benefits of the Insurance scheme in the same ways, and to the same extent, that other insured persons who are members of approved societies will do; but this is due, not to any inequality or inherent fault in the principles of the Insur- ance scheme, but to unhappy social and economic facts which are not within the de- termination or control of the framers of the Act. The Act itself is founded upon a prin- ciple of strict impartiality. It does not arbi- trarily divide the community into grades or classes. It throws the full benefits of the acheme open to all who become members of approved societies; and it goes further by extending such help as it possible to those who, from physical or other causes, are out- side the scope of insurance. The deposit-contributor class are not made uninsurable by Mr. Lloyd George's Act. The Act finds them an uninsurable class, alien from all effective help and succour, and brings to them substantial but provisional benefits which are to issue in more effectual benefits in three years' time, when we have knowledge and experience to guide Uff. The Insurance Act, in short, is the first import- ant instalment of succour and help that thii unfortunate class has received.</p>
        <p>It has become a habit among certain critics of the Act to write and speak of de- posit contributors as if they were, or are likely to be, a large proportion of the 16,000,000 insured persons coming within the scope of the scheme. That is an entirely erroneous notion. No one at this stage can say with certainly what the total number of deposit contributors will be; but the breadth and elasticity of the strictly insurance provisions of the Act, and the in- ducements offered to approved societies to cast their nets wide, make it reasonably cer- tain that the rsons compelled to become deposit contributors will represent but a very small proportion of the workers of the coun- try. We say " compelled" advisedly. Some may elect to become deposit contributors who could, at their own option, become mem- bers of approved societies. If so, they alone will be blameworthy. The doors leading to the full benefits of the Act are wide, and it is broadly true to say that they are open to all save a very small minority of the nation who, owing to incurable disease, or chronic ill-health, or known vicious habit.,, are not in anft conceivable circumstances an insur- able class. Many even of those who, under ordinary conditions of insurance, would be rejected as " bad lives" will unquestionably be eligible for admission to approved socie- ties, and so able to qualify for the full bene- fits of the Act; for, thanks to the induce- ments offered, the medical tests imposed by large societies are sure to be low' and in seine cases (e.g., the Manchester Unity of Oddfellows and some of the large industrial insurance societies and trade unions) are, it ts understood, to be abolichea althaAther It has been alleged that the deposit con- tributor class will be largely recruited from (a) domestic servants and (b) enforced lapses from approved societies. Such an allegation plainly misrepresents the provisions of the Act. Domestic servants are freely eligible for admission to approved societies; no single class of workers, indeed, is more eligible; and if, despite this eligibility, they are so foolish as to enroll themselves in the ranks of deposit contributors the fault will be theirs alone. Similarly, it is difficult to see how anyone who is familiar with the pro- visions of the Act as to arrears can suggest that the number of deposit contributors is likely to be appreciably swelled by "lapses " from approved societies. Extreme stress of circumstances may cause a few " lapses " from approved societies; but, speaking broadly, the provisions of the 'Act regarding arrears are so uniquely generous, and so wide in their scope, as to make it almost im- possible, save in very exceptional circum- stances, for insured persons to drop out of membership. A large proportion of the criticism with which Sections 42 and 43 of the Insurance Act have been assailed is, unfortunately, of a negative and destructive kind. Much of it entirely ignores the positive benefits con- ferred upon deposit contributors by the Act. It is necessary, therefore, to point out that the hardship in their ,case, real but inevi- table as it is, is relative and not absolute. Although they are necessarily an uninsurable class, the Act does not leave them where private and voluntary inlurance has left them—stranded and helpless; it organises in their interest certain assured help, and adds the contributions of the State and of the employers to their own deposits. In other words, the deposit contributor shares in the ordinary benefits of the Act, but to an extent, and during a period of time, that are determined by the amount of funds standing to his credit in the Post Office account. This statement is subject to one important qualification. So far as two of the statutory benefits are concerned, the benefits accruing to the deposit contributor are not limited by the amount of his credit balance. Even in cases where his credit balance is exhausted, his right to medical benefit and to sanatorium benefit continues , until the expiration of the current year, 1 and these benefits may be extended beyond that period if the local Insurance Committee think fit to do so. The Act also contains provisions which, subject to the consent of the local authority and of the Treasury, place additional funds (derived in equal pro- portions from local rates and national revenues) at the disposal of local Insurance Committees for medical and sanatorium benefits, and these provisions will naturally work to the special advantage of derosit con- tributors. Moreover, deposit contributors have their interests specially safeguarded by direct representation on flu. Inral insursnee</p>
        <p>Committees. 'They share in the benefits anti also in the control of the scheme. No one pretends, and least of all Mr. Lloyd George, that sections 42 and 43 of the Act represent the last word in ameliorative help for the unfortunate clans who nrill be com- pelled to become deposit contributors. The scheme set up is avowedly a provisional and tentative scheme, and it comes automatically to an end on December 31st, 1014. Before that date Parliament is pledged to address it- self afresh and directly to the case and needs of deposit contributors. What may justly Da claimed is that the provisions relating to deposit contributors confer positive and sub:, stantial, but necessarily limited, benefits upon a class whose needs cannot be met by in- surance; and that, upon the whole, they are the best that could be done in present circum- stances and within the limits of our present knowledge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
