      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>AMATEUR THEATRICALS AT LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>AMATEUR THEATRICALS AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Amateur theatricals always possess a certain degree of interest by reason of the permnnel of the partici- pants, but the dramatic entertainment given in the Parish Rootn, Latchford, on Monday evening was exceptionally attractive on its merits, and had cone cf the stiffness or awkwardcess so often associated with amateur efforts. It was, in fact, distinctly creditable in eery respect and thanks are due to the Mit see Broadbent for arranging this delightful means of aiding the funds of the Christ Church Company of the Church Lads' Brigade. It was pleasing to see such a large audience, including many prominent towns- people sod brigade officers and supporters' The first dramatic item was the short sketch "The Collaborators" presented by Miss R. Lister-Kaye and Mr. P. R. Doll. It was one of those pleasit g but ft any trifles of the approved curtain riser" type, the diameters being a lady playwright with high flown notions of the dramatic fitness of things and a gentleman collaborator of a prosaic and matter of fast, turn of mind, who insists upon unpleasantly emphasising the impracticability of the thrilling situations so brilliantly conceived by his romantic companion. The parts were admirably taken by Miss Lister Kaye and Mr. Doll, who thoroughly entered into the spirit of the episode, and the hearty applause which followed the perform- ance was fully deserved. The principal feature of the programme was the ccmedietta "Domestic experiments," a bright little play unburdened by any semblance of a seriJus plot yet conceived on rather original lines and bristling with mirth-provoking incidents. Lady Paddington has been so unsuccessful in her attempts to solve the eternal " servant problem" that she decides upon the novel experiment of inviting members of the aristocracy who have seen better days to accept subordinate positions in her household Thus it comes to pass that Lord Stoneybrook, fresh from Kloodyke, is seen masquerading as cock and as he atones for short- comings in the culinary department by drawing-room accomplishments of an unimpeachable kind he is voted a distinct acquisition. Miss Agatha St. Orme, a smart young lady addicted to Carlyle, Maeterlink and Ibsen—and also to musical comedy becomes the housemaid and son captivates Sir Joseph Faddington; while Captain Kempton-Park, who has retired from the army for pecuniary reasons and devotes all his spare time to Sandow exercises, is installed as footman. The play has not been long in progress before it is discovered that there is a prior attachment between Lord Stoneybroke and Nina, the daughter of Sir Joseph and Lady Faddington, and as his lordship's uncle considerately quits this mortal sphere and leaves him inheritor of a dultedom,the match commends itself to all concerned and, after many diverting situations, the play ends with " Success to the duke and duchess." Without particularising, it may be said that all the characters were well sustained, the artistes teeming quite at home in their respective ioles and acting with naturalness, brightness and confidence. The cast was as follows :—Sir Joseph Paddington, Captain Anderson ; Lord St.ineybroke, Mr P It Doll • Captain Kempton- Paik, Captain N B Grounds ; Lady Faddington, Miss Broadbent; Nina (her daughter), Mrs Lloyd; the Honourable Geraldine Fitz-Maurice, Miss M Broad- bent ' • Miss Agatha St. Orme, Miss It Lister-Kaye. The eccond act afforded the opportunity for the introduc- tion of acme delightful songs, duets and dances, by the members of the company, which were highly appreciated. The St Elphin's and St. Paul's compinies of the Church Lads' Brigade went through physical drill and gymnastic displays in a smart manner which testified to the excellence of the instruction they receive. The Latchford Academy orchestra, under the leadership of Mr. W. Turner, played selections in creditable style.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>LIVERPOOL'S MONSTER ORGAN.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL'S MONSTER ORGAN.</p>
        <p>Lecturing to the rtembers of the Liverpool and District Organist and Choirmasters' Association, in the Common Hall, Liverpool, on Monday night, Mr. Henry i Willis, juu., sketched n an informative manner the developments in the construction of organs, witih special reference to tenet] design, and incidentally dwe t on the poslbilities of the instrument to be built in the new Liverpool Cathedral. Not only, he said, would this embrace the fullest and most modern equipment that organ architecture could provide, but in size and capabilities it would form by far the largest instrument of its kind in Great Britain, and possibly also in Europe. Concerning the pressure, he declared that the most elaborate I devices a ould be utilised, and a matter of interest to organists was the large use of a pressure of ten inches in the manual scheme for flue work, and es much as fifty inches for reed work—a scale which, it was believed, had never before been approached. Mr. Willis, the lecturer, is a member of the firm of Henry Willis and Sons, organ builders, of London. The St. George's Hall organ was built by his grandfather, while within quite recent times the firm have been responsible for the erection of the instrument in the Cathedral Lady Chapel, together with those in the parish churches of Liverpool, Walton, and Port Sunlight. In his address Mr. Willis asserted that the specification made in connection with the scheme and the hope of itg fulfilment were due solely to the endeavours of Mr. W. J. Ridley, a nephew of Mrs. Barrow, who for this purpose, it may be recalled, has made a most generous gift of £lB,OOO.</p>
      </div>
