      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>_ OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>_ OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS REMIXED.]</p>
        <p>it is all very unfortunate and distress- ing," she said slowly, "but we will not dis- cuss the subject again, dear. It is too pain- ful. I am sure Mr. Hawke is punished enough by your leaving him. We will make preparations to go abroad next week. Don't refer to what has happened to part you and your husband, dear; we will keep that a secret froth everyone." "I am afraid Isobel will not do that," Stella said slowly, "somehow she hates me very bit- terly, though I can't understand why.' Lady Mary grunted. "Humph ! she probaltly wanted to marry Clifford herself; it's a pity she didn't." Stella was silent, and after a few minutes Lady Mary made an excuse to lease ner lone. The girl's white face disturbed her, And made her long to go and tell Clifford Elawke what she thought of him. Th ngs A-ere in a hopeless tangle now, and she saw 16 way out of it for any of the three people most concerned. A servant met her on the ttairs.</p>
        <p>"Mr. Tennant is in the morning-room, four ladyship. He says it is very urgent that de should see you at once!" Lady Mary hurried downstairs, and opened the cluor of the morning-room. Juspar len- naht turned and went up to her with out- stretched hands. His face was pale and showed signs of sleepless nights. "My dear boy," Lady Mary sighed, "I am cHighted to see you, but you ought not to have come."</p>
        <p>"Why not? " h said quickly. "Stella is here. She has left her huoband, and of course in the circumstances you should not meet."</p>
        <p>"Why has she left him? " he said quickly. "I did not know. I came to see you—to tell you that I know for a fact that that man was the instigator of the plot against my life, and that there are other things being said about him. The man is a monster of iniquity !"</p>
        <p>"I know," she said quietly; but what do you mean to do? Stella knows now that it was her husband who kept you apart, and for that reacon she has left him for ever; but-s Tie is still tied to him, mu know. It is a dread- ful thing for you all." Jaspar set his teeth. "Poor darling!" he muttered, "let me see her—just to say good-bye. I must! " Lady Mary put her hand on his arm. „ . . .</p>
        <p>"I Can trust you. Jaspar." she said slowly, "you won't let her learn anything frcuh against that man. Remember he is her hus- band."</p>
        <p>"You may trust me.," he replied quietly, "for her own sake I will say nothing."</p>
        <p>A few momente later be tapped at the bou- doir door. There was no answer, and open- ing it softly he saw a sight that thrilled him with tenderness. Stella was lying in a low armchair, her eyes close t 4 and her long lashes lay on her pale cheeks like shadows. Her handu lightly clasped were on her lap, and the bright gold of the wedding ring on her Linger was the only outward sign of the barrier 'tetween them.</p>
        <p>He went up to her, and bending down he pressed a light kiss on her fingers. The next moment her eyes opened and a vivid blush coloured her face as she sprang to her feet with a little cry. "Jaspar! You! Why did you come here?" she cried quickly. "I did not know you were here till Lady Mary told me," he said quickly, "then I had to come because I wanted to see you again. You forgive me, dear?" "Yes," she murmured, "I forgive you. I wanted to see you, too, to say good-bye. You have heard from Lady Mary, I suppose, what has happened?" He nodded.</p>
        <p>"Yes, and I wish I could have the right to tight your battle, my darling, but I am com- pelled to stand outside your life." Her eyes shone with tears. "At least you are my loyal true friend," she whispered, "but oh, Jaspar, if that—that awful misunderstanding had not happened how different things might have been." His hands gripped hers, and then let them go. He longed to tell her that he knew all Clifford Hawke's perfidy and treachery, but pity and love kept the word.; back. For her dear sake he could not let go his loathing and hatred of .the man who had won her by trickery: _</p>
        <p>"I ;hall always be near," he answered tenderly. "I shall not leave England till you have gone away with Lady Mary, for you will stay with her, won't your' Stella nodded. "Yes. I will live with her for some time, after that—she paused, and then added, "I can't say what I want to say, dear—he is my husband, you understand." "I understand," he replied gravely. "You need tell me nothing. I know.</p>
        <p>Then he raised her hands to his lips and pressed along tender kiss on them: -</p>
        <p>- "Though we may not meet again I shall always hope and long for the sight of your face and the sound of your voice," he said in a low impassioned whisper, and then he dropped her hands just as the door opened and Lady Mary .came into the room, employ- ing an unusual amount of bustle as she did so.</p>
        <p>Stella turned to her with a momentary embarrassment, which gave way to an ex- pression of anxiety as she noticed the grave look in Lady Milry's eyes. "What has happened? " she said quickly. "Don't be anxious, dear, but I have opened a telegram that came for you," Lady Mary replied quietly. "I was afraid lest there was some news in it that might have to be broken to you, and it certainly is puzzl- ing to say the least of it." Stella reached out her hand for the tele- gram. Her face had grown very pale. The next moment it had fluttered to the ground and a cry of amazement rose to her lips. `Lottie missing !" she exclaimed, " what can it mean?"</p>
        <p>Jaspar picked up the telegram and read it aloud. It had been sent by the housekeeper at Clifford liawke's town house, who had evi- dently known her mistress was with Lady Mary. "Lottie disappeared. Is she with you, Bates?" Jaspar repeated slowly. "Is Lottie here, Mrs. Hawke?" he went on. Stella clasped her hands. "No, I left her there intending to take her away later on. Perhaps she has been hurt or run over again? I wish now I had brought her straight on here. Ido hope she is not hurt."</p>
        <p>"Perhaps your husband can help us," Jaspar said doubtfully. "I'm afraid not. He ignored poor Lottie, and though I asked him to be kind to her till I sent for her, he probably has not spoken to her."</p>
        <p>"Don't worry," he went on. "I will go now and do my best to get some news of her. It may be an unnecessary alarm on the housekeeper's part; I will begin now," he went on, "and either wire or call with any news I have."</p>
        <p>She looked at him with shining eyes. "Thank you very much," she murmured, "it is so good of you!" "I am glad to be of service to you," he plied, and Lady Mary remembered her own youth in turning away a moment while their hands met in a long lingering clasp. When he had gone Stella went up to Lady Mary. "It's no use, dear," she said 9uickly, "I must go away when we find Lottie • I must not meet him again—l love him, and I have no right to love any man." "Courage, dear !" Lady Mary replied quietly, "you are young, and life is full of In the meantime Jasper had hurried away, Ms mind full of suspicion. He had not told Stella all that that telegram had suggested to his mind, but he was going to pay a visit to Clifford Hawke's town house, and make a few inquiries. Poor little Lottie ! She had been the means of his meeting Stella, and had becoim a kind of link between them. She must be found, for she had been a dear loyal little friend, and her sweet face had always , been particularly precious to him on account I of her haunting likeness to that sister whose 11111134,DV marriAae htd driven his mother to</p>
        <p>have a chat about the old Colony one dayl he went on. "I have a pressing call to mat -- just now, but this evening I shall be free, and will be glad to see you at the Artiste Club _about nine."</p>
        <p>"Thanks very much," was the slow reply. "I shall remember. I, too, have an appoint- ment that must be kept. But to-night I will call on you with pleasure. My name," he went on, "is Steinway—Paul Steinway." Paul Steinway !" The London street grew dim and distant in Jaspar's sight, while he seemed to see again the wild Australian bush where he first heard the name of Paul Steinway—the man who had been a witness to the marriage of his dead sister to the man who had deserted her.</p>
        <p>"You know my name !" Steinway said</p>
        <p>"Know it Jaspar laughed bitterly. "I should think so. I have been looking for you ever since I came to England a couple of years ago." "Who are you?" Steinway said sharply. "The youngest brother of Alys Tennant— Jaspar Tennant." There was a moment's pause, and then Paul Steinway reached out his hand. "It is quite true," he said simply, "there's a Divinity that shapes our ends, rough hew them as we may. What do you want of me?" "The name of the man who married and deserted my miserable sister and her child," Jaspar said slowly. A strange light came into Paul Steinway's eyes. "I will tell you; we are not a stone's throw from his house," he replied, "but first I have an appointment with him, and after that I will met you and tell you everything, only I must deal with him first."</p>
        <p>"I can't wait any longer," Jaspar said im- patiently. The idea of being so near and yet so far from his vengeance was madden- ing. "Won't you tell me now, so that I can wait while you are with him—or go in with you?" Paul Steinway shook his head. The younger man's ardour was fresh, and con- trasted with his own life long patience that had waited all these years for the completion of his vengeance. "You won't have long to wait how," he said. "I must go in alone—my interview will not be a long one, but must be iv Private one. If you like you may wait till I come out."</p>
        <p>Jaspar hesitated. He remembered how anxious Stella would be to hear about Lottie, and that she might think he had forgotten his promise if he failed to turn up with news. "I can't watt long," he said. "I am searching for a young protegee friend of mine who is missing from her home, and I pro- mised Mrs. Hawke to let her know about this at once."</p>
        <p>The face of Pa;il Stolowav liqd flushed</p>
        <p>and he reeled with a sierly sensation of en- lightened horror. -The gateway that Paul Steinway had passed through led to Clifford Hawke' s house —Clifford Hawke, who was now the husband of the woman he loved with all his soul! And Clifford Hawke was tho man who bad deserted his sister!</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>P\ l''," Id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>BID FOR A BRIDE</head>
        <p>A BID FOR A BRIDE</p>
        <p>BLANCHE EARDLEY, Author of "Kitty Bell—Actress," "The Lady</p>
        <p>Killer," Bce.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXII. (C,ontinned.) AN UNEXPECTED MEETING.</p>
        <p>Paul Steinway stared into Cardew's face for a few seconds without speaking. .1-fis own face had gone a curious grey colour, and his eyes shone with a peculiar steely light. "Then it was murder !" he said in a low voice.</p>
        <p>"Yes, I thought you</p>
        <p>Cardew nodded. knew." No. I only bluffed Hawke that I did. I suspected it though I had no real tangible evidence," replied Steinway slowly. "I am not surprised, though; I always thought he was capable of it. I have letters now from people who knew him out there as John Ford —and John Clifford latterly. The secret .1 had was concerning his first wife, whom be married out in Tarragoola..lt was after that that he became suddenly rich and left the district."</p>
        <p>"Yes," Cardew said. "Just about the time that Nat Radbourne disappeared so mysteriously. It's odd that we both knew so much and yet kept our own counsel. But when you mentioned what you sort of sus- pected, I said nothing, but made up my mind to do a bit of detective work myself, and I have succeeded. Paul, my boy! you can go to-morrow to Clifford Hawke and tell him that we hold the proofs of his murder of Nat Radbourne. I'll give them to you if you like," he went on; "they're in my pocket now—one or two little bits of evidence that would hang the popular financier!" "Don't give them to me," Steinway said quietly. "I have a fancy to have nothing on me of any importance just now. You keep everything, old man—not only your evidence about this murder, but some of my papers too. You know I have made a bit of money lately financially, and am going to give back- a little bit of the Hades that Clifford Hawke gave me some time ago! The best of it is." be laughed grimly, "that he flatters himself he was rid of me and my suspected know- ledge of him in that fire in Claverton-street. I have let him think so too—it served my purpose to be 'dead ' a bit till lie showed his hand more plainly! But now I think I have him in the hollow of my own hand, and he won't get out of it either. My turn is coin. ing soon." And you are the man to profit by it," Cardew said briskly, "Anything I can do for you, old man, I will do, you may depend Upon it, from calling on your beet girl, to witnessing your will." Steinway smiled. "There's one or two things you can do for me, old pal," he said earnestly; "since we parted for ever as I thought, there is a little girl I've taken a great fancy to—a dear little slip of a thing who is the image of what poor Alys Tennant might have been before she married this scoundrel, Clifford Hawke. Her name is Lottie Bond, and I met her quite accidentally. I have made my will and left her everything—with the exception of a legacy to you, old man. Would you be a witness to the will and keep it for me with the other papers concerning Hawke?" Cardew held out his hand</p>
        <p>"Yes, certainly, -only don't talk about wills in that serious way, Paul; you are a long journey from death yet, I hope, and you'll be marrying one day and be the faille: of a happy family." "No," Steinway smiled sadly. "I once had thoughts like that, but recently I've had a feeling that marriage and families are not for me. Should anything happen to me you will promise to call on Lottie Bond. She lives with Mrs. Hawke, by the way—the second wife—and tell her, Lottic, that she is provided for." Cardew nodded.</p>
        <p>"You may rely on me, Paul, but to-morrow you will laugh at these previous arrange- ments." .</p>
        <p>"I don't think so," Steinway replied gravely. "You ace, Cardew, my dear chap, I have some method in my madness ; to-mor- row I am going to pay a call on our mutual friend, Mr. Clifford Hawke, and I like to be prepared for the worst."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>CHAPTER XXIII. ALI'S TENNANT'S HUSBAND,</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIII. ALI'S TENNANT'S HUSBAND,</p>
        <p>"I have tried, and I have failed But I can't help it, Aunt Mary, I am mißerable at the tangle I have made of everything, but I could have borne it so long as I did not know this last thing—but this has finished my make-believe of married life with Clifford Hawke."</p>
        <p>----- Lady Mary gazed at Stella's white sad face and she could not find it in her heart to re- proach her. She had learnt from the girl when she had come to her the night before of the scene that had taken place between the !husband and wife, and her heart had ached with sympathy. "I know, dearest, what you feel now," she said gently, " but remember you are young, and your husband is a man in the prime of life—for years you might be tied to each other, unable to free yourselves. Think what a long life lies before you if you cut yourself quite out of his life." Stella turned to her with flashing eyes and suddenly flushed cheeks. "Could I be more lonely in the future than I have been in the past?" she said quickly. "My marriage was a mistake—my husband plotted vilely to keep the man I loved away till I was his wife, he bad murder on his soul, so far as he knew, at the very moment when he was standing at the altar with me. Oh, Aunt Mary," she went on passionately, "don't turn from me when I want your help and sympathy. I have no one but you now. My father is too angry with me for being un- happy, and my cousin Isobel is openly glad that I have found out my mistake.' "Wretched girl!" Lady Mary snapped. "I always said she was a snake in the grass." Then she went on tenderly, "You know I am with you heart and soul in everything. I am thinking only of the future for you. It is a dangerous thing for a woman to be living apart from her legal partner when her heart is in danger of being the property of another man."</p>
        <p>"I never loved Clifford, and I do love Jas- par," Stella said in a low voice, "but my love is not the kind that brings regrets and misery in its wake, neither is his. We will never meet unless it is by accident; if we do we shall both remember the barrier that is between us." Lady Mary was silent. She was remember- ing a similar scene she had had with Jaspar Tennant only a couple of days ago, and the man's outburst of passion had been similar to the girl's, only she was a woman of the world, and- knew that it was easy to make brave re- solutions when away from temptation, and she regretted that the final breach had oe. curred between Clifford Hawke and the girl who was his wife in name only.</p>
      </div>
