      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>SHIP CANAL PIONEER.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL PIONEER.</p>
        <p>-- Death of Sir Bosdin T. Leech, of Manchester.</p>
        <p>By the death of Sir Bosdin T, Leech, which occurred at his residence, Oak Mount, Timperley, on Tuesday morning, Manchester loses one of her test known citizens, and one who his given over 30 years devoted work to her civic and commercial advancement. Enteling the Council in 1880, he at once threw all his energy kto schemes for the development of the city. In the preliminary work necessary to tle con- solidation of the local Acts of Parliament be was the prime mover, but it is for his remarkable efforts on behalf of the famous Ship Canal that his name will be banded down. Ilia wide knowledge of the city's com- merce, his keen appreciation of its immediate need and his unqualified optimism as to the success of the venture caused him to throw his whole energies into sezuring the great canal. De was one f those who attended the first meeting in the house of Mr. Daniel Adamson at Didsbury in 1882, and thence onward he was actively associated with all the ups and downs that were encountered before the scheme was completed. The two volumes in which be has faithfully recorded the history of the canal provide one of the most interesting romances of modern eninsering</p>
        <p>SALVATION ARMY SELF-DENIAL WEEK. " The War Cry " contains a letter frcm General Booth announcing the result of the Salvation Armyi self-denial week. So far a total of £56,027 has been received, but it is estimated that this will be increased to £59,037, when the effort has been concluded in South %Vales and on the Tees, where the strike has involved almost complete paralysis. The decrea-e on last year's figures is £B,lOO. General Booth says: "In spite of the vast dislocation in trade caused by the coal strike, the suspension. of communications, the eholesale dis- charge of working people from their emplo3 most, and in the midst of the general anxiety and distress manifest on all hands, we have, by the bles3lng ot God, been able to gather for Ills kingdom a sum which, though it will fall short of last year's mout, and, alas! will fall short of our need.., is nevertheless a call for our deepest gratitude. . . We have had vic'ory cnce more, a victory snatched from the very verge (1 dismster, a victory which promises greater victories still in the years to come."</p>
        <p>The clot log of a house at 133 serth has beo3 orderod by the Flint Rural District Council because it has no water supply, no drainage, no light, no ventilation, and no bedroom. The living-room is used for sleeping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Royalty End Parie.—The Q.reen is not so well acquainted with Paris as Queen Alexand a, but there is some idea that her Majesty may pay a private visit to Faris, during the stay of the Prince of Wales with the Marquis and Marquess de Breteuil. King Edwarit, who made no secret of his Lye of France, first visited Paris in 1855, when a boy of fourteen. During the decade from 1860 to 1870, the Prince was a familiar figure at the Court of Napoleon and the Empress Eugenie, and, after la'a marriage, the Princess—now Queen Alexandra —frequently accompanied him. The Creteuils were, on several occ: sions, Ur) guests of King Edward and Quee n Alexandra at Windsor for the Ascot races and were also entertained by their Majesties at, Sandringham. The Marquis is a typical representative of the old French nobility, but he knows this country well, and was at one time a regular visitor to Scotland for the fishing season. His wire is an American, and before her marriage was Miss Garner of Nsw York. She and her sister, who became Lady Gordon Cumming, mere left orphans, whilst quite young and both inherited a large fortune, on coaling of age. The Marquia's brother also married an Ant,rican lady, and the Prince of Wales will sees good deal of American society in Paris,during his stay at the Hotel de Breteuil etich is one of the most princely mansiors overlooking the famous Avenue du Bois de Boulagne. Memorials to Queen Victoria and King Edward.— Princes Christian is to unveil the statue of King Edward which has been executed by Countess Feodora Gleicben, and erected in the grounds of King Ede ard VII. Hospital at Windsor. The ceremony, at which many members of the Royal Family are to be present, is to take place the end of the month. Meanwhile attention has been directed to the sTgnal honour paid to the memory of British Sovereigns, by the inauguration of the two Riviera memorials to Queen Victoria at Nice, and to King Edward at Canoes. In their personal sense they nee intended to com- memorate the long connection of the Br.tish Bey I Family with tt e South of France. During the last twenty years of her life, Queen Victoria paid repeated visits ti the French Riviera and although familiar with many of the resorts, she loved none so well as Nice. 0,.e of her last desires, not destined to to be fulfilled, was th st she might once re-visit Nice in the spring of 1901. In another aspect, the two Riviera memorials have a greater signitlmuce, and symbAise a graceful act of international amity. It is a striking proof of the world-wide love and respect in which Queen Victoria and her eldest sou were held, that these two memorials should have been erected in Republican France.</p>
        <p>Ihe Feminist Movement in (ho East.—The Oriental races are going in strongly fir son 'I and politic kl reform, and our militant suffragists must look with envy on the successes achieved by the leaders of the Feminist movement in the East. It is true that in Turkey feminine freedom has received something of a check. Turkish women who manifested a taste to don he hobble dress in place of the harem E kirt, have pro- voked a reaction, and it would appear as if they had flaunted their newly won liberties with unwise ardour. In China the women have been more successful in glamouring for recognition on equal terms with men. There is a Woman Suffragist Federation in Canton which has gone one better than anything as yet done in this country—in conducting a campaign in the most approved Parliament Square style. The women in attacking the Nanking Assembly overcame the Chinese policemen, entered the Chamber and put the President and legislators to flight. So at least, it is reported, although it is diflioult to realise that such a remarkable change hts taken place in Chinese women, who not s long ago used to boast of their subjection to man, and their physical ineapacities. The strong athletic European woman they held in scorn. It was their habit to mock at " foreign devils " as being afraid to have more than ore wife, for fear of getting a thrashing in time of domestic trouble. Under paid Shop Assistants.—Retail drapery estab- lishments—both in London and the provincial towns,— are meeting with some trouble in finding trained shop assistants. It is hardly surprising that this should be so, seeing that the majority of women and girls thus employed do not earn a " living wage." It Is .the custom for shop assistents to denounce the " living- in " s) stem, tut mikes a girt has parents or relatives who can afford her a home, she is better off provided for in this way than in trying to maintain herself independently upon her wages of £3O i or Lto a year. If it is a choice of evils, the" living-in " system s the best, but few are contented with it, and shop assistants are beginn;ng to agitate not only fot the principle, but tout for the substance of the minimum wage. Good saleswomen in leading houses earn substantial salaries, but long hours and inadequate ray are the lot of tile vast majority of female shop assistants, and it is scarcely to be wondered at that women and girls are trying to avoid an occupation which does not afford them a "living wage." Some Popular Materials.—Satin-faced foulard is such a useful summer dress fabric, and the new patterns so charming, that there is no doubt of its popularity this reason eclipsing that of last year. Its many virtues are so well-known that to enumerate them here is quite unnecessary. Chiffon cloth, thickly woven, and yet transparent is not altogether a novelty. Unfor- tunately for the women of moderate means, its price is prohibitive, but it is worth the money it costs. Many of the new, and not a few of the old materials have herders, some of which are flowered, whilst others have merely a band of a contrasting colour. Satin will rival taffetas. especially a new make with a strong back and very brilliant surface. Taffetas is a fabric that appears to possess boundless possibilities in the matter of variety. The latest designs—to follow the flowered fabric —are striped and chequered, and very quaint they both are. Not every woman however will be able to wear these advantageously, as they require height and</p>
      </div>
