      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Great Sankey.</head>
        <p>Great Sankey.</p>
        <p>WHIST DAMS AND DANCE.—This successful function took place on Frid ty in the St Mary's schools, Great Sankey, when over 100 were present. It was the second whist drive and dance promoted by the Penketh Tannery Prize Brass Band and was looked forward to with a good deal of interest. The whist players consisted of 16 sets and all entered into this game heartily. Messrs Ernest Dumbill and George Carman acted as M.C.'s. The prizes were won by the following :—Ladies : 1. Mrs James Ray ; 2, Mrs Herbert Glover ; 3, Mrs Albert Bowyer. Gentlemen : 3, Mr James Hughes ; 2, Mr Wilfred ;3, Mr H Davies. Dancing was entered into with great zest until a late hour. Tte music being supplied by Messrs J and F Hall. The Nl.C.'s for claiçing -m Nes r§ R Plump! on end Tenstall who discharged their duties in e. elicient mv.a.er. The refreshments were in the capable hands of Mesdames G. Clare, J Ray, jun., and W Woods. GREAT SANKEY CHURCH —The vestry meeting in can nection with the above church took place on Thursday evening in the Sankey facia oh, when be Rev J R Jones occupied the chair. The Vicar nominated Mr Newbold his warden and Mr E Du Jabal was appointed people's warden. The following gentlemen were appointed sidesmen : —Messrs C A Littler, D it Griffiths; it Graham, Thomas Ray, James Ray (seal, James Ray (jun), D Howard, Thomas Kilshaw, J B Al ken, George Clarke, James Millington, W W Taylor, F Kertland, Peter Hill, Garnett and Haslam. Mr. George Clarke was re-elected auditor. Messrs 11 Faired ugh and C A Littler were re-elected lay representatives. Mr E J Dumbill presented the statement . f accounts which showed that the year had been commenced with a balance of £1.7 8s 4d in hand. The total receipts had been £25l Os 10d and the balance in hand at the present aline was £4 2s Id. There was a sum of £6 18s 2d in favour of the special repair fund. The report was unanimously adopted and the Vicar proposed a hearty vote of thanks to all church workers wraith was carried in the usual manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE BAPTIST CHURCH.—The Rev. 11. Ogden preached the Sunday school anniversary sermons In connection with this church on Sunday, when there' were large congregations. In the morning the preacher based his sermon on the three aspects of the child, the physical, mental, and spiritu A, and in the evening he dealt with the home life of the child. Special anthems wire sung at both the morning and evening services by 1 the children and emir. The soloist at the evening service was Miss Maud Hipkiss. A musical service was held in the afternoon, Mr W H Sherburn presiding An address was given by Mr. 0. Davey-Smith on "The lessons to be learnt from a sheet of newspaper." Solos and duets were beautifully ',rendered by the Misses J Woodland, M Jones and H Spruce, of the Piimitive Methodist Church, Legh-street, and anthems were sung by the choir and Sunday saw] scholars. The organist at the services was Miss Ada Grounds, and Mra James Kenworthy conducted. Miss Isherwood acted as the accompanist. The collections, which were well above the average, amounted to .£8 18s. ST. THOMAS' BAND OF Hoe.—An entertainment in I connection with the above as given on Thursday evening in the parish room. There was a good attend- ance. The chair was taken by Mrs Bedell supported by Mrs Milling. The programme which was of a varied and interesting character, was well sustained through- out and was concluded by a comic sketch "Curing a toper," in which the following took part : Misses Connie Vernon, E Bagoley, Clara Vernon, J Harmer and Messrs It Hatton, J Probert and J Livingstone. On the motion of Mr Kerr, seconded by Mr Noden votes of thanks were passed to Mrs Bedell, Mrs Milling, Mr and Mrs Whiteman, Misses Hallows' Acton, Baguley, Mr Davies, Mr Holmes and all who bad taken part. Mr Hallows said bow pleasing it wss t 3 see so many parents and friends present. He regretted the absence of their Vicar who WAS away from home but from whom he had received a very kind note wishing them every success. The singing of the National Anthem brought a very successful meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>PARISH COUNCIL-ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL-ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Sto Aston Heath Parish Couccil was held in the Hall Cafe on Monday evening. Mr II J Wt stbrook presided aud at the outset said he would like to take that opportunity of acknowledging their extreme loyalty th him during Ile time he hal officiated in the capacity of chairman of that Council. lie hoped his successor, whoever he might be, would have the Same happy times as he had had during his term of office. . _ _</p>
        <p>_ Mr E Pearson, in proposing Mr. James Whitley as chairman, said he thought they wraild agree with liirn when he 'aid that no-one was better fitted for that cffice than Mr. Whitley who bad rendered the Council such earnest and useful service. lhey all knew bow indefatigable he had been as chairman of the Lighting Committee in mapping out the whole of the parish. He had been very enthusiastic in all his work for the Pariah Council and the Parochial Committee. (Hear, hear). Mr. Mclntyre in seconding said it was well knows to everyone how enthusiastic Mr. Whitley had been in his work for the Council. He had rendered much valu- able service not only as a parish councillor ,but in every office be had held. He had filled every office with dignity to himself and also to the parish. (Hear, hear.) In supporting the proposition Mr J H Clarke thought it was a most fitting and proper thing to elect Mr Whitley as chairman. He knew of no-one in the village who bad taken more interest in the parish. He had kept them well posted in regard to the gas question—(hear, hear)—and kept some of them from getting too extravagant in regard to that question. (Hear, hear, and applause) Mr C Hallows and Mr J it Woods a's° spoke in eulogistic terms of Mr Whitley, and Mr Westbrook endorsed everything which had been said by the previous speakers, adding that he thought Mr. Whitley 's conduct in the past had merited all that had been said of him. (Hear, hear.) He then vacated the chair in favour of his successor, who in his opening speech said the honour had come as somewhat of a surprise. He thanked them for the honour they had cdnferred upon him, although be had thought there ware more competent men than himself, lie also thanked the various speakers for the kind words they had spoken. It could not really be expected that he (the speaker) could fulfil the office with the honour and ability that it had been filled in the past. They had been fortunate in bygone years in having good, Sound sensible gentlemen in that office. He had felt a degree of—he was going to say conceit—pleasure in being under the government, of the gentlemen who had acted as Chairmen of the Council in ne past. He would endeavour to maintain in the future, the dignity which his prechcessors bad built up. Meat, ter) He would also like to add that he was glad to have such an esteemed clerk as Mr. Bradshaw to point the finger in the right direction. (Hear, hear and applause.) Mr J H Clarke said that the whole of the parish had been very much, indebted to the late chairman. Mr. Westbrook, for tflhe creditable way in which he had conducted the affairs of the pari.,h for the past four years. The village had without doubt succeeded and prospered to some ”tent under the chairmanship of Mr. Westbrook. Daring the last two years he bad had to deal with two ereat events, the death of the King, and the Coronation. The obserwnces on both occa ions were carried out successfully and a great deal of the success was due to the chairman He had proved himself a very able chairm!in. (Applause). Mr J R Wood seconded in a brief manner and Mr Whitley in supporting eaid fie had been the right man In the right place. (Applause). Mr Westbrook, in response, said he had taken more interest in his position as chairman because her was a n tive of Stockton Heath. He bad dose his,best with great pleasure to himself. (Applause). ELECTION 017 OVERSEERS. Messrs Whitley, PGarson and Warburton were re-elected overseers, and Mr Whitley ill his reply said that was about the 15th year he had held that office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>COUNCILLOR F. A. lIARRIS.RESIGNS.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR F. A. lIARRIS.RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>----- A letter was rrad from Mr. F. A. Harris, stating that owing to pressure of other engagements he was desirous of resigning his position as councillor. The resignation as accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>-NEW COUNCILLOR.</head>
        <p>THE -NEW COUNCILLOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson i-n—pro-p-o- Warburt-n be elected as councillor in place of Mr. Harris said that he had been on the Council; before end was a valuable member and it had been a loss to them and the parish when he was not re-elected. Mr J McDermitt seconded and the proposition was heartily canied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>FINANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the balance sheet which showed that they had commenced the year with a balance in hand of .£3 83 and had finished up with a deficit balance of £2 5s 3d. The total expenditure had been .£5 13s 4d, and he estimated that a sum of £l5 would 09 required for the coming year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Mxammer, atiurclay, April, 20, 1912</head>
        <p>Mxammer, atiurclay, April, 20, 1912</p>
        <p>It was decided that a precept be made on the overseers. Mr John Milner was appointed to attend to the trees in the pariah for the salary of V 10s. par annum and it was decided that any questions with re,gard t o the duties of Mr. Milner he left in the hands of the chairman and clerk.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. JOHN JACKSON.—It is With deep regret that we record the death of Mr. John Jackson, of Holly Cottage, Heath-road, Penketb, which took placs on Tuesday, after an illness of only four weeks' duration. The deceased, who 4v,s 74 3 ears of age, was well-known in bowling circles in the village, and took a teen interest in the sport, being one of the instigators of the Heath-road Bowling Club. Idebtified with the Wesleyan Chapel, he took a deep interest in all the affairs of his church, and had held many principal offices, having been a member of one of the classes for over 30 years.—The funeral tu ill take place to-day (Saturday).</p>
        <p>PENKEFH PARISI COUNCIL. The annual meeting of the above was held on Monday evening in the Council school, Stocks-lane. The following members were present : Dr Murray (who presided); Mks Tirsley. Messrs William Davies, Jan es Ditclifleld, James Woodward and E Foster, clerk to the Council. —.----.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>ELECTRICITY SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>ELECTRICITY SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The question of the electricity was brought forward and it was decided to ask Mr Ashton at the next parochial meeting what could be d,,ne for the parish.</p>
        <p>SPEED OF MOTOR TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>—.- On the question of the regulation of tie speed of motor cars &amp;c., through the village being raised, the Chairman said no-one had been more annoyed than himself by the way in %hid) motor cars had been driven at excessive speed through the streets of the village. The matter had, however, been left in the hands of the police The speed of the motor cars coining down from Hill Cliffe was outrageous. (Hear, hear).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Penketli.</head>
        <p>Penketli.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. JAMES WATSON.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Watson's long and faithful servir:es as hoadmaster of the Penketh day school mere fitt'ngly recognised on Saturday in the Wesleyan Sunday school, when a good gathgring of old s 'holars, colleagues and friends of Mr Watson's attended. Others certainly would have been there had the passenger train service been normal. Mr Arthur Wilkinson (an old scholar) presided and was supported by Mr Aaron Appleton (another old scholar), Mr James Watson, and the Rev John Gunnell, bon secretary of the Testimonial Com- mittee. The Chairman first called upon Mr. Gunnell to give a report of the comn3itte 'a work Mr Gunnell said the committee's work had been a pleasing task to them all, and they hal a very generous resporse, nearly 200 subscribing. (Applame). The Chairman then addressed the gathering. He said that when Mr Gunnell informed him that it would give his old s.iloolmaster (Mr Watson) pleasure f..r him to take the chair at that meeting he readily assented, becars, he was only too pleased to give Mr. Watson all the pleasure he cculd. Ile was proud to he there that eveoing to bear Hi testimony to his old se:oolmister, an he was sure that Mt.</p>
        <p>&lt; .4 op.:* • 4 • • Photo</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MR. 'JAMES WATSON.</head>
        <p>MR. 'JAMES WATSON.</p>
        <p>Bates.</p>
        <p>Watson's influence on the many children that had passed through the schools had been a great influence for good and bad helped them to start life a great deal better than they otherwise would have done, because cne of the main points in Mr. Watson's teaching was character ; and they had been able to appreciate this a great deal more as th3y grew up to be men and women. —The Chairman went on to say that Mr. Watson did not confine his work to the day schools, but had done his best in other spheres, especially in the Sunder school, and his work generally had resulted in a great ' deal of good. Mr. Aaron Appleton, in rising to present the illuminated address and purse to Mr. Watson, said it, was customary when making presentations to say very nice antcomplimeutary things, but what pleased him in regard to this was that he knew Mr Watson would wish for nothing but the truth. Never had a testimonial been so thoroughly deserved as in thl3 case. (Applause.) The committee in their work of love on this occasion felt sure that Mr Watson had won the old scholars' high esteem not only for what he had taught but bad impressed on them the value of character, as he realised that character made the man. Many of them would never forget the short prayers and addresses that had been given -by their old schoolmaster. Mr Watson's efforts were to influence their lives so that they would become useful citizens, and as far as he was able, he certainly had succeeded. (Applause). In regard to the addrets he thought that it was a work of art, and reflected great credit upon Mr Routledge, who had so geuerously done the work. (Applause). On behalf of tue old scholars, parents, colleagues and friends, he asked Mr Watson to accept this illuminated address and cheque for £lBO at a small recognition of the work done amongst them. If Mr Watson felt despondent at any time if he would look at the -address and remember the kindly feeling they had•for him, it would give him courage and help him to realise that he had made his life one of useful. nets. Mr Appleton also referred to Mrs Watson and said he was sure that she had a part to do and she had done it well or Mr Watson could not have been so successful. They hoped that Mr and Mrs Watson would have a full measure of health and strength on the days to come. (Applause). Mr. Watson in his reply said be hardly knew how to express his feelings under the circum- stances. He felt he was unworthy of all the many expressions that had been given utterance to, that even- ing. His gratitude to them all was almost inexpressible. He would like ho be able to thank tnem all personally for what they had done and so generously given, if his work had been successful be owed a good deal to his colleague' and he especially made mention of Mrs. Smith who had worked with him for so many years, Mr. Watson went on to speak about his early days as schoolmastsr in Penketh and said that things were far different now, which was a great advantage to teachers lnd scholars alike. He looked back to the times when he with one of the scholars used to set vff on Saturda, mornings to Manchester for books, slates or whatever they wanted. The audience was amused at Mr. Watson telling them how he used to repeur slates, Sco. After further remarks Mr. Watson mid he thanked them all for the love they had shown towards him and he sincerely thanked them all for their kind expressions. (Applause ) At this stage several had to leave to catch their tranis home. The Chairman being one of them, Mr C. E. Parker was asked to preside dnd readily consented. Short speeches were given by old scholars and friends amongst them being Mrs Garnett, Mrs Jenks, Mr Aldcroft, Mr Carman and others. Soogs were given at intervals by Miss Baker aid Miss M Wilkinson. and selections by the Penteth Tannery Prize Band. Refreshments were served during the evening by Mrs Bell, Miss Boardman and others of Mr. Watson's ole colleagues. The illuminated address, which was engrossed by Mr. R. Routledge read To %Ir. James Watson, on hi, retirement, after n arly 40 years service as Headma,t-r of the Penketh day scools, Wesleyan day school trout 1873 to I9oB,County Council school from 1908 to 1912. We cannot allow so long a term of faithful services to close without expresaing our high appreciation of your sterling character, your blameless reputation and sour faithful work. The school monagets with whom you have served gladly testify to your marked efficiency and great kindness as a teacher and your conscientious discharge of all the duties of your office. Your many colleagues cherish grateful memories of your inspiring example and your keen and kindly interest iii their work and welfare. You have also placed the parents under a deep sense of obligation by your tireless devotion to the mental arid moral training of their children Your oil scholars on every hand witness to your skill in teaching, your great tact and patience in discipline, and will ever set a high value on your character and influence, while the present staff and scholars much regret your retirement, and the consequent severance of so happy a boni. We also recognise the valuable work you have done in the village apart from your school'. If esteem is the harvest of a whole life spent in usefulness, yours cannot fail to be a rich and full one. We ask you, dear sir, to receive this address and the accompanying purse as a small token of our sincere regard for you person- ally, and also our warm appreciation of your manifold services with ardent wishes for continued health and much happiness in your comparative retirement. Signed on behalf of the committee and subscribers Charles E Parker, school marager, Isabella Smith, assist ant teacher, 1883-1897, Henry Mainley, old scholar, J G Withenshaw, hon. treasurer; John Gunnell, hon. secretary. " Some lines written by Mr. H. Mainley were read at the meeting. We quote three verses : Our pleasant task to-night, It is to honour one Whose life of usefulness And duty, nobly done, The admiration doth compel Of us, who know and love him well. Head of our village school, For nine and thirty years i ; Dickens could not n him A model find for Squeers. We ever found him Just and kind Although unto our faults not blind. And now the time hascome His task he must lay down, Well earned retirement his, A strenuous life to crown. And, at the parting of the ways, , We wish him peace and length of days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Dr Murray was again re-elected chairman far the ensuing year and Mr J G Withinshaw, vice-chairman and Dr Murray, Messrs J G Withinshavr, and James Ditchfleld were appointed overseers. Miss Tinsley proposed and Mr J Ditchfleld seconded that Dr Murray be re-elected as the representative of education in connection with the C.,uncil at Earlestown and this was agreed to. MAIN ROAD THROUGH VILLAGE. . . . . _ _</p>
        <p>The Clark intimated that he had forwarded the resolution passed at the ratepayers' meeting to the authorities at Preston, and alsJ a copy to the County Councillor, and the followinereplies had been received: —The Clerk to the County Council wrote : I "am in receipt of your letter of the 26th lost, setting out a resolution passed at the meeting of the parishioners of Penketh with regard to the condition of the main road thrcugh Penketh, and beg to state that the same shall have attention." Mr Forshavr (the County Councillor) wrote : "this matter shall have my best nal earliest attention and I hope to ba age to have the same remedied."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>INSANITARY PROPERTY.</head>
        <p>INSANITARY PROPERTY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk slid he hal sent the following letter to the Sanitary Authority at Warrington At the rate- payers meeting held on the 25th of March, a resolution was passed urging the Rural District Council to hasten the inspection of houses in Penketb, and also suggested that houses that have been inspected should be re-inspected to see if the Inspector's instructions have been carried out. _ _</p>
        <p>Inspector Kerfoot wrote in reply :—" Kindly inform me of the particular property meant as coming under the words ' Insanitary Property." The Clerk said he had replied as follows :—" I cannot inform you where the alleged insanitary property is."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>COUNCIL SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>COUNCIL SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>After discussion had taken place on different items raised at the epayers' meeting, Mr Davies said that there was widespread feeling in Penketh that children should be admitted to the Council School at the age uf four years and he propcsed that the Clerk writs the Council school manager and ask them to urge the Education Sub-committtee to reduce the age of children commencing !attendance at school from five years to four. Mr J Woodward after speaking on the matter, seconded the motion and it was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST LANTERN SERVICE.—There was an excellent audience in tha Primitive Methodist schoolroom on Sunday night, when the last lantern service of the season was held. The Rev R W B White- way gate a shori and pointed address on the Ten Virgins, and advised the people to ha ready for the coming of th 3 Bridegroom. The illustrated story followed, Mr W T Moss r ading the tale with Mr W Mcss, jun., r..anipulating the lantern.</p>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH VESTRY MRETING.—The annual vestry meeting of the Lymm Parish Church was held in the vestry on Thursday evening. The Rector, Rev G B Thurston, M.A., presided. Tho Rector nominatod Mr Callender as his warden and Mr .1 G Lister-Farrer as the people's warden. It was decided to reduce the sidesmen from 12 to 8. Elected by vote of vestry as parishioners' sidesmen : Messrs Beckett, F E Evans, T F W Dixon, and R 011iver. Rector's sidesmen: Messrs W G Lecomber, R Heathclte, A B Richardson and W 11 Burgess. Messrs G W C Hartley and S Broadbent were appoi ted lay delegates to the Diocesan Confer- et co. The Rector announced that Miss Gregson and Mrs Spate expressed a wish to present a handsome brass lectern in commemoration of their father and mother. It was agreed that a faculty for same be applied for forthwith. Mrs Rylands proposed, and Mr F F Evans seconded a grateful vote of thanks to the family from the vestry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>LY MM ÜBBAN COUNCIL</head>
        <p>LY MM ÜBBAN COUNCIL</p>
        <p>ANNUAL M ETING-A DEADLOCK.</p>
        <p>A deadlock of an extraordinary character wag encounter.d and fortunately overcome at the annual n,eeting of the Lymm Urban District Council on fuesday evening, when the members assembled had some difficulty in getting a response to their call for a chairman for the ensuing year. Mr W Mullard (clerk) 'resided and there were also present :—The Rev G 13 'hurston, Messrs 0 G Smith, T H Warburton, W G roves, J Holt, R H Gotol ed, Davies, S Lockyer, W D Mort (surve)or) and W L Donaldson (gas lager). A returning officer, the Clerk reported that the re • elections bad resulted in the return of Messrs T .rarburton (Heatley Wards, RII Gotobed (Lymm Ward), and J H Holt (Booth's Hill Ward). All three gentlemen were retiring members. RETIRING CHAIRMAN THANKED.</p>
        <p>pr Thirst Gefives, maiiiger' As r recent NV,</p>
        <p>In proposing a vote of thanks to the late chairman (Mr C U Smith), Mr Groves said be conducted the business of the Council in a highly creditable manner and no member of the Council had, in his opinion,tilled the office cUth greater honour. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>Mr. Davies, in seconding said Mr Smith had disposed of a large amount of important work in connection with the council and their best thanks were due to him. (Hear, hear). Mr Gototed bad great pleasure in supporting the motion and mentioned that Mr Smith was the only chairman he bad known since he had been a member of council, that was for three years, and everything had been carried out in a satisfactory manner. They owed hintrt deep debt of gratitude. (Hear, hear). In response Mr Smith said he did not think be was conceited enough to think that he deserved all which had been said of him. He was conscious of his defects but he claimed with all modesty that he had endeavoured to keep one object before him and that was the public welfare. That was what they were all there for. (Hear, hear.) So long as as he was on the Council he felt it was his duty to do all he could to further the best interests of Lymm. (Hear, hear.) Mr lockyer, in proposing Mr Warbert m as chair- man,said that gentlemin had been the deputy chairman for three years and a member of the Council for nine years. There was no seconder to this proposition and after a short silence the Clerk called for another nomination, whereupon Mr Smith proposed Mr Groves. No words of his were needed to recommend that gentleman to Vie' Council. He efts their oldest member and keen on Council work generally and was a wonderfully well- informed pram ical man of business. Further be possessed qualifications which were not present in some of uhem namely that be had unlimited leisure to devote to the affairs of the Council. (Hear, bear). • In seconding the proposition the Rectar Said there e.ere other retsons why Mr Groves should occupy the chair. He was getting old and they c..uld not expect the same vigt or he had shown in times past to last for much longer. With his great knowledge of the district and the zeal he had manifested not only as a member of the Commit but on many important works, it would be a fitting tribute to him to elect him once more as their chairman. (Hear, hear). Mr Groves was extremely grateful to the Council for the compliment which had been paid him but he had always held the opinion that the office ought to go r“und. He must therefore decline to accept the offer. In spite of Mr Daviesie pressure, Mr Groves still adhered to his decision.</p>
        <p>Another silence followed after which Mr Davies propozed the Rector as chairman and Mr Smith seconded, but the rev, gentleman also refused to accept the office. His time was fully occupied and it was on that ground that he declined the offer. Mr Warburton sild the minds of the members had been made up before they had entered the Council Chamber. This charge was repudiated by several members. Alter another puss a proposition was made to the effect that the Council should settle the matter in camera. This proposition was carried and the press and public then retired. For nearly an hour the Council deliberated in private and when the public were re-admitted Mr Smith proposed the election of Mr G ovei as chairman.</p>
        <p>Mr Davies seconded and the proposition was unani- mously carried. Mr Groves said be accepted the office with some reluctance, but he was plessed the appointment was unanimous.</p>
        <p>VICE-CHAIRMAN. Mr. Warburton WEIS re-elected as vice-chairman on the proposition of the rector, seconded by Mr. Lockyer Mr. Warburton said he was not anxious for the honour which be bad had for several years—the honour of sitting at the end of the table. Mr. Groves : You know one of the conditions on</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
