      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Dr. Williams'</head>
        <p>Dr. Williams'</p>
        <p>which I accepted the office of chairman was that you were to be the vice-chairman. Tho resolution was then put to the meeting and heartily carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>COMMITTEES.</head>
        <p>THE COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>It was decreed that the monthly meet'ng of the Council he held as previously, on the last Thursday of the month at 7.30. The chairmen and vice-chairmen of the committees were then elected as follows : Roads Mr Davies (chairman), Mr Lockver (vice-chairman) Sanitary and Sewage Farm ; Rev E B Thurston (chair- man), 1-lousing and Town Planning; Mr G L \Wilford (chairman) Building and Fire Engine ; Mr Holt (chair- man), Gas ; Mr Gotohed (chairman), Mr Smith (vice- chairman), Finance ; Mr Smith (chairman), General Purposes ; Mr Groves (chairman), and Mr Warburton (vice-chairman). SALARY OF THE SANITARY I INSPECTOR INCREASED. The Rev G B Thurston moved and Mr Warburton seconded that the salary of the Sanitary Inspector be increased from .V,O to £7O per year. The motion was carried unanimously and Mr Mort in returning thanks said he would always do his best to promote the interests of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>ATTENDANCE CF THE PUBLIC.</head>
        <p>THE ATTENDANCE CF THE PUBLIC.</p>
        <p>Mr Smith said he was pleased to see so many rate- payers present taking an interest in the proceedings of the council. Ile hoped they would go away feeling grateful to those %km spent their time in conducting public business. 'the Council then resolved itself into committee to consider the general district rats and a letter and con- tract re waterworks. lINATLEY B P. BOY SCOUTS ANNUAL CONCERT.—The Heatley troop of the B P. Boy Scouts held their second annual concert in the Lymm Drill Hall on Saturd,y night. Despite the fact that two of the principal artistes were unable to fulfil their engagement the large audience assembled showed their appreciation of the committee's eff, rts to provide an acceptable pro- gramme by hearty and frequent applause The open- ing item was a solo y Mr J H Brazendale (tenor) entitled" The young Royalist." This was followed by a dance by Miss Jackson,who though young, is an adept of the terpsichorean art ; she gracefully acceded to the request for an encore. Master F. Ingham's splendid enunciation of his contribution " The village Blacksmith " fully compensated for any objections that may have been advanced against the singing of such a hackneyed piece,and deserved the hearty applause given Was Fay Mallender was a successful exponent of magic and mystery, her tricks of the magic balls, and the handkerchiefs and parasol, being specially good. The committee were fortunate in securing, at the lest minute, Miss Jessie Reid, whose musical monologues were much enjoyed. Mr J Webb, jun., regretted that he had to announce another disappointment, Mr Scott Joynt having just telegraphed his inability to come owing to a very bad cold. Mr J H Brazendale sang another solo " The animals' band " and merited the hearty applause accorded him. A hornpipe dance by Miss Jackson and Master Percival was given, and repeated, at the request of the audience. Miss klallender gave another exhibition of mystery and magic. Her card tr:cks, aid magic host items being well applauded. An Indian danc3 by Master C Bridge, in full war paint, was well received Nod an encore was demanded and granted. Mr J H Brazendale brought the first part of the programme to a close with his popular song " Give rue dat water melon." Ile kindly responded to the requests for an encore. After the interval a scout play called "How a boy became a scout" was admirably acted, the various characters being well performed Mr T Kay and Mrs Sharp, causing much merriment.. The varioue scenes in the story of the boy were closely watched and fully deserved the enthusiastic applause given, which was renewed when, after repeated calls for the" Author," Mr T Nash, the respected scout master appeared on the stage. We hope to be- favoured with another of his original productions at the next annual concert. Miss Field gave another musical monologue while the stage was being cleared for the final part of the evening's entertainment. lhis took the form of camp scenes in which sounding the reveille, drill, boxing, single sticks, semaphore and morse signalling, ambulance aid in the mimic explosion, which by the way, caused quite a laughable commotion among the audience, handbell ringing, song by Mr F Cowell', chorus by the scouts, march past and salute, all of which %%ere worthily executed. The singing of the National Anthem brought the concert to a close. Miss Field and Mr J Webb, jun., were proficient accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LORD WOLMER'S BIRTHDAY.</head>
        <p>LORD WOLMER'S BIRTHDAY.</p>
        <p>Viscount—Wolmer. celebrated --hiti-i;eni.;fifth birth- day on Monday. The eldest son and heir of the Earl Lula Countess of Selborne, he uses a courtesy title derived from the viscounty created, together with the earldom, 30 years ago for his grandfather, who had been first raised to the peerage 10 years before as Baron Se'borne. He began his political career earlier than did his father and grandfather, entering the House of Commons in December, 1910, as member for the Newton Division. In the previous June, a few days after his parents' return from South Africa, Lord Wolmer married the Hon. Grace Ridley, sister of Viscount Ridley. They have a daughter who kept her first anniversary last month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Pink Pills.</head>
        <p>Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>Appleton Thorn</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL TREAT.—This treat which had been postponed from Christmas, took place in St. Cross Schoolroom on Friday evening and proved highly enjoy- able. Forty-seven children and ten adults Eat down to an excellent tea,and others came in later. Among these present were Mrs Marsh and children, Miss Penney, Miss Foster, the Rural Dean (the Rev end lion C Cross!, the Vicar and Mrs Evans, the teachers, the Misses Beswick, Evans, Garner and Wright, the organist and Mrs Sewell, aud several of the parents. Each child received a chocolate Easter egg, and after tea games were engaged in. Then there waa the presenta- tion of prizes, which on this occasion were very numerous and handsome, as the attendance had been so excellent. Several scholars never missed a single time last year, and the senior girl, Hannah Warburton, never lost a mark for conduct or lessons. Mr Cross then addressed the children, and told them to treasure their Sunday Sunday lessonS, as they might remember some of them all their lives, and be led the very hearty clapping for the teachers. Finally, the Vicar gave a magic lantern entertainment with coloured slides and descriptions, beginning with the life of Christ. Then there were s%enes in Queen Victoria's reign, and theta the story o'f Cinderella, uhich the Vicar explained came from France in the 17th century, and through a. mis-translation glass slippef was substituted for fur- slipper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WIDNES FOOTBALL CLUB AFFAIRS. SUPPORTERS VOTE CONFIDENCE IN THE CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Friday night there was a good crowd of football enthusiasts at the Empire Rink, Widnes, called by the Widnes Northern Union Club committee, to further dis- cuss the Tilley incident. It will be remembered that about twenty members of the club got up a petition asking the committee to push forward negotiations to secure Jim Tulle, from Warrington. Tilley was trans- ferred to the Wirepullers six seasons ago for the sum of £7O, and after that period of active work he was placed on the transfer list at a fee of £EO. At the public meeting held recently a resolution was passed declaring it advisable to secure Tilley's transfer at a maximum figure of £3O. The committee resented this resolution, and threatened to resign. Friday night's meeting was presided over by Councillor Clarke. Mr. Smith, reviewing the policy of the com- mittee, referred the members to its successful working In the face of the majority at the last meeting, he begged to tender the resignation of the whole ot the committee. The attitude the committee had adopted had not been in any childnh or unsporLrca 'like spirit or out of pique. It was upon Tilley's own initiative that he west to Warrington, for, in the first instance, his transfer was refused, and subsA quently granted. Recently Warrington had made an offer of £3O, but not one of the committee would put his hand to the sign - ing of the oheque. Mr. Burrill moved " thtt the resolntion passed at the general meeting held on Mach 26th be rescinded, and that the members record their complete confidence , in the committee, and also to express a strong hope that in the best interests of the club the present officials will continue in office." The resolution was carried with only six dissentients. RUNCORN'S NEW CIVIC CHIEF. The Runcara Urban Council on Thursday night elected Mr. Rebert U. Posnett chairman. Mr. S. Taylor, who bad occupied the position a record number of times, declined re-electi?n, and nominated his successor. Mr. Posnett is a Literal, cud the leader of the Conservatives (Mr. John Little!), seconding the nomination, spoke warmly of the ability rad considera- tion of Mr. Poseett, from whom, he said, every member could depend upon receiving fair play. Mr. Posnett, in reply, alluded to the growing feeling in the town that the day of party strife and sectarian squabbling v.a3 over, for ever it was hoped,nnd that the prospects were those .of harmony and peace. The Council bad serious difficulties to face in the nest twelve months, but a united front would pull them through end pot the town in a better position. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Taylor was elected vice-chairman.</p>
        <p>MATRIMONIAL DISCORD. On Tuesday the Runcorn magistrates (Dr. M'Dougall and Mr. E. Imison) gave their decision in a case heprd a fortnight ago, in which Mrs. Miles summoned her husband, William Miles a svell-known Runcorn trades- man, for desertion. The woman had previously eummoned her husband for petsistent cruelty, but the case was dismissed. Mrs. Miles went to her husband's house, and it was alleged that while she was there the. furniture was taken outiof the house and she was left in darkness the gas having been turned off at the meter. She left the house that night at 11 45 by getting out of the window. The magistrates now dismissed the case on the ground that cohabitation bad ceased by the adverse action of the wife, and further, the return to cohabitation on the part of the wife was not bons-fide, The Bench agreed to state a case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <p>THE CLOCKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker and Mr. Rylands.</p>
        <p>Fir,—Referring to Mr. Parker's interesting letter in your issue of Saturday week, it might be instructive if Mr. Parker could favour me with a reply i to the follow- ing questions, whieh no doubt he can n a very few words. 1. What advantage in this particular industry does he conceive Germany possesses which makes her economically better suited for the manufacture of clocks than Great Britain? 2. How is it that German manufacturers, although handicapped by very high protective tariffs, and the consequent increased cost of production, are able to export cheap clocks in apparently such vast quantities to this country ? 3. Having regard to the well-known large amount of capital available in Great Britain, does Mr Parker suggest that any industry for which this country is better suited would be lacking supplies of capital, if any capital were attracted to the clock industry as the result of a protective tariff? 1. If Mr. Parker is correct that an import &amp;impels a corresponding export, which incidentally I der y the exact opposite being in my opinion the truth— would it be economically impossible that the importa- tion of foreign clocks might be balanced by the export of capital, or in o ber words by the sale of some foreign securities instead of by the export of goods which have given employment to our workpeople ? 5. Would it not be of greater advantage to this country if, instead of importing clocks, we imported foodstuffs, wool, and other necessaries of life to a similar value for the supply of the requirements of the working population engaged iu the manufacture of such clocks in Great Britain ?—Yours faithfully, W. PETER RYLANDS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am pleased to see Mr. Itylands' letter on this question. It is a striking contrast to the matter of some of the letters, and manners of some of the writers in the column in which my name is prominent. It is difficult in a few words to answer any questions raising so many important issues, but I will do my best. Reply to question 1. I know of no economic advan- tage Germany possesses to aid her in the manufacture of clocks, but it would be extraordinary if such a people did not specialize and become successful in some trades. A Lancashire man knowing what we have done ia cotton and other trades should feel no surprise at this, unless he claims for Englishmen a monopoly of brains, skill, and enterprise. 11. The Protective tariffs of Geimany do not directly hamper the manufacture of clocks. The raw material for making a clock forms a very small part of its value ; it is not imparted and therefore the cost of clocks is not directly affected by duties. 111. No, Ido not suggest this for a moment. IV. Mr. Rylands asks " Would it be economically impossible that the imp-rtation of clocks might be balanced by the export of capital, or in other words by the sale of some foreign securities ?" Certainly it would be possible by the latter, but not by the former. There is a confusion here, the two methods are not alternative. The export of capital, that is I presume for investment abroad, would have exactly the opposite effect to that of the sale of foreign securities. Ido not understand this coming from Mr. Rylands. Surely he is aware that when capital is exported it is not sent abroad in English eovereigns but in English goodo. Money plays little or no part in international exchange, it facilitates this exchange as a measure not as a means. The clock maker in the Black Forest would have no 1189 for English sovereigns, nor would the German bankers. It is true there is a limited amount of movement in British gold. Some countries at special seasons, such as harvest time, buy English gold to maintain their reserve when all their own coinage is required for facilitating exchanges in their own country. Capital being exported in goods in this case we should not only have to export goods to pay for the German clocks, but also goods to the value cf the capital exported which Mr. Rylands has in mind. Mr. Rylands has got the thing wrong way round. It would be right for him to say that the importation of foreign clocks might be balanced by the import of capital, not the export. In other words if a German took up a British investment, his capital would have be imported, and the importation of clocks might facilitate this, and no payment in British goods would be required, just as in the reverse, an exportation of capital would be effected by an export of Blitish goods which would call for no foreign import to balance. Mr. Rylands is right when he says that the clocks might be balanced by the sale of some foreign security by a British holder. But the British holder before he can sell must first buy, Own, and pay for, the security in question. That is, when he originally purchased his security, and exported his capital to pay for it the value went abroad in British goods, made by British workers. We come back, therefore, to the same principle that goods pay for goods. The trans- ference of capital does not abrogate this principle of exchange, but only temporarily disturbs its action by delay or acceleration. My contention is the same as stated in a Special Blue Book prepared by the Board of Trade on British and Foreign Trade, which says " of the whole of the commeicial and financial transactions between any country and the outside world which cover a period of years, though tot necessarily within the limits of any single year, must balance one another : " this balance is effected through the variations (f the rates of exchange, that is in the variations in the relative values of the money measure emplsyed by different countries. This alters the relative value of the goods so measured and facilities or compels exports and imports to right the balance V The fifth question I quote as it is the most important. Mr. Rylands asks, " Would it not be of greater advantage to this country if, instead of 'repotting clocks, we imported foodstuffs, wool, and other pecessaries of life of a similar value for the supply of the requirements of a working population engaged in the manufacture cf such clocks in Great Britain ? " Does Mr. Rylands imply by this question that he would propose that fcodstuffs, etc., should come into this country free ? If so he is at variance with what we have understood Tariff Reform to stand for, for the last ten years. Mr. Dollar Law a few days ago told the Irish people that they would be the greatest beneficiaries under Tariff Reform. These benefits could only come to Ireland through a tax on foodstuffs raising the price of the agricultural products, enrich- ing the Irish farmer at the expense of the English consumer. One of the Tariff Reform leaflets puts it very clearly as follows : . " SAINUCIO!s; FOR ERIN." "Ireland will secure both a higher price for all she sells in English markets by being pro- tected from competition with America, Denmark, and France, and she will pay less for all she purchases,the duties having been largely rsduced. Let us make a rough budget of the profit under the new fiscal departure for the Irish farmer. First there is the proposed duty on foreign wheat of 25., and the proat represented by the advance in price in the English market will be Gs. an acre. All quite plain except why Ireland will "pay less for all she purchases." If Tariff Reform according to Mr.Rylands brought foodstuffs from abroad instead of clocks, where would the promised benefit come from for the Irish or English farmer to compensate him for the extra he would have to pay for clocks and other manufactured articles? Mr. By lands' suggestion coupled with Mr. Bonar Law's statement and th 3 T. R. Leaflets circulated in Ireland, lays his cause open to the complaint which with good reason has been made against Tariff Reformers. that they advocate a - duty on foodstuffs for the purpose of raising prices in localities where focdstufft are produced, and are silent about the tax on focd- stuffs, or state it will not raise the price, where their audiences or readers are consumers of foodstuffs and not producers. Before we can get to close quarters we must know where Mr. Rylands stands. his rptestion suggests that imports of free foodstuffs should take the place of taxed clocks. Is this Mr. Rylands' position? I contend it would not be advantages us by tariffs to increase our imposts of foodstuffs to make up for manufactured articles excluded by duties. By checking manufactures by tariffs it would place at a disadvantage our focd producers, artificially depressing what they sell, and artificially raising the price of what they buy, thus favouring one class at the ext ense of another—the constant result of all tariff legislation. This folly is what is now called Tariff Reform , • CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, April lab, 1912.</p>
        <p>• . , . - i 1 i 4'; pi11.6,,dv to the- Rig 11L1 • - • . EDITOR,:- ~- ~..,.:4,_. -----, _ ~</p>
      </div>
