      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Disendowment.</head>
        <p>Disendowment.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Let me first acknowledge the kindly tone o Mr. Jarman's letter. At the same time I must say that I do not consider, and I believe that none of your readers will c,insider, that he has answered the ques- tions which I put to him. he has stated the basis on which be asked the question quoted by me and he has also given his opinions on certain points. These I did not ask for, and, if I may say so without offence, I am cot interested in Mr. Jarman's private views. We all have cur peculiarities. When Mr. Jarman speaks publicly for Nonconformists, as he apparently did at St ckton Heath, he should be prepared to give the Nonconformist reasons and not his own indi- vidual ones for his statements. It is a peculiarity of Mr Jarman th.it he dues " cot like the system which ties down gifts for the propagation of doctrines ages ! after the givers are dead in spite of ever increasing light and knowledge." This is certainly not the view of Nonconformists in general, for they ire constantly receiving gifts for the purpose mentioned by Mr. Jarman " in spite of ever increasing light and know- ledge." The question in connec ion with the Dier.- dowment proposal is: —Why slit uld the Endowments of the Church be treated differently f out the endowments of Nonconformity ? Tvrentv-five years' uninterrupted possession under the Act of 1811 secures dissenters in the possession of thtir pr .perty. On what grcunds therefore should the tturcti he deprived of her property which she has enjdyed prior to the year 1662 ? Furthermore, will the catise of Christianity be promoted in crippling Vie most powerful ieligious agent in the land by diverting to secular uses property devoted to and never more usefully emplo3 ed in the spread of the Christian faith ? This is the point which should appeal to the hearts and col st is ces of really Christian men. And those nr n are surely most entitled to be classed as Chrisian who look at this matter not with the limited and palsied view of the petty party poitician but with the Pauline view. of one who is actuated by chain'', is kind, not envious and thinketh no evil.</p>
        <p>The endowments f the Church w, re given to each individual parish f r all the i..hab tints in the parish who care to receive h.-r ministrati s. There are those who have refus.d, or sail I say pre fel rd not, to receive her ministrations. M. Jam ii is one. Very well. But there are othf rs who pref r to r.ceive the ministrations of the Church. lam ne What right has Mr. Jarman to say to me that bocau•e he will not theret,re I shall not s'•are in the benefactions and benefits which his forefathers at.d mioe provide.l both for him and for me ? is this toleration ? Even the Prodigal Sun did not demand the disestablishment of his elder brother.</p>
        <p>As regards the olive branch and the nettle, I fail to recognize either of these products of vegetation in Mr Jarman's speech or in my letter. 0 ire branches are certainly not required in this matter. Justice and equity are required. The endowntonts of the Church are either her property or they are not. She has possessed them for hundreds of years and the onus is upon those who seek to deprive her of them, to show that she is not entitled t.-) retain them.—Yours truly, _</p>
        <p>H. J. WESTBROOK,</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath, 19 h Ap:il, 1012.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Wire Tangle.</head>
        <p>The Wire Tangle.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt was not without a shock of Burp' ise that I read the letter of Councillor J. Broadhurst in your contemporary of Saturday last. That " Workman Jim" has written it is surely no excuse for publishing such an uncalled-for personal attack upon one of your correspondents. In my letter to you, sir, I put a question to Mr. Harold Smith, M P., bearing on a statement which he has made on /Imre than one occasion, and I had hopes, seeing that it was of some local public interest, that he would reply. Instead, " Workman Jim," a member of the local Town Council, pro- ceeds to put me in my proper place ; to charge me with dishonest motives, to introduce my wife into the discussion, and to venture finally, in a most insolent manner, to insinuate that the " son of an ironworker " should not dare so much as to attempt to address the Member for Warrington. There is not one single word in his letter which deals with the subject matter. If such a discredi'able piece ef writing is to be taken as a simple of" the new Con- servatism," which he is evidently out to promulgate, then lam afraid that all honest men will be more disgusted than ever with the piffle that passes for politics. , I cannot understand Councillor Broadhurst'e attitude. There was a time when be went out of his way to express his friendliness towards me; in fact, when I had the temerity some years ago to put up for the position of elective auditor for the Borough be was pleased to support my nomination. If he now goes out ot hie way to attack me, I suppose I ought not to complain. Neither should lif he did not h t below the belt.</p>
        <p>In the first place he sees " sarcasm" in my letter, although it is not of my making but of his under- standing He then states : "Now let us examine this ' detached gentleman's credentials' and ask Who is Edmund Osborne? ' Echo answers: 'Past President Junior Liberal Association,' 'Special contributor to the local Radizal Press,' and 'Chief supporter of Liberal candidate on the Board of Guardians at the last election.' "</p>
        <p>An echo usually "repeats," but this one "answers" questions of his own fai tin g in his own way, and doubtless he thinks they ate sufficient to repudiate my assertion that lam not a " detached " gentleman. We shall see. _</p>
        <p>I arrt no longer President of the Junior Liberal Association. I resigned " because I felt whilst the society was formed for purely educational purposes— for the discussion of political and social problems yet it was dubbed 'Liberal.' I thought it was unfair to invite young men to bind themselves to a political party before they really understood what that party stood for; and I recollect it was only on the assurance that it was knowledge and not party tactics the members were seeking that I consented, in the first instance, to become a member." (Extract from a paper I delivered before the society). I am not a special conttibutor to the local Radical Press. . .</p>
        <p>Tarn not a member of any other boa political organization. I was not the chief supporter of the Liberal candiddte on the Board of Guardians at the last Election. As to that insinuation I feel ashamed to think that even " Workman Jim " would try deliberately to mislead your readers in such a cowardly way. He knows full well that the only candidate I supported, and only half-heartedly at that, as I knew she bad already too much work on her hands, was my wife. Surely he might have refrained, in common courtesy and decency, front such a contemptikle piece of priggishness. And lastly, perhaps " Workman Jim" will say what he means to convey by the innuendo that I am the "son of an ironworker.' Does he think that I can know any more or lees about econcmics because I happen to be the son of an ironworker; or does he think that I being the son of an ironworker, must confine all my correspondbnce to the sons of ironworkers? Or rather does he not imagine that I, being the son of an ironworker, ought to be ashamed of myself for daring to address a ques- tion to the Member for Warrington ? In conclusion, may I be permitted, as a " detached " gentleman (the son of an ironworker if you like, for I am not asbamed of the fact), to ask that the question I put to Mr. Harold Smith, M P., may he answered honestly, and that no further attempts be made to shelve the subject by the coarse and irrelevant insinuations of ignorant and peevish persons.— Yours truly, EDMUND OSBORNE, 22, Bold-street, Warrington. ' 18th April, 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Appeals to Force Lead to Barbarism?</head>
        <p>Appeals to Force Lead to Barbarism?</p>
        <p>Sir,—On reading the hrticle which appeared under the above heading in your paper of last week, I was surprised to find that whilst Mr. C. E. Parker criti- cised the strike as a weapon for the settling pf industrial disputes, he offered us no better remedy. Some of the observations and also the conclusions arrived at, gave one seriously to think that Mr, Parker bad not gone very deep into matter before taking up his pen. For one to assert that a strike, violent or peaceful, of no matter what dimensions, wculli lead back to.barbarism, is certainly to display an ignorance of the laws of social progress and the forces which were instrumental in enabling civilized people to leave the stage of barbarism. The underlying motive of Mr. Parker's article is to belit tie the strike weapon or force, and to impress the public with the idea that labour is impotent con- sidered in tho light of capital. It is well for one to remember when decrying force as a necessary agent in social progress that the policeman's baton, and incidentally the bayonet of the soldier, are the only means of enforcing present-day legal obligations upon the people. Remove force, and law would crumble.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>Th-k Exadainer, Saturday, April, 20, 1912.</head>
        <p>Th-k Exadainer, Saturday, April, 20, 1912.</p>
        <p>Mr Parker says that if the masters had used their staying powers to the full, the men would have been beaten. lu reply let me inform Mr Parker that if Labour was conscious of its power and used all its resources Labour would be the master. _</p>
        <p>" Property has proved itself invulnerable" Why ? If the mines had stopped all pumping operations immediately they laid down tools, capital would have depreciated. Would property have been then invulnerable? The worker has too much tespect for property; but he is learning. I agree with Mr. Parker, all this leaves the problem unsolved and in ieply to his question " Is the present relative reWatd of laud, capital and labour equitable," I say no; and further that it is the growing inequality of these relatioos which is at the root of these industrial conflicts. What is more there can be no equity between them ; one thrives at the expense of the other. Capital is the vearasite of labour, not its co-opetator. Labour is the source of all wealth and equity can only be obtained when labour receives the full return cf its product. Between labour and capital there is a conflict which can only end by the extermination of capitalism. Mr. Parker suggests that the national progress can be controlled by moral consider'ations I invite Mr. Mr Parker to look at the condition of the working cla,s t )-day, ju lged in the light of the productivity of lab ,ur ;to wit, e3s the hysicd degeneracy of our town dwellers, the insanitary conditions of the factory and home life.Tuese poor people are incapable of pro- viding for a month's cessalon from work without having to apre el to charity. Then take a look at the insolent disp'ay of lumuty in suburbia, c matrast the dwellings of a Wigan miner with the Mddle class horn's of LyiLain, and then tell Us what inflaence mor.lity cmn have on these great economic questions. It would be interesting t e know, seeing that Mr. Parker asks 'ls th 4 relatim between Capital and Labour to remain constant ?" if he thinks it possible for it to remain constant, and if not, by what means the relati ns are to be arijusted. CHAS. DLIKES.</p>
        <p>Duqrlonald avenu., Stockton 11,-ath, Wdrrington, 181.11 April, 1912</p>
        <p>days were spebt in their native town. Warrington having shown in so many similar ways its loyal and affectionate reg*.rd for its sons and dsughters, is worthy of all praise and is a</p>
        <p>BRIGHT EXAMPLE TO OTHER TOWNS. May I to-day congratulate the town on possessing the beautiful medallion, designed and modelled by Mr, Allen, which I have had the honour to unveil." Continuing, Mrs. RIDGWAY said it was perhaps difficult to picture our country as it was in the beginning and the early part of the 18th century. It was a time of indifference to religion—so much so that Montinesquine coming ct this time to England, said " Everyone laughs if one talks of religion in the higher circles. ' In politic', too, it was a time of drowsy inaction. The poor were brutal and ignorant. Schools there were none, save the Grammar Schools of Edward and Elizabeth, and there was absolutely no religious or moral training. It was a time of darkness. Happily the dawn was at hand and already breaking over some parts of the laud, despite the callousness and misery. The darkness grew gradually less dense, and a religious revival, started by a small knot of Oxford students, swept in gro it waves over the country. It was at the dawn of a brighter day, when wisdom was looking shyly from her hiding place and the spirit of philanthropy hovered near' bringing with the movement of its wings a breath of freedom, that our poeteia was born in the Leicestershire village of Kinworth-llarcourt on Juna 20th, 1743. The daughter of Dr Aikin, a Nonconformist minister, a very learned classical scholar, Anna Letitia, called after some relation of high degree on her mother's side, grew up to exceptional cirzunistances. In that secluded a.d tranquil !p at Nancy, as she was called in child- hood, spent her 30ung girlhood, thriving physi- cally and mentally and knowing little of the outer world and its striving after better things. It was the beginning of ane v day, not only ia religion but in art and Ikerata.re. There were Fielding and Ilogarth— coarse in expression but strong and vie ‘rous and tend- ing towards moral expanAon. The dull imactivity, the sleepy indiff ,rence was aroused; it was a time of expectation. The little child aho was to gain so grant a reputation in the land came just at the right moment. How remarkable a child she was, we learned from a letter written by her m Aber many years after- wards apropcs of her grandchildren whom she thought backward. We were tad that" Nancy at two years old could read stories from her wise book " and that " long before she tt!ained the age of three she could read as well its st wom-n.' Fr )m her infancy she e j ye] the ex•raord n .ry advantages of a highly cultured and li'e ary environment. We were told that Dr. Doddridge for soma years was an inmate of her father's hoase. Mrs Aikin, a clever and charm- ing woman, was the child's principal instructress, and no doubt added her entreaty when the young girl pleaded with her father to he taught Latin and Greek. She was granted her wish but not without protest, for there was a strong prejudice against teaching girls more than w-,s necessary for their domestic duties. In after years, when urged by a friend, Ars. Montague, to establish a ladies' college, Mn. Barbauld gave as one . of her raas D 3 for refusal that A GIRL'S BEST EDUCATION WAS OBTAINED AT HOME. , Although Dr. Aikin was the heal of a boys' school, so careful was the mother to preserve the gentle bearing of her attractive childhood, that Mrs. Barbauld always ascribed the shyness and bashfulness which ahe felt iu society to the strictness and seclusion of her upbringing. "We can imagine" continued Mrs. Ridgway "the ' pleasure of the fifteen year old girl when Dr. Aikin decided to accept the invitation of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>GREAT AUTHORESS.</head>
        <p>A GREAT AUTHORESS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S TRIBUFE TO MRS. BARBAULD.</p>
        <p>TABLET UNVEILED.</p>
        <p>Honour was done to the memory of a great Warrington authosess on Thursday afternoon when a tablet erected by the Warrington Societe t 3 mark the site of the h. use in Dial-street wheie kris. Barbartld (or Anna Letitia Aikin, as she was then) lived from 1758 t 1774, was unveiled by Mrs. T. J. Ridgway, of Wildersmoor, Lymm. Although outside a compara- tively lim ted circle the name of Mrs. Barbauld does not count for much in these days, there was a time when she was ore tithe best-known figures in Engliet literature —in fact Wordsworth called her "the first of cur literary womrn " and envied her one of her shotter poems which, as Mrs. Ridgway pointed but, his become immortal. Some of Mrs. Barbauld's happiest days were spent in Warrington. Her con- nection with the town began in 17.58, when her father, the Rev. Dr. John Aikin, Newt:to classic il tutor at the Warrington Academy, and lasted un it her marriage in 1771.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>-WARRINOTON ACADEMY</head>
        <p>-WARRINOTON ACADEMY</p>
        <p>In 1754 to becoMe-ii;-c-id-s-siCaliti7o-rt-itris very pleasant to think that the fifteen following years were probably the happiest of her life and certainly the most brilliant. The alvent of this clever fam ly in the old town was truly an event, and among the polished and literary circle which then existed Anna Letitia Aikin took her place and became thriugh ber talent, charm and beauty, greltly admired and beloved. It wan not, however, until 1773 that she published her first work, persuaded by her brother, Dr. Alain, a medical mln,and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>UNVEILING THE TABLET TO MRS. BARBAUI:D IN HOWARD</head>
        <p>UNVEILING THE TABLET TO MRS. BARBAUI:D IN HOWARD</p>
        <p>It was originally intended that the unveiling of the tablet should take plan a last year in commemoration of the publication of Mrs. Barbauld's last poem, "Eighteen Hundred and Eleven," —a striking forecast of Britain's decline and fall, which had a very hostile reception from the reviewers. A postponement was however, found necessary, but that did not in any degree lessen the interest which was taken in the ceremony. The tablet, it is interesting to nbte, is the fifth which the society has erected to mark places of historic interest in the borough. The others have been placed on the Old Academy, on the site of the houses where Dr Joseph Priestly, the scientist, and John Howard, the prison refoimer, lived, and on the site of the old Roman station at Wilderspool. The memorial to Ms. Barbauld is the design of Mr. E. B. Allen, a student at the Wa,rington School of Art. It is of bronze and bears a medallion portrait of the authoress, together with the inscription :—" Site of the residence of Anna Ltetita Aikin (Mrs. Barbauld) 1758-1774. This tablet was erected by the Warrington Society, 1011." Mr. John Fairhurst presided at the ceremony and amongst others present were : Mrs Ridgway, Alder- man and Mrs A Bennett, Master Bennett, Council! n• land Mrs C Jackson Holmes, Councillor Henshall, Mrs R Garnett, Mr and Mrs G W A Brown, Messra•H Stuart Page, J Richardson, jun.,C M.edeley, C Mawoon, E J Hall, A Mounfield, J Longland, E Wharton, W H Sherburn, J Gittens, Brander and Scotson. Another interested spectator was Shiv Ram, a Hindoo, who is engaged in religious work in this c mntry: Mr. JOHN FAIRHURST, who presided, raid they were met to do honour to one of Warrington's noblest women —Mrs. Barbauld —who at ote time lived at that house. She came to Warrington when she was fifteen years of age. Her father was the principal of the Warrington Academy, which was then a well known college. Mrs. Barbauld was a great writer of poems, hymns and hooks, and some other works were written during her residence in that house. She was well known in the literary world and the popu'arity of her writings in her day was shown by Vie fact that one poem was in such demand that it reached is fourth edition in one year. It was intended that the tablet should be unveiled in 1911, because that year marked the centenary of the publication of Mrs. Barbauld's last great work. Unfortunately Mn. Ridgway was away and therefore the ceremony had to be poitponed. Mrs. Ridgway was known to many of those present as a writer of very admirable books and it was very fitting that she spould unveil the tablet. (Hear, hear.) He (Mr. Fairhurst) hal many reminiscences of that house. It was a rather strange coincidence that in his younger days he attended school there. He did not for one morbent suggest that any portion of the mantle of Dr. Aikin fell upon him— (litughter)—bnt he remembered other things that did—(laughter)—and probably be was no worse for the little incidents that happened there. (Laughter and applause). Mrs. RtionwAy, in unveiling the tablet, said: In reverent memory and with s' ncere admiration I unveil this tablet, on behalf of the Warrington Society, to the memory of a great writer, a great poetess, a great and good woman—Anna 1-53 titia Barbauld. (Applause). AT THE ACADEMY. --- Mrs. Ridgway's Eloquent Tribute. The brief ceremony over, the company walked io the Old Academy, where an interesting meeting was held under the presidency of Mr. Feirhurst. Mrs Elmo WAY delivered an eloquent estimate of the life and work of Mrs. Barbauld. " Our gathering to•day in this town of many memories "she said "is an act of love and reverence which we, with glad hearts, right willingly pay, with sincere admiration. We woull yet again lay a laurel wreath at the feet of a great writer and a noble woman—Anna Ltetitia Barbauld who, coming to Warrington at the zenith of her youth and' beauty, brought to it such grace and charm that the whole town appears to have become her devoted lover. Nearly one hundred years after her death does this devoted lover remember the enthusiasm her personality evoked, the delight of her poems, the cultured atmosphere she created, and would once more offer the fair Sybil a gift, one not likely soon to fade away, one upon which our children's children may gaze with wonder and love, having been taught to revere and cherish the memory of this great and good woman whose bappie</p>
        <p>CE.</p>
        <p>an unprecedented success was hers, four editions being called for within the year. In the same year was published Miscellaneous pieces' by the brother and sister. It was, however, long before this that our poetess discovered her gift of song and in her poem The invitation' there are many pleasant allusions to the Academy—words of filial affection. lhe college is spoken of as the nursery of men for future years' and of the time spent within its walls she says : " 0 golden days! 0 bright unvalued hours! What bliss, did ye but know, that bliss were yours ! " Mrs. RIDG WAY then touched upon the many staunch and lifelong friendships the authoress formed at Warrington which at that time held high its head for men of world-renown—scholars, inventors and philosophers—dwelt in its quaint and picturesque streets, and learning flourished. The celebrated Dr. Priestley was a tutor at the college, and soon became the friend and remained to the end of his life a devoted admirer of the poetess. Dr Er,field, the Rev. Edward Owen, William Eyres, NVilliam Rlscoe and John Howard—the two latter frequent visitors to the town —soon beeame worshippers at the feet of genius and not less so because it was enshrined in so fair a casket. We heard of delicate, regular features, of sweet blue eyes and dazzling complexion, of a lithe and graceful figure and quick, pretty mays—truly a fascinating ensemble. Among the learned scholars and refined minds of that day, found in Warrington, Anna Lmtitia Aikin held her own, giving as well es receiving, and seeing for the first time something of real life, men and manners. Despite her cleverness, she was exceedingly simple, full of the joie e vivre and her brave spirit, remarkable in youth, grew as the years rolled en into a spirit of Christ-like courage and heroism which guided and helped her through many dark hours. John Howard, of illustrious memory, loved the young poetess and it seemed certain that had not her word already been given and her troth plighted, she would have been his wife. Touching upon the possibilites of such a union Mrs. Ridgway said: The man with his heart aflame for his poor, pitiable brothers, the woman with the fine intellect and dormant maternal love— surely the outcome would have been a great poem of brotherhood, instinct with God-love. But what use was it, to dream? It was not to be. John Howard went back with renewed consecration to his word of tender alleviation and Nancy went forward with br .tve heart and firm steps into her new life. In May 1774 sha became the wife of the Rev Rochemout Barbauld who was descended from a family of French Protestants.</p>
        <p>Mrs RIDGWAY then passed under review the life of Mr. and Mrs. Barbauld in Norfolk where they kept a school, and afterwards in retirement at Elampstead, and mentioned the friendships which the poetess formed with Wordsworth, Charles Lamb and other great literary men. In 1791 she published her poem to Wilberforce on the rejection of his great Bill. It began "Friends of the friendless. Hail ! se generous band." Her poem many years before on Corsica had brought her into ro'ations with Boswell. She also knew Dr. Johnson whom she nick-named "the great bear " and spoke of as " a great, rough, surly animal, but not ill- humoured." Dr. Johnson in turn spoke of her as " suckling -fools and chronicling small beer," while Horace Walpole, who did not share her political lean- ings, referred to her as " the virago Barbauld." Miss Ridgway alluded to the tragic: death of Mr. Barbauld and to the cloud which it cast over the life of the poetess. To be occupied helped her in some measure to forget her terrible grief and woon afterwards she published an edition of British novelists in some tPty volumes Then came the poem on the King's illness and a little latter the longer poem " Eighteen hundred and eleven " which provoked much a storm of indigna- tion against the author. It described the luxury of Britain, warned her of coming disaster and disgrace, and prophesied that on some future day a traveller from the Antipodes would from a broken arch of Blackfriars' Bridge, CONTEMPLATE THE RUINS OF ST. PAUL'S. It was the original of Macaulay's New Zealander sketching, amid the deserted desolation of London, the ruins of the great church. Mrs. Barbauld was over- whelmed by the cruel and vulgar criticism which the poem received and though her friends tried Ito comfort her, and Miss Edgeworth wrote a warm letter of indignant sympathy, she refused to be consoled. Although death did not claim the authoress until 1825, no other work was published by her. Death touched her so gently that she closed her eyes only to behold peace. Mrs. RIDGWAY concluded: A noble character of high purpose and courage, granted God-like gifts and yet with a warm, deeply loving nature, and modest, simple</p>
        <p>ways, Anna Lmtitia Barbauld passed through life, leaving wherever she went a strong influence for good, an incentive to look always for the best and highest. Beautiful in her youth, she remained beautiful to her last day. Her grace and charm and unassuming demeanour, added to her wide mental culture created a most fascinating personality. Through her marvellous influence over her young pupils, it is not too much to say that our whole country is in her debt. Harriet Martineau spoke of her as one of the first writers in our language, and the best example we have of the benefits of a sound classical eduo3tion to a woman. Ilazlitt, who learned to read from her" Story Books for Children " spoke of her as "a very pretty poetess, strewing flowers of poetry wherever she went." Mrs. Barbauld lived in stirrmg times, and her forceful character made it impossible for her to keep silent. She spoke and wrote without tear—</p>
        <p>A PATRIOT AND A LOYAL DAUGHTER OF OUR COUNTRY. . _</p>
        <p>To-day the works of thiii-a-u'thOi:.are hardly remembered Her essays—marvels of pure stile and literary excellence —are forgotten. Her poems, if known, are deemed out of date and old-fashione,. Such is the verdict of many But there is an except•on. A little poem, written when she was over seventy and published after her death by her niece, Elizx Aikin, has become immortal. Madame Darblay was wont to read the lines every night before she went to rest, and Wordsworth, who also committed the lines to memory, told a friend " I am not in the habit of grudging people their good things, but I wish I had w ritten those lines." Life ! I know n A what thou al t But know that thou and I must part But when, or how, or where we met I own to me's a secret yet. Life! we've been long together Through pleasant and through cloudy weather ; 'Tis hard to part when friends are dear; Perhaps 'twill cost a sigh, a tsar, Then steal away, gire little warning; Choose thine own time, Say not" Good night" but in some brighter clime Bid me" Good morning." MRS. RIDGWAY THANKED.</p>
        <p>Mr FAIRHURST described Mrs. Ridgway's address as one of the greatest tr...ats they had ever enjoyed at the Academy. (Applause). Alderman BENNETT, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs Ridgway, said he bad been spellbound by the grace and charm or her tribute to our great local poetess. In asking Mn. Ridge-ay to unveil the tablet the society they made an ideal choice. We bad not had many great female writers in Warrington—with the exception of Mrs Barbauld he did not think there was another until the time of Sarah Grand, who lived in Warrington many years and wrote her famous books here. The local line had not, however, died out, and we bad a magnificent representative of literature living quite close to the town. They were proud of Mrs Ridgway and grateful that she bad performed that day's ceremony so charmingly. He bad been longing to see that day for a great many. years and he was grati- fied that at last his wishes had been gratified by the erection of the memorial. Alderman Bennett then voiced the thanks of the society to Mr. Davies, the owner of the house, Messrs Brander and Scotson, the tenants the headmaster and students of the School of Art and particularly Mr E B Allen whose design for the tablet had done justice to the school and to himself and was one of which the town might pardonably be proud. Touching upon Mrs Barbauld's great poem Eighteen hundred and eleven" the alderman said it was utterly misunderstood at the time of publication and its reception clouded Mrs Barbauld's declining years and helped to bring down her grey hairs with sorro-v to the grave. It would have been pecii:iarly appropriate if the society had been able to make atonement for that want of appreciation in the year 1911. After a hundred years had elapsed they were trying in a tardy way to do justice to Mrs. Barbauld's memory and to show appreciation of the patriotism which bad prompted that magnificent pro- duction " Eighteen hundred and eleven. " The speaker alluded to the fact that the day's ceremony had been witnessed by a very able Hindoo, Shiv Ram, who had expressed himself as being tremendously interested in the Society's policy of erecting tablets to distinguished people and had announced his intention of advocating the idea in India on his return. Warrington might therefore once more hand on the torch to other lands and lead the way in the perpetuation of the memory of the great. (Applause.) In a happy allusion to Mrs. Ridgway's literary work, Alderman Bennett said the ceremony had taken place in " The sweet o' the year," the gtrden which was the site of Mrs Barbauld's former home would henceforth be to him " The garden of content " and he should look upon the gate to which the tablet had been rased as ' The gate beautiful." (Applause). Mr. H. STUART PA G E seconded and also spoke highly of Mrs Ridgway's discerning, appreciative and beautiful eulogy of the !poetess whom they were deligl td to honour. To read the life and works of Mrs Barbaulel was to become a keen admirer of the peat ss and of the woman. To rainy minds it was incomprehensible that it so often happened that the Eves of great writers an i especially of poets, bad been in inverse ratio to the high conception of their verse. In Mrs Barbauld,how- ever, we had one whose life and works were" bells of sweet accord." To know her was to love her. The true position of Mn. Barbauld in English literature was much greater than was g.nerslly suspect-d. One reason why she had not attained it, was because she associated herself with causes which were unpopular in ber day. All he r womanly sympathy and poetic genius was poured out 1 t the feet of those whom she considered to be opprehsed and downtrodden and that was not con- ducive t literary popularity. (Hear, hear). The vote was heartily passed. A similar compliment was paid to Mr. Fairhurst for ' presidinz, on Iha motion of Mr. C. NIAD ELM' seconded by Mr. J. LONGLAND. The company then inspected with interest a collection of Aikin books and relics. Refreshments were after- wards served.</p>
