      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-04-20_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>THE TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>It is officially announced that Msjor B. Fairclough (hom lieutenant in the Army) has been appointed Lieutenant-Colonel in command of the 4th Battalion, the Prince of Wales' Volunteers (South, Lancashire Regiment), in succession to Lieutenant-Colonel J. D Fairclough who has retired on the completion of his period of service in command. The retiring colonel has been connected with the battalion since 1890, joining as second lieutenant. He received the Territorial Officer's Decoration in 1910.</p>
        <p>The new colonel began his military career in 1893 as second lieutenant. He went to South Africa in charge of an active service company and holds the South African medal and five clasps._</p>
        <p>The Drum and Fife Band.</p>
        <p>The battalion now boasts a drum and fife band which owes its inception to the initiative of the Adjutant (Captain Elgood) and the generous support of officers and friends of the battalion. Practices have been held during the past six months under the direc- tion of Quartermaster-Sergeant O'Brien, of Orford Barracks, who has worked hard to bring the members to the requisite degree of proficiency Sergeant Drummer Clarke has also taken a keen interest in the project. The band consists of 23 fifes and 13 drums. Captain G. R. Crosfield has presented a leopard skin for the big drummer, Colonel J. Reid Walker and Mr. Collingwood have each given a side drum and Mr. J. Charlton Parr has given a sash for the sergeant-drumm4r. The band turned out fcr the first time last evening. They assembled in front of the Town Hall where the first post was sounded an then marched along Sankey-street, Bridge-street, Mersey-street, Butter- market-street, Horsemirket-street, Winwick-street, Tanners-lane, Froghall-lane, Crosfield-street and Sankey-street. On returning to the Town Hall the last post was sounded. _</p>
        <p>It UTE lunar' TO-DAY. To-day there will be a battalion rcute march to Penketh, falling in at the Drill Hall at 3 30. Efforts are being made to raise the battalion to its full establishment this season, and 150 recruits are still requiz ed. Young men between the ages of 17 and 23 who wish to serve their country by joining its defensive forces are invited to throw in their lot with the local Territorials. Medical. inspection of recruits takes place every Monday and Friday at. 8 pm. Extra allowances are granted to all married men, irrespective of rank, who attend camp for the full period.</p>
        <p>Padgate. PARISH COUNCIL—Tbe first Parish Council meeting of the year was held in the C.E. schoolroom on Tuesday evening. Mr R Denham presided, and there were also present :—Messrs Critchley,lloughton, Howard, Potter. Walker and F Wright (clerk).—A letter of apology was read from Dr Bennett, the only absent n ember. Messrs Denham and Howard were thanked for their past services ha chairman and vice-chairman respectively, and were unanimously re-elected. The overseers— Messrs Denham, Houghton and Walker were re-elected. Decided that Mr George Mather be the nominee of the Parish Council to the Lanai hire Education Sub- committee Earlestown No. 30cDistrict. Dr Bennett was appointed Parish C well representative manager of the Padgate Church of England school._ The Clerk was instructed to communicate with the County Council respecting the dusty state of the main roads in the township, urging that something be done at once either by watering or tar-spraying to remedy the nuisance. PROMM:ThE LEAOUR.—In connection with the local branch of the aho•e institution, festivities were held on Thursday evening In the C.E. schoolroom. About 300 members and friends sat down to a substantial tea prerared under the capable superintendence of Mrs Grounds. A short programme of vocal and inst rumental music followed.' Mr G Mather presided and Mr F Blackwell acomranied with his well-known skill. The Vicar voiced the thanks of those present to artistes and chairman. In the whist drive the lady prize winners were : 1, Miss Grounds ; 2, Mrs Warburton ; the successful gentlemen being : 1. Mr J Clare ; 2, Mr J Howard. The music for dancing was supplied by Messrs Wainwright and Stanley, Nhile Mr W Brimelow acted as M.C.</p>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDING. Prince—Hooton. , Ile wedding took place on Thursday, at St. Eliigs•'th Church, Wallasey, of Mr. Ernest Hope Prince, c" i„ editorial staff of the Manchester "Evening Chros.',4 youngest son of air. and Mrs James Traylo Pr toi of Warrington, and Miss Dorothy Hildred R. t youngest daughter of Mrs. James Iloaton, of Wei pool and of 1, Heathbauk Avenue, Liscard, v7l3,e„t well-known in shooting and sporting circles 00 ,4"...1) sides of the water. The service was performs' the Rev. Samuel Palmer. ti The bride, who was gowned in powder blue ea ti veiled with rose trimmed grey ninon, attended by two bridesmaids, Miss Trixie flootoot sister, and Miss Rhona McMullan, her niece, ;it carried shower bouquets of pink tulips and Ilia, roses and wore gold bangles, the gifts of the br," groom. The bride,who was given away by ber, fs'7l, had a bouquet cf white lilac and losrs. Wirt Tomlinson, of Cardiff, acted as best man and McMullan discharged the duties of groomsrssei and MIS Prince left in the early evening for nede and Torquay. The many presents received isc'eie handsome gifts from the staffs of the " Chronictb and "Dispatch" and from his former colleagues ce Warrington Press.</p>
