        <p>THE CORPORATION OF WARRINGTON is prepared to receive loans, either at six months notice or for a term of years.—Application to be made to the BOROUGH Twlssuuma, Municipal Offices, Warrington.</p>
        <p>BRADFORD BARTON VIOLINIST. MEMBER OF THE HALLE ORCHESTRA, SOLOIST AT THE CLARA BUTT CONCERTS. TEACHES AT DAWSON'S MUSIC WAREHOUSE, 59, SANKEY STREET, WARRINGTON. CHESHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL SCHOLARSHIPS. The Cheshire County Council invites applications for a number of Scholarships to Females for Instruction in Dairy Work, and tenable at the Dairy Institute, Worleston, for fourteen weeks commencing May 27th, 1912. Application forms may now be obtained from the Director of Education, City rd., Chester.</p>
        <p>C OUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON To THE ELECTORS OF TIIE TOWN HALL WARD. LAM LS AND GENTLEMEN, In consequence of the regrettable death of Councillor Rogers a vacancy has occurred in the Town Council for Town Hall Ward. I have again been requested, by representative Burgesses of all political parties, to offer myself as Candida te. It is with alarm I view the increasing rates, and the consequent detrimental effect upon trade, and you could rest assured of my efforts being used to curtail any unnecessary expenditure, also all matters relating to sanitary arrangements affecting the health of the inhabitants would be carefully considered. I am in favour of a standard rate of wages to all workmen, including Corporation men. I also feel strongly that townspeople should have preference of employment. As stated when I last contested this Ward, should you do me the honour to elect me as your representa- tive, my actions shall be unbiassed by any party consideration. My only desire in seeking this honour is to be of use to my fellow-townsmen. Respectfully soliciting your support on April 25th. I beg to remair., Yours sincerely, FIRTH LINDLEY.</p>
        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON The Health Committee are prepared to receive applications for the appointment of an Assistant Inspector of Nuisances. Wages to commence £7B per annum, rising by yearly inereases oi £6 103. to a maximum of £125. Candidates most hold the usual qualifying certificates and have had practical experience under a sanitary authority. Applications most be made in the candidate's own handwriting on forms to be obtained from this office. The application with copies of not more than three recent testimonials, must be sent in to the undersigned endorsed "Application for Assistant Inspector of Nuisances." not later than MAY 11th. Health Department, J. COOTE HIBBERT, 78, Sankey st. Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>BLACKBURN PHILANTHROPHIC FRIENDLY COLLECTING SOCIETY. CIIIEF OFFICES: KING STREET, BLACKBURN. SEVENTY-SECOND ANNUAL REPORT (ABRIDGED) DECEMBER 31sT. 1911. To TIIE MEMBERS, Your Board of Management have pleasure in presenting to you the Annual Financial Statement of Accounts for the year ending 51st December, 1911, and their Report thereon, together with the Auditors' Certificate. On perusing the same you will observe that the Premium Income was £119,788 12s. 51, an increase of £6,953 16s. 21d. over that for the previous year. Having regard to the number of Trade Disputes, Lock-outs and Strikes, which caused much unemployment, and also to the nervous apprehensions caused by the introduction of the National Insurance Bill, all of which had an effect upon Industrial Life Business, it is submitted that the results in this connection are very satisfactory. The Income from all sources amounts to £141,214, 6s. 1011„ being an increase for the year of £2,903 lls. Od. The Mortality Rate was much heavier than that experienced during 1910. The Claims, as you will see, numbered 6,270, and cost £53,318 14s. 81d., an Increase in the previous year's experience of 1,037 in number and £7,281 4s. 2kl. in amount. A detailed comparison of the Claims paid by the Society with those that would have been paid for like Premiums by large Industrial Insurance " Offices doing buain-as in the areas covered by the Society's Representatives reveals the almost incredible fact that your Society paid no less than £12,000 more than those "Offices " would have had to pay on the same Claims. Your Board, therefore, feel that they can ask of you your hearty support and loyal assistance in bringing the superior benefits and advantages of this Society before the notice of your friends. One result of the great Increase in Claims, which, are, after all, not excessaive or abnormal, is that the Increase in Funds is not quite equal to that for the Year 1910, the Increase being £43,887 143. 21d., as against £44,379 lls. 11d. This smaller Increase is due, and alone due, to the heavier Claim List, the Management Expenses being less than they were for 1910. The Funds of the Society now amount to the colossal sum of £603,511 4s. 6d., equal to over dye" times" the Annual Premium Income, thus placing the Society in an unique position. NATIONAL INSURANCE ACT. Your Board purpose, subject to the conditions and regulations Wog favourable, establishing an Approved Society to transact business under the Act, and thus conserve the interests of the Society and provide opportunities for its Members to do their Compulsory or Voluntary State-aided (as the case may be) Insurance business with the Society. under terms and upon conditions as easy and as acceptable as those that may be offered by any other Approved Society. Your Board appreciate the fact that the success of Societies established under the National Insurance Act will depend absolutely upon efficient and economical management, and, if they ultimately decide to form an Approved Society, they feel tbat they can ask your whole-hearted co-operation to build up a large Approved Society from the fact that the past record of the Society warrants them in raying that if any Society can work the Measure successfully and with advantage to its Members, they can. RICHARD PEEL, SECRETARY. BALANCE SHEET OF FUNDS AND EFFECTS.</p>
