        <p>income from all sources was £203. The porch cost £127 4s Pd, and they bad a balance in hand of £53 17s 3d. It was decided to apply for a faculty for the erection of eight tubular bells and to dispose of the existing bells. A resolution, protesting against the proposed Disestablished and Disendowment of the Welsh Church, was carried unanimously, on the propo- sition of Mr Lowe, seconded by Mr Gorton, Warrington P.S.A. Society. An open .neeting of the Warrington P.S.A. Society was held last Sunday at the Park Picture Palace when there was a very large attendance. Mr Starkey pre- sided, and the Rev. W. Cooper gave a splendid address. The musical part of the programme, as Mr. Cooper remarked, was alone worth coming to bear. Mr Turner's Band gave two very fine selections, and also accom- panied the singing. Miss McCollom sang " Gates of , the West" and Mr A Davies sang "Nazareth."lllr Clegg read tit) lesson, and the meeting closed with Mr I Nelson offering prwyer. Emmanuel (Reformed) Church. The Easter Vestry Meeting was held in the Institute, Knots ford-road, on Thursday, April 11th. The Rev W T Jaye prodded. The balance sheet and summary of accounts was presented by Mr Robert Lees, who remarked that he was again able, thanks:to the generous support of the members of the church, to present a satisfactory statement. The income for the year amounted to £2563s ld,while the exr enditure amounted to £`..50 13s 4d, leaving a balance in band of £5 Ps Pd. (Applause.) The reports and accounts of the various organism h us of the church were then presented, and revealed a highly encouraging ccndition of affairs. The Rev. W. T. Jaye, in a brief speech, laid emphasis upon the happy relations existing between ministr and people ; it had been a harpy year's work. It was a god sign too, that the offertories showed an increase cn the se f the previous year. Be was glad also to note the slowly increasing Cr ngregaticns assembling at the (+ming services, lut he wished more would attend morning service. For all the kindnesses shown him he was grateful, and he felt that while fully recognizing the peculiar difficulties with which tbey as a church had to contend, there was a bright and prosperous inture before ti ern. Let there be reliance upcn God, and intelligent loyalty to Reformation principles, and he was confident that tbeir wants would be supplied. The rev. gentleman then asked Mr W A.herion to again hiAd office as minister's warden, remarking that he could m t wish for a letter warden. Mr. Atherton replied that he would be happy to accept office again, and do all he could to help the church he so dearly loved. Mr Robert Lees was unanimously re-appointed people's warden. Mr John Bibby and Mr W Atherton were appointed to represent the church at general synod. The following gentlemen were c lected vestrymen.—R Armstrong, W Bibby, W Brown, A Johnson, J Jones, J Lister, F Lloyd, T Lloyd, 1' Moon. The usual votes of thenks were proposed and carried.</p>
