        <p>[BY REDCAP]. kCK LE(.114)11.N'4. Our picture this week is that of a pair of Black Leghorns. They are a very popular vatiety cf the Leghorn family and a most useful variety, their chief feature being egg-production. They are wonderful layers either in confinement or on a free range. They ate a very suitable variety for town rung ; being dark in plumage they do not easily show the dirt. Besides the single combed variety, we have the rose-combed Black Leghorn. These latter are almost equally as good fcr laying as the other, but not near so popular.</p>
        <p>advise you., Besides, you will remember that up to this point you have, or should have gainel sufficient knowledge to guide ytu as to the quality and value of the stock birds under consideration. HAPAZARD PURCHASES. Hapazard purchasing of stcck is a sure road to failure. Sometimes a novice will visit a show and purchase a winning cockerel, and afterwards makes other purchases to mate up with the show bird. It is almost 'a certainty that the two separate purchases, will be entirely of different strains, and what happens? The progeny from this hapazard mating turn out to be nothing more than wasters. Prize stock must be line bred. It is not always the bf at show I.ird which makes the best for the breeding of show specimens. (To be Continued).</p>
